tel 1.0

base stratiform
import soundness

info   TEL, the Typed Elastic Lists data language: presentation-preserving documents, derived codecs, schemas and BinTEL
detail
    _Stratiform_ is the reference implementation of [TEL](https://github.com/propensive/tel),
    an indentation-structured data language with schemas. A TEL document is a tree of
    *compounds* — a keyword, its space-separated *atoms*, and indented children — with
    comments, blank lines, remarks and tabulated rows all part of the text:

    ```
    tel 1.0

    # the cast
    name Percy  # lead
    age 30
    address
      street 1 High Street
      city Northtown
    ```

    The library's central value is `Tel`: a parsed document, or a single compound, which
    keeps every detail of its presentation so that a program can read one value, change it
    and write the file back without reformatting what a person wrote. Around it are three
    layers of meaning:

    - **Codecs.** A `Tel` decodes into a case class with `as[T]`, and a value encodes with
      `in[Tel]` (or `.tel`), each field a child compound keyed by the field's name in
      kebab-case, through codecs derived from the type (`Tel.Decodable`, `Tel.Encodable`). A
      source reads straight to a type with `read[T in Tel]`, and a type which opts in with
      `Tel.Parsable` is parsed directly from the bytes with no tree built at all.
    - **Schemas.** A `Tels` is a TEL schema — itself a TEL document — which `Tel.Type.assign`
      checks a document against, producing a typed `Tel.Element` tree and reporting each
      failure as a `Tel.Error` with a `Telp` path to the offending compound. A schema derives
      from a Scala type (`Tels.tels[T]`), composes in *layers*, and a `Tel.Provider` turns one
      into typed records at compile time.
    - **BinTEL.** A typed document encodes to a compact binary form guided by its schema
      (`bintel`, `Bintel`), identified by a `SchemaSignature` or carried self-contained, and
      hashed by content with `valueHash`; a `Tel.Acceptance` lets a reader tell a writer which
      schema compositions it accepts.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    val person: Person = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel].as[Person]
    val same: Person = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Person in Tel]
    val text: Text = Person(t"Hal", 41).in[Text over Tel]        // t"name Hal\nage 41\n"
    ```

    A literal document is `tel"…"`, parsed as the code compiles and usable as a pattern; a
    location is a `Telp` path, written `telp"/office/city"`. Editing goes through
    `Tel.Mutation` operations and `Tel.Revision` logs addressed by a `Tel.Pointer`, or through
    `source.open[Tel](Read & Write)` which parses a source, offers the machine operations on
    a `Tel.Handle`, and writes the document back when the block ends. Unverified navigation
    (`tel.name`, `tel.items(0)`) is gated by `import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel`; a document
    checked with `verify[T]` navigates with the compiler checking every field.

    Three facts about the codecs are worth knowing before the first surprise. A Scala
    `Boolean` is a *scalar*, written as an explicit `true`/`false` atom, never a TEL flag
    (bare keyword presence): the derived schema and BinTEL agree on that mapping, and a value
    encoded as a flag would fail validation against its own derived schema. An `Optional`
    field reads `Unset` for a missing keyword or one with neither atom nor child, and the
    `optionalityOptions` givens (`strictTelAbsence`, `lenientTelFaults`, …) change that per
    scope. And the document's final newline is the last block's trailing blank line: a
    `Tel.Block#trailingBlankLines` count, which every mutation conserves, so a round trip
    neither gains nor loses one.

    The library is split into components:

    - `stratiform.core` — everything above: `Tel`, `Tels`, `Telp`, `Tel.Provider`, `Tel.Mutation`,
      `Tel.Revision`, `Patterns`, `Positional`, `TelReader`, `Bintel`, `SchemaSignature`,
      `SchemaResolver`, `Varint`, the `@assign` and `@retract` layer annotations, and the
      `dynamicAccess`, `optionalityOptions` and `conversions` choice packages.
    - `stratiform.base256` — `Base256`, the binary-to-text alphabet BinTEL signatures and
      acceptances travel in as a single word.
    - `stratiform.staged` and `stratiform.binaryStaged` — `Inlinable` and `BintelInlinable`,
      the macro-time code generators behind `Tel.Parsable.staged` and the staged BinTEL
      parsers, which compile a monomorphic parser for a case class.
    - `stratiform.optics` — `telLens`, `telOrdinalOptical` and `telEachOptical`, panopticon
      lenses over a `Tel`, in the `optics` package.
    - `stratiform.time` — `Instant` and `Duration` codecs, in the `encodables` and
      `decodables` packages.
    - `stratiform.http` — `telPostable`, `telServable` and `telConstruable`, for sending and
      serving TEL as `application/vnd.tel`.
    - `stratiform.duplex` — `Framed`, length-prefixed BinTEL messages over a `Duplex`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the choice packages merge with
    the umbrella's own. A given which selects a policy is imported by name — `import p.*` does
    not import givens — so a program writes `import soundness.*` and then, as it needs them,
    `import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel`, `import optionalityOptions.strictTelAbsence`, `import
    optics.telLens`, `import encodables.instantTelEncodable`, or `import
    parsing.trackPositions` (zephyrine's toggle, which makes a parsed `Tel` carry source
    positions for `locate`, `locateKey` and `enclosing`).

refer stratiform.Tel
refer stratiform.Tels
refer stratiform.Telp
refer stratiform.Bintel
refer stratiform.Ratel
refer stratiform.Tel.Mutation
refer stratiform.Tel.Revision

entry .Tel   a TEL document or compound, keeping its presentation; the home of the codecs, schema typing and BinTEL
  detail
      A `Tel` wraps one `Tel.Subtree`: either a `Tel.Document` — the root, with its pragma,
      interpreter directive, line endings and blocks — or a single `Tel.Compound`. Both have
      `children`, an array of `Tel.Block`s, so navigation (`#field`, `#fields`,
      `#childCompounds`) treats the two forms alike. A `Tel` parsed from text is
      document-rooted; one produced by encoding a value is compound-rooted, and `show`,
      `Tel.emit` and `Tel.lend` wrap such a value in a document before rendering it.

      A `Tel` is made by reading any byte or text source (`.aggregable`, `.loadable`), by the
      `tel"…"` interpolator, by encoding a value (`value.in[Tel]`, `value.tel`), or by the
      constructors `Tel.scalar`, `Tel.compound` and `.make`. It is decoded with `#as[T]`, rendered
      with `show`, streamed with `.emit` and `.lend`, typed against a schema with
      `Tel.Type.assign`, and edited with `Mutation`, `Revision`, `#modify` or a
      `Tel.Handle`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A document, parsed
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val document: Tel = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
            document.field(t"name").let(_.primaryAtom)   // t"Percy"
      frame
        label Decoded into a case class
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

            val document: Tel = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
            val person: Person = document.as[Person]      // Person(t"Percy", 30)
      frame
        label Or read to the type in one step
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

            val person: Person = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Person in Tel]
      frame
        label And verified, so navigation is checked as the code compiles
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

            val checked = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel].verify[Person]
            checked.name.as[Text]   // t"Percy"
            checked.email           // does not compile
      ##
      ```

      Decoding needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]`; under a `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary every
      field's error accrues with a `Tel.Focus` naming the field's path, and, for a document
      parsed under `parsing.trackPositions`, the source span of its value.

      The companion is assembled from the traits `Tel2`, `Tel3` and `Tel4` (`object Tel
      extends Tel2`), whose members — the derived `decodable`/`encodable` fallbacks, the
      primitive and collection codecs, the `field` fallback for direct parsing, and
      `Tel.Provider` — it inherits, so they are reached through `Tel` but documented under
      the trait which declares each. Since `Tel.Decodable`,
      `Tel.Encodable` and `Tel.Parsable` are nested in `Tel`, every given in the companion is
      in implicit scope for them: no import is needed for a codec. A file which does `import
      Tel.given` lexically prefers the companion's blanket `encodable` over any other
      library's `Encodable in Text` for its types, which is a trap when encoding a `Telp` or
      an `Acceptance` to text: call the specific instance, or `acceptance.encode`, directly.
  refer stratiform.Tels
  refer stratiform.Telp
  refer stratiform.Tel.Mutation
  refer stratiform.Tel.Revision
  refer stratiform.TelReader
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  entry #as   decodes this document as a type, through its `Tel.Decodable`
      `tel.as[Person]` runs the `Decodable in Tel` for the type — the derived product codec
      for a case class, a text codec for a scalar, a collection codec for a `List` — and then
      locates every `Tel.Focus` the decoders accrued against this root, so that under a
      `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary each error carries its field's path and, if the document
      was parsed under `parsing.trackPositions`, the span of the text it is about. It raises
      `Tel.Error` with reason `Absent` (401) for a required field with no keyword and
      `NotScalar` (402) for an atom the field's type cannot parse.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel].as[Person]            // Person(t"Percy", 30)
      safely(t"name Percy\nage old\n".read[Tel].as[Person])   // Unset: `old` is not an Int
      ```
  entry #enclosing   the innermost compound at a source line and column, as a path and a region
      `tel.enclosing(line, column)`, with 1-indexed coordinates, is the `Tel.Enclosure` of the
      innermost compound whose extent contains the coordinate: the `Telp` path which reaches
      it and the `Tel.Error.Position` to highlight — the inline-atom run when the coordinate
      falls within it, the keyword otherwise. It is the reverse of `locate` and `locateKey`,
      for an editor resolving a cursor to a node. The document must have been parsed under
      `parsing.trackPositions`; without a position index, or for a coordinate before the
      first compound (which only the root encloses), the result is `Unset`. Blank, remark-only
      and payload lines belong to the nearest preceding compound.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import parsing.trackPositions

      t"person\n  name Percy\n  age 30\n".read[Tel].enclosing(2, 4).let(_.path)   // telp"/person/name"
      ```
  entry #selectDynamic   dynamic field access, `tel.firstName`, by the kebab-case keyword
      `tel.firstName` looks up the child compound with keyword `first-name`, as an empty `Tel`
      when there is none. On a plain `Tel` the access is unverified and needs a `Tel is
      Dynamical` in scope: `import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel`, or a `dynamically[Tel]:` block;
      without one the macro reports _dynamic field access on an unverified `Tel` requires
      `import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel`…_. On a `Tel of P from R` produced by `verify[P]` the
      macro checks that `P` has the field and types the result as `Tel of <field type> from R`,
      so a misspelled field is a compile error and no import is needed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel

      t"first-name Percy\n".read[Tel].firstName.primaryAtom   // t"Percy"
      ```
  entry #applyDynamic   indexed access, `tel.items(2)`, into a field's children or a repeated field
      On a plain `Tel`, `tel.items(2)` is the third child compound under the `items` field,
      or an empty `Tel` when the index is out of range; it needs the same `Dynamical` as
      `#selectDynamic`. On a verified `Tel of P from R` whose field is a collection, it is the
      third sibling compound sharing the field's keyword — TEL's representation of a repeated
      field — typed as `Tel of <element> from R`; a field which is not a collection is a
      compile error.
  entry #keyword   the compound's keyword, or the empty text for a document root
  entry #atomTexts   the texts of this node's atoms, inline first and then any source or literal payload
      A compound's atoms in presentation order: each `Tel.Atom.Inline` text, and then the
      text of the `Tel.Atom.Source` or `Tel.Atom.Literal` which follows the line, if any. A
      document root has no atoms.
  entry #primaryAtom   the first atom's text, or the empty text if the node has none
      What the scalar codecs read: a compound `age 30` has primary atom `30`. A compound with a
      keyword and no atom has the empty text as its value, which is TEL's spelling of an
      empty string.
  entry #childCompounds   every child compound, flattened across the node's blocks
      The compounds of each `Tel.Block` in order, with comments, tabulation headers and blank
      lines dropped from the view.
  entry #field   the first child compound with a keyword, if any, as a `Tel`
      `tel.field(t"name")` is `Unset` when no child has the keyword. A keyword may repeat;
      `#fields` returns every occurrence.
  entry #fields   every child compound with a keyword, in document order
      A repeatable schema member, or a `List` field, is encoded as sibling compounds sharing a
      keyword, which a single-valued accessor cannot express.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"item 1\nitem 2\nitem 3\n".read[Tel].fields(t"item").map(_.primaryAtom)   // Array(t"1", t"2", t"3")
      ```
  entry #modify   replaces a field's compound in place, or appends it if absent
      `tel.modify("name", Tel.scalar(t"Hal"))` re-keys the value's compound to the field's
      kebab-case keyword and puts it where the existing compound was, keeping the surrounding
      blocks, comments and blank lines; a field with no compound is appended to the last
      block. A document-rooted value becomes a compound carrying the document's children. It
      needs a `Tel is Dynamical` (`import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel`), as the field is named
      by text rather than checked against a schema. Finer edits — one atom of a line, a
      remark, a flag — are `Mutation.Op`s applied to a `Tel.Pointer`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel

      t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel].modify("name", Tel.scalar(t"Hal")).show   // t"name Hal\nage 30\n"
      ```
  entry #Self   the typeclass subject: `Tel` itself
  entry #Metadata   the metadata type a loaded document is paired with: `Tel.Metadata`
  entry .Layer   the class a schema layer object extends: `object verse extends Tel.Layer("verse")`
      A layer (TEL §20.3) is declared once, at the top level or in an object, as a singleton
      whose name is part of its type, so that `@assign(verse)` and `@retract(verse)` refer to it
      by its object and a misspelt layer is a compile error rather than a second layer. The
      name is read from the type by `Tel.Name[verse.type]`. In a derived schema
      (`Tels.tels[T]`), the fields assigned to a layer form its overlay, and the enum cases
      retracted from it are excluded under it, which is what `Tel.Acceptance` and `fulfil`
      offer and serve as components.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object withPhone extends Tel.Layer("with-phone")

      case class Contact(name: Text, email: Optional[Text], @assign(withPhone) phone: Optional[Text])
      ```
  entry .Name   the name of a layer, from its type: `Tel.Name[verse.type]` is `"verse"`
  entry .telOpenable   the `Openable` for a readable and writable TEL source: `source.open[Tel](Read & Write)`
      In implicit scope through `Tel`'s companion for any `source` which is `Readable to Tel`
      and `Writable by Data`. Opening parses the source on entry, hands the block a
      `Tel.Handle` whose grants follow the mode, and on normal completion writes the
      document back if it was mutated or `Tel.Flag.Force` was passed; an abort or an
      exception skips the write-back. It outranks `Tel.telViewOpenable` whenever the source can
      also be written.
  entry .Position   a 1-indexed source position: the alias of `Tel.Error.Position`
      The `type` and `val` `Tel.Position` both name `Tel.Error.Position`, where the type is
      defined; it is what `tel.locate(path)` and `tel.locateKey(path)` return.
  entry .Focus   where a decoding or validation error is: a `Telp` pointer and a source span
    detail
        The focus `Tel#as` and `Tel.Type.assign` accrue against under a `validate[Tel.Focus]`
        boundary: the keyword path of the field or compound being decoded, as a `Telp`
        (`/person/name`, with an occurrence index after a repeatable member's keyword), and
        the `Span` of the text the error is about, filled in lazily from a document parsed
        under `parsing.trackPositions` and otherwise left empty. An error registered outside
        every field takes the root focus. Decoding points the span at the value; schema
        validation at the keyword, which an unknown-keyword diagnostic must underline.

        Under `validate[Tel.Focus]` with an accruing error type, decoding `name Percy` and
        `age old` as a `Person(name: Text, age: Int)` records one `NotScalar` error at the
        focus `/age`, with the span of `old` when the document was parsed under
        `parsing.trackPositions`.
    entry #pointer   the keyword path, as a `Telp`; `Telp.Root` at the root
    entry #span   the source span of the value, or `Span.empty` when it is unknown
    entry #withSpan   this focus with the span of the value at its pointer, located in a document
        Resolves the pointer with `tel.locate`; a document with no position index, or an
        unresolvable path, leaves the span empty.
    entry #withKeySpan   this focus with the span of the keyword at its pointer, located in a document
  entry .supplementPositions   fills in every accrued focus's span from a document, pointing at values
      Called by `Tel#as` after decoding: for each `Tel.Focus` accrued so far it supplies the
      span of the value at the focus's pointer, through `Tel.Focus#withSpan`. A no-op unless
      the ambient `Foci` tracks and the document carries a position index. Public because
      `Tel#as` is inline and expands into user code.
  entry .supplementKeyPositions   fills in every accrued focus's span from a document, pointing at keywords
      As `.supplementPositions`, through `Tel.Focus#withKeySpan`: used by `Tel.Type.assign`,
      whose diagnostics are about members rather than values.
  entry .edited   applies a `Revision` to a document: `tel.edited(revision)`
      An extension on `Tel`. A `Revision` is a composed log of `Mutation.Op`s; applying it
      raises `Mutation.Error` at the first operation the document rejects, and the result is
      a new document with its presentation otherwise untouched.
  entry .verify   checks a document against a type and re-types it for compile-checked navigation
      An extension on `Tel`. `tel.verify[Person]` decodes the document as `Person` through its
      `Tel.Decodable` — raising `Tel.Error` if it does not conform, since TEL scalars are
      untyped text and only decoding can catch a non-numeric `Int` — and then returns the
      same document typed as `Tel of Person from Person`. The phantom `Topic` is the current
      position in the type and `Origin` the root, so `#selectDynamic` and `#applyDynamic`
      resolve each field against the type as the code compiles, with no `dynamicAccess`
      import: a misspelled field, or a field the type lacks, does not compile. Fields reached
      this way are `Tel`s, decoded with `as[T]` at the leaf.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Play(title: Text, hero: Person)

      val doc = Play(t"Othello", Person(t"Othello", 40)).in[Tel]
      doc.verify[Play].hero.name.as[Text]   // t"Othello"
      doc.verify[Play].hero.rank             // does not compile
      ```
  entry .Nature   how a codec's type may occupy a line's atom positions: `Scalar`, `Flag` or `Struct`
      TEL §20.2 lets a compound's inline atoms fill its members positionally — `person Percy
      30` fills `name` and `age` — and `Nature` is the codec-level approximation of the
      member classification which decides that: `Scalar` is atom-assignable and never
      skipped, `Flag` is atom-assignable when the atom text equals the keyword, and `Struct`
      (a nested record, sum or map) can only be filled by a keyword child. Every derived codec
      is `Scalar` or `Struct`: a Scala `Boolean` is a value, so it is a `Scalar` with an
      explicit `true`/`false` atom, the mapping the derived schema and BinTEL share. `Flag` is
      opt-in for a custom codec of a genuinely flag-shaped type, whose field is then never
      required (absence reads as `false`).
  entry .Encodable   a TEL encoder for a type which also carries the shape of what it writes
    detail
        `value is Tel.Encodable` is an `Encodable in Tel` which also reports its `#shape` as a
        format-neutral `Morphology`, its `#nature` and whether it is `#optional`, so a schema
        derived from the type (`Tels.tels[T]`) is coherent with the codec by construction. A
        case class derives one through `Tel.encodable`; the primitives, `Optional`, the
        collections and `Map` have theirs in the companion. A product encodes as a compound
        whose children are one compound per field, keyed by the field's name in kebab-case or
        by its `@name[Tel](…)` annotation; a scalar as a compound with one inline atom; an
        empty `Optional` as nothing at all; a collection as repeated compounds sharing the
        field's keyword; and a `Map` as `entries` compounds each holding a `key` and a `value`
        child. The atom form escalates (inline, then source, then literal) so a multi-line or
        space-edged text always reads back as written.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        Person(t"Percy", 30).in[Tel].show    // t"name Percy\nage 30\n"
        Person(t"Percy", 30).tel             // the same `Tel`
        ```

        A custom encoder is built with `.apply`, giving its shape and nature; a type with an
        `Encodable in Text` encodes as a scalar through it automatically.
    entry .apply   builds an encoder from a shape, an optional nature and optionality, and a function
        `Tel.Encodable(() => Morphology.Str, Tel.Nature.Scalar)(value => Tel.scalar(…))`. The
        shape is an explicit thunk so that the instance's capture set can name it.
    entry #shape   the `Morphology` of what this encoder writes
    entry #nature   the `Tel.Nature` of the type: `Struct` unless overridden
    entry #optional   whether a field of this type may legitimately be absent; `false` unless overridden
    entry #constructed   the §22.2 canonical child form of a value: `encoded` unless overridden
        For derived products, the compound with its leading scalar fields in atom position
        (`person Percy 30`), as `Mutation.construct` builds it; what `Tel.canonical` uses for
        nested values.
    entry #canonicalized   the §22.2 canonical presentation as a document root: `constructed` unless overridden
        The root carries no atoms, so a derived product's fields are top-level children whose
        own nested records carry inline runs.
    entry #Form   the form this encoder produces: `Tel`
  entry .canonical   a value in its §22.2 canonical presentation, with leading scalars as inline atoms
      `value.encode` keeps the fully keyword-child wire form, which typed navigation and the
      optics rely on; `Tel.canonical(value)` instead puts the leading scalar and flag fields
      of each nested record in atom position, as a person would write them. Both forms decode
      to the same value.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Wire form
        code
            import soundness.*

            case class Address(street: Text, city: Text)
            case class Delivery(name: Text, address: Address)

            Delivery(t"Lear", Address(t"1 Heath Rd", t"Town")).in[Tel].show
            // t"name Lear\naddress\n  street 1 Heath Rd\n  city Town\n"
      alternative Canonical
        code
            import soundness.*

            case class Address(street: Text, city: Text)
            case class Delivery(name: Text, address: Address)

            Tel.canonical(Delivery(t"Lear", Address(t"1 Heath Rd", t"Town"))).show
            // t"name Lear\naddress    1 Heath Rd  Town\n"
      ##
      ```
  entry .Decodable   a TEL decoder for a type which also carries the shape of what it reads
    detail
        `value is Tel.Decodable` is a `Decodable in Tel` with a `#shape`, a `#nature`, whether
        it is `#repeatable` (a collection, gathering every same-keyword sibling) and
        `#optional`, and what it yields when its keyword is `#absent`. A case class derives
        one through `Tel.decodable`; a type with a `Decodable in Text` reads its primary atom
        through it; the primitives, `Optional`, collections and `Map` have instances in the
        companion, and `Tels` reads from its own TEL document through `Tel.telsDecodable`. The
        product decoder matches children by keyword, fills fields from the entry line's atoms
        first (§20.2 positional assignment, in declaration order), keeps the first occurrence
        of a non-repeatable field, and falls back to a declared default. It needs a
        `Tactic[Tel.Error]`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int = 18)

        t"name Percy\n".read[Tel].as[Person]       // Person(t"Percy", 18)
        t"person Percy 30\n".read[Tel].as[Person]  // does not compile
        ```
    entry .apply   builds a decoder from a shape, an optional nature and optionality, and a `Decodable in Tel`
        `Tel.Decodable(() => Morphology.Str, Tel.Nature.Scalar)(tel => …)`, the
        `Decodable in Tel` typically written as a lambda over the `Tel`.
    entry #shape   the `Morphology` of what this decoder reads
    entry #repeatable   whether this decodes a repeated field, gathering every same-keyword sibling
    entry #nature   the `Tel.Nature` of the type, which drives positional atom assignment: `Struct` unless overridden
    entry #optional   whether a field of this type may be absent without error; `false` unless overridden
    entry #absent   the value of a field whose keyword never appeared: by default, decoding an empty `Tel`
        Primitives raise `Tel.Error(Absent)` from the empty node; `Optional` yields `Unset`
        unless `optionalityOptions.strictTelAbsence` is in scope; a collection is empty.
    entry #Form   the form this decoder reads: `Tel`
  entry .Parsing   what a direct parser and a field parser share: reading one entry from a `TelReader`
    detail
        The substance of `Tel.Parsable` and `Tel.Field`, which add nothing to it and exist
        only so that neither is a subtype of the other. Where a JSON reader sits at a
        self-describing token, a TEL value is an *entry* — a keyword line and the subtree
        beneath it, whose extent depends on the entry's indent — so the operative method is
        `#parse(reader, indent)`, called with the reader just past the keyword; the instance
        consumes the line's remainder and the subtree. The no-indent `#parse(reader)` reads a
        whole document. A parser carries the same `#shape`, `#nature`, `#repeatable` and
        `#optional` metadata as a `Tel.Decodable`, and `#parseAtom` and `#parseFlag` read a
        value which arrived as a positional atom on the parent's line.
    entry #shape   the `Morphology` of what this parser reads
    entry #repeatable   whether this is a collection parser, gathering occurrence by occurrence
    entry #nature   the `Tel.Nature` of the type: `Struct` unless overridden
    entry #optional   whether a field of this type may be absent without error
    entry #parseAtom   the value of one positionally-assigned atom; only a `Scalar` nature accepts one
        The default raises `Tel.Error(AtomAtNonAssignablePos)` (303).
    entry #parseFlag   the value of a `Flag`-natured field made present by a bare atom matching its keyword
    entry #parse   reads this value from the reader: one entry at an indent, or the whole document
        `parse(reader, indent)` consumes the entry whose keyword has just been read;
        `parse(reader)` reads a document with no entry line, and by default yields `#absent`.
    entry #absent   the value of a field whose keyword never arrived: an error unless overridden
        Raises `Tel.Error(Absent)` (401); the AST bridge delegates to its decoder's own
        `absent()`, and `Optional` yields `Unset`.
    entry #Transport   the transport the reader reads: `Tel`
    entry #Reader   the reader type: `TelReader`
  entry .Parsable   direct parsing from bytes with no document tree: the opt-in surface of `read[T in Tel]`
    detail
        A `value is Tel.Parsable` reads its value straight from the parser's bytes through a
        `TelReader`, so `source.read[T in Tel]` never materialises a `Tel`. It is deliberately opt-in: there is no blanket given, so
        no read changes behaviour until a type declares one, by hand with `.apply`, derived
        with `.derived`, generated as a monomorphic parser with `.staged`, or bridged from an
        existing decoder with `.fromDecodable`. The primitives, `Tel` itself and the
        collections of parsable elements have instances in the companion. When a type has
        one, `.aggregableParsed` and `.readableParsed` win over the tree-building path;
        otherwise `Tel.aggregableIn` parses the tree and decodes it, exactly as before.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        given Person is Tel.Parsable = Tel.Parsable.derived

        t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Person in Tel]   // parsed with no tree built
        ```

        A field of a derived parser resolves through `Tel.Field`: its own `Parsable` if it has
        one, a text codec, structural derivation, or the bridge over any `Tel.Decodable`. A
        case class with both a `Reflection` and a hand-written `Tel.Decodable` is derived
        structurally on this path and so diverges from its decoder; the remedy is one line,
        `given MyType is Tel.Parsable = Tel.Parsable.fromDecodable(myDecodable)`. The three
        decode paths — the tree, the derived direct parser and the staged one — agree on
        keywords, positional atoms, defaults and error foci. The companion's remaining
        members (`wireKeywords`, `positionalTable`, `focusing`, `atomInt`, …) are the support
        points a staged parser's generated code calls; they are public only because that
        code is expanded into user modules, and are not for direct use.
    entry .apply   a parser from a shape and a function over the reader
        `Tel.Parsable(Morphology.Str)(reader => …)`; the function receives the `TelReader`
        positioned just past the entry's keyword and must consume the entry.
    entry .derived   the one-line opt-in: `given MyType is Tel.Parsable = Tel.Parsable.derived`
        A Wisteria-derived direct parser for a product or a sum (a sum always goes through
        the tree bridge, since its variant keyword cannot be known before the entry is read),
        wrapped as a nominal `Parsable` so the read trigger finds it. A method rather than a
        given, so that a type parses directly only once it has said so.
    entry .staged   a macro-generated monomorphic parser for a case class, with no per-field boxing
        `given MyType is Tel.Parsable = Tel.Parsable.staged` generates a parser whose field
        values live in typed locals, whose keywords dispatch by packed-`Long` comparison and
        whose record is built by a direct constructor call. Its semantics match `.derived`
        exactly; its `shape()` is `Morphology.Any`. It requires a top-level or object-nested
        case class with a single parameter list; sums and method-local classes use
        `.derived`. The generator lives in `stratiform.staged`.
    entry .fromDecodable   bridges a `Tel.Decodable` to direct parsing by materialising each entry as a `Tel`
        The universal bridge: one entry (or, at the top level, the whole document) is built as
        a `Tel` and decoded. It keeps a repeatable decoder's gathering, decoding every
        occurrence together as a synthetic document, and is the escape hatch when a custom
        decoder must beat a derived direct parser.
    entry .fromField   a nominal `Parsable` delegating to any `Tel.Parsing` instance
    entry .iterable   the shared collection parser, for any `Iterable` with a `Factory`
        Repeatable: the derived product parser gathers every same-keyword occurrence, and a
        positional atom on the parent's line becomes an element too (§19.2). A single entry
        decoded as a collection is one element; a whole document is every top-level entry.
    entry .optionality   the shared `Optional` parser: `Unset` for a missing keyword or an empty entry
        Honours `optionalityOptions`: strict absence raises `Tel.Error(Absent)`. Only absence
        is policed on the direct path; a fault surfaces mid-stream, where the entry has been
        partly consumed, so it raises as the inner parser does.
    entry .Direct   the base class of a generated parser: the reader arrives as a neutral carrier
      detail
          Generated code is capture-erased, so `.staged` parsers extend `Direct` and implement
          the protected `parseEntry` and `parseWhole` over an `AnyRef` reader; the capability
          is asserted here at the rim. Not for direct use.
      entry #parse   reads an entry at an indent, or a whole document, through the protected hooks
      entry #shape   `Morphology.Any`
    entry .parseField   calls a field's parser with the reader as a neutral carrier (generated code)
    entry .absentField   calls a field's `absent()` through a neutral carrier (generated code)
    entry .assemble   constructs a product from a filled value buffer through its `ProductReflection`
    entry .wireKeywords   the wire keywords of a product's fields: `@name` renames, else camel-to-kebab
    entry .missing   raises `Absent` for a required primitive whose keyword never arrived, continuing with a sentinel
    entry .scalarFault   raises `Absent` or `NotScalar` for a present entry with no usable atom
    entry .focusing   wraps one field's read in a `Tel.Focus`, compiled away under the inert default `Foci`
    entry .focusingUnlocated   as `focusing`, for a field with no entry of its own in the source
    entry .keywordIndex   linear keyword dispatch over a product's wire keywords
    entry .repeats   whether a field parser gathers occurrences (is a repeatable collection parser)
    entry .parseElement   one element of a repeatable field, from the reader
    entry .parseAtomElement   one element of a repeatable field, from a positional atom
    entry .gathered   the collection built from a repeatable field's gathered elements
    entry .positionalTable   the per-field profiles a staged parser's atom phase uses, built once
    entry .positionalAssign   runs the §20.2 atom phase for one entry line over a profile table
    entry .positionalCount   how many atoms a positional slot received
    entry .positionalText   the text of one atom in a positional slot
    entry .duplicateFill   raises `NonRepeatableTooMany` (308): a field filled by an atom and again by a child
    entry .atomInt   an `Int` from a positional atom, or `NotScalar` with the offending text
    entry .atomLong   a `Long` from a positional atom, or `NotScalar`
    entry .atomBoolean   `true` or `false` from a positional atom, or `NotScalar`
    entry .product   the derived product parser's engine: keyword loop, positional pre-pass, defaults, foci
        Takes a thunk of the fields' wire keywords, parsers and defaults, and a constructor
        over the filled buffer. Every missing required field accrues under a
        `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary, since absent keys are only known once the entry
        region is consumed.
  entry .Field   the typeclass the product derivation resolves per field: how a field reads from a `TelReader`
    detail
        The operational face of direct parsing. `Tel.Field` is a `Tel.Parsing` with its own
        resolution chain, which `Tel.Parsable` is kept out of so that a type's nominal
        `Parsable` never competes with the field fallback: `Tel.fieldCollection`, `Tel.fieldList`,
        `Tel.fieldSet`, `Tel.fieldSeries` and `Tel.fieldOptional` resolve the element-wise wrappers
        first, and `Tel.field` is the universal fallback — a `Parsable` if the type has one,
        then a `Decodable in Text`, then structural derivation, then the bridge over a
        `Tel.Decodable`. A user writes a `Tel.Parsable`, not a `Tel.Field`.
    entry .apply   adapts any `Tel.Parsing` instance for use as a field parser
  entry .Type   type assignment: checks a document against a `Tels` schema and produces its typed `Tel.Element` tree
    detail
        TEL §20.2. `Tel.Type.assign(tel, schema)` walks the presentation tree with the schema,
        matching each compound to a member of its parent's struct, filling members from the
        line's atoms positionally and then from keyword children, resolving references and
        selects, and checking required, repeatable, contiguity and key constraints. The
        result is the semantic model: a `Tel.Element.Node` for each struct or flag, a
        `Tel.Element.Value` for each scalar, with children in member order. Every document
        fault is a `Tel.Error` whose reason is the E3xx code (`UnknownKeyword` 306,
        `RequiredMemberAbsent` 307, `NonRepeatableTooMany` 308, …) and whose location rides
        on the accrued `Tel.Focus`, so under a `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary the walk
        continues past each offending node and reports every fault together, each with a
        `Telp` to the compound; outside one, the first fault aborts. A malformed schema
        (`UnresolvedReference` 209, `DuplicateDefinition` 210) always aborts, and nesting
        beyond 256 levels fail-stops with `NestingLimitExceeded` (501).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        val schema: Tels = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")
        val element: Tel.Element = Tel.Type.assign(t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel], schema)
        safely(Tel.Type.assign(t"nam Percy\n".read[Tel], schema))   // Unset: E306 and E307
        ```
    entry .assign   assigns types to a document under a schema, optionally with validators and codecs
        Three overloads. `assign(tel, schema)` runs no §21 checks, so a schema's own
        bootstrap never needs a registry. `assign(tel, schema, validators)` runs each
        scalar's named validators through the `Tel.Validator.Registry` (`ValidatorRejected`,
        310) and its declared patterns (`PatternRejected`, 315). `assign(tel, schema,
        validators, codecs)` additionally checks each scalar's declared encoding against the
        `Tel.Codec.Bindings`: the codec must accept the value (`EncodingRejected`, 312) and
        the name must resolve (`EncodingUnresolved`, 313). The result is `Tel.Element raises
        Tel.Error tracks Tel.Focus`.
  entry .Validator   the named validators a schema's scalars are checked by: requests, responses and a registry
    detail
        A schema constrains a scalar with validator names (`field name identifier`), and a
        `Tel.Validator.Registry` answers each `Request` with a `Response`. The built-in
        `Registry.builtins` knows `string` (anything), `identifier` (kebab-case: lowercase
        letters, digits and single hyphens, not at either end), `type-name` (an uppercase
        letter then alphanumerics) and `sigil` (one symbolic character, not whitespace, a
        letter, a digit, a bracket or `+`). An application registers its own validators as a
        `Registry` and composes it with `Registry.withFallback`, so a domain constraint lives
        in the schema rather than in every program reading it; an unknown name is rejected
        with the diagnostic _unknown validator 'name'_.
    entry .Request   what is validated: `Scalar(method, value)` or `Struct(method, element)`
    entry .Diagnostic   why a value was rejected: a `Scalar` message with an optional span, or a `Struct` message with per-field diagnostics
    entry .Response   `Valid`, or `Invalid(diagnostic)`
    entry .Registry   a validator registry: `builtins`, or a custom one composed with `withFallback`
      detail
          A `Registry` is one method, `apply(request): Response`, so a custom one is a lambda
          or an object. `Tel.Type.assign(tel, schema, registry)` consults it for every
          validator a scalar names.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*

          val custom: Tel.Validator.Registry = new Tel.Validator.Registry:
            def apply(request: Tel.Validator.Request): Tel.Validator.Response = request match
              case Tel.Validator.Request.Scalar(t"even", value) =>
                if value.s.toIntOption.exists(_ % 2 == 0) then Tel.Validator.Response.Valid
                else Tel.Validator.Response.Invalid(Tel.Validator.Diagnostic.Scalar(t"not even"))
              case _ =>
                Tel.Validator.Response.Invalid(Tel.Validator.Diagnostic.Scalar(t"unknown validator"))

          val registry = Tel.Validator.Registry.withFallback(custom)
          ```
      entry .builtins   the four built-in validators: `string`, `identifier`, `type-name` and `sigil`
          A `Struct` request is always invalid for these, with _validator 'name' not applicable
          to struct values_.
      entry .withFallback   a registry trying a custom one first and the built-ins for any name it does not know
          A custom response whose diagnostic begins _unknown validator_ is retried against
          `builtins`; any other response stands.
      entry #apply   the response to a request
  entry .Codec   a scalar encoding (§21.7): text to bytes and back, bound to a schema's encoding names
    detail
        A schema may declare that a scalar's text carries a named *encoding*, so BinTEL writes
        the scalar as the codec's bytes rather than its UTF-8 text. A `Tel.Codec` pairs
        `#encode`, from accepted texts to bytes, with `#decode`, succeeding on exactly the
        encoder's image. Codecs are application-defined and reached by name through a
        `Codec.Bindings`; the library defines two, `.base256` and `.schemaSignature`, which
        BinTEL's acceptance schema uses and `Bindings.builtins` binds. Validation with
        bindings (`Tel.Type.assign(tel, schema, validators, codecs)`) rejects a value the
        codec refuses (E312) and an encoding name which does not resolve (E313); with no
        bindings at all the encoding checks are skipped.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val short: Tel.Codec = new Tel.Codec:
          def encode(text: Text): Tel.Codec.Encoded =
            if text.length > 8 then Tel.Codec.Encoded.Invalid(t"longer than eight characters")
            else Tel.Codec.base256.encode(text)

          def decode(bytes: Data): Tel.Codec.Decoded = Tel.Codec.base256.decode(bytes)

        val bindings: Tel.Codec.Bindings = name => if name == t"short-word" then short else Unset
        val all = bindings.orElse(Tel.Codec.Bindings.builtins)
        ```
    entry .Encoded   the result of encoding a text: `Bytes(data)`, or `Invalid(message)` for a rejected text
    entry .Decoded   the result of decoding bytes: `Value(text)`, or `Failure(message)`
    entry .Bindings   the resolution of encoding names to codecs: a function from a name to an `Optional[Tel.Codec]`
      detail
          `Unset` means the name is not recognised, which is E313 at validation and B13 at
          BinTEL decode. That is distinct from configuring no bindings at all, which skips
          encoding checks entirely. A `Bindings` is a single-method trait, so a lambda is one.
      entry .none   bindings under which no name resolves, so every declared encoding is an error
      entry .builtins   `base-256` and `schema-signature`, the two codecs BinTEL's acceptances need
      entry .orElse   `first.orElse(second)`: bindings resolving through the first, then the second
      entry #apply   the codec bound to a name, or `Unset`
    entry .Resolver   a resolve-once cache over a `Bindings`: each name is looked up at most once per run
      entry #apply   the codec for a name, cached, with a cached `Unset` recording a definitive unknown
    entry .base256   the `base-256` codec: a BASE-256 word's bytes, every byte sequence the encoding of exactly one text
        A rejection names every character outside the alphabet, as `Base256.decodeStrict`
        reports them.
    entry .schemaSignature   the `schema-signature` codec: as `base-256`, rejecting bytes which are not structurally a signature
        A signature is 33 bytes, or `37 + 2·(n − 2)` for `n ≥ 2` components, XORing to the
        cadence byte `0x79`; a malformed one is E312 at validation, before any palimpsest
        search runs.
    entry .signatureComponents   how many components a byte sequence encodes if it is structurally a signature, else `Unset`
    entry #encode   the bytes of an accepted text, or why the text was rejected
    entry #decode   the text of a byte sequence in the encoder's image, or why it failed
  entry .Encoded   declares, as a given, that a type's scalar text carries a named §21.7 encoding
    detail
        `given Money is Tel.Encoded["decimal-varint"] = Tel.Encoded()` says that `Money`'s
        TEL scalar form is to be written under the `decimal-varint` encoding. The name is a
        literal type, so the staged BinTEL parser reads it as the macro expands and the
        schema derivation reifies the same declaration into the derived `Tels.Scalar`'s
        `encoding`: one declaration, coherent across both layers. The type also needs a
        `Decodable in Text` (and, to encode, an `Encodable in Text`), since the codec maps
        the scalar's text to bytes.
    entry .apply   `Tel.Encoded[Self, "name"]()`: the instance, with the type parameters usually inferred from the given's type
  entry .Acceptance   what a reader can consume, told to a writer: schema signatures in order of preference, with options
    detail
        BinTEL §8.4. When two peers hold different schema libraries, a reader sends an
        acceptance: a list of `#alternatives`, each naming by `Signature` the composed schema
        it will read under (its *invocation* schema), whether it accepts a self-contained
        document, whether it can resolve any published layer of the base's lineage, and which
        further layers or atoms it would like included, as hash-prefix `Component`s. The
        message is itself a TEL document under the built-in `acceptance` schema, pinned as
        `specification.tel/acceptance:1.0.0`, and has three interchangeable forms: text
        (`#encode`, read back with `text.as[Tel.Acceptance]`), binary (`#framed` or the bare
        `#bintel`, read by `Acceptance(data)`), and a single BASE-256 word (`#text`, read by
        `Acceptance(text)`). Whichever travels, `valueHash` is that of the bare form.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        val acceptance = Tel.Acceptance
          ( Tel.Acceptance.Alternative
              ( Tel.Acceptance.Signature(contact.signature(List(t"with-address"))),
                components = List(Tel.Acceptance.Component(contact.prefix(t"with-phone"))) ),
            Tel.Acceptance.Alternative(Tel.Acceptance.Signature(contact.signature(List())), selfContained = true) )

        acceptance.encode                     // the TEL text
        acceptance.framed                     // a framed BinTEL document
        acceptance.encode.as[Tel.Acceptance]  // read back
        ```

        A writer answers with `.serve` (or, from a value with a derived schema, `fulfil`),
        choosing the first alternative it can satisfy and the richest composition the
        alternative permits; a reader decodes the reply with `.receive`, taking the first
        alternative whose invocation schema the arriving document's composed schema is a
        subtype of. Where the formats are Scala types, `Tel.Acceptance[(A, B)]()` derives
        the whole exchange and `#read` decodes what arrives as `A | B`.
    entry .apply   builds an acceptance from alternatives; reads one from bytes, BASE-256 text, a `Tel` or a `Tel.Element`
        `Acceptance(first, rest*)` lists alternatives in order of preference.
        `Acceptance(data)` reads a framed document — recognised by its magic number and
        required to carry the acceptance schema's signature, else
        `Acceptance.Error(WrongSchema)` — or the bare document root. `Acceptance(text)` reads
        the BASE-256 word strictly, raising `Base256.Error` for a character outside the
        alphabet. `Acceptance(tel)` reads a parsed document, whose pragma, if it identifies a
        schema, must identify this one by coordinate or signature. `Acceptance(element)` reads
        the semantic model, raising `Acceptance.Error(Malformed)` for any other shape. The
        inline `Acceptance[(A, B)](selfContained, anyPublished)` builds a `Typed` acceptance
        from a tuple of types.
    entry .reference   the pinned coordinate of the built-in schema: `specification.tel/acceptance:1.0.0`
    entry .signature   the built-in schema's 33-byte signature, as a BASE-256 word
    entry .signatureData   the built-in schema's signature, as bytes
    entry .decodable   reads an acceptance from TEL text: `text.as[Tel.Acceptance]`
        Needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]` and a `Tactic[Tel.Acceptance.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   writes an acceptance as TEL text: `acceptance.encode`
    entry .Served   what a writer produced for an alternative: the alternative, composition, signature, schema and framed document
      detail
          The result of `.serve` and `fulfil`. `#hashes` is the composition chosen, base
          first; `#signature` its schema signature; `#schema` the composed `Tels` the document
          is encoded under; and `#document` the framed BinTEL bytes, in external-schema mode,
          or self-contained when the alternative permits it and the composition includes a
          layer the reader did not name.
      entry #alternative   the alternative served
      entry #hashes   the component hashes of the composition served, the base first
      entry #signature   the signature of that composition
      entry #schema   the composed schema the document is encoded under
      entry #document   the framed document, to send
    entry .serve   the writer's side: the first alternative a held value can satisfy, and its document
        `serve(acceptance, held, composition, element, codecs)`: `held` is the writer's
        `SchemaSignature.Lineage`, `composition` the hashes the value is held under (base
        first), and `element` the typed value. An alternative is served when its requirement
        decodes against the lineage as a composition beginning with the base, and the value
        projects to a composition which is the requirement plus each further component of
        the writer's own, in order, that the alternative permits — named by it or a prefix,
        or any layer under `anyPublished` or `selfContained` — and that keeps a subtype of the
        requirement. `Unset` when no alternative can be served. Needs tactics for
        `Tel.Error`, `Bintel.Error` and `Tels.Resolution.Error`.
    entry .Reading   a reader's reading of a document: the alternative, the document's composed schema and the invocation schema
      entry #alternative   the alternative the document was served under
      entry #document   the composed schema to decode under, with every component the writer included
      entry #invocation   the alternative's own schema, to project to
    entry .served   which alternative a document was served under, resolved against a library
        `served(acceptance, library, documentSignature)` resolves the signature in the
        `SchemaSignature.Library` and takes the first alternative whose schema the composed
        schema is a subtype of; `Unset` when none is.
    entry .receive   the reader's side: resolve, decode under the alternative served, and project to its schema
        `receive(acceptance, library, data, codecs)` unframes the document, finds its
        `Reading`, decodes the body under the document's composed schema and projects the
        element to the invocation schema. `Unset` when no alternative matches.
    entry .Typed   an acceptance derived from a tuple of Scala types, reading what arrives as their union
      detail
          `Tel.Acceptance[(Contact, ContactV1)]()` makes one alternative per member of the
          tuple, in its order of preference: each requires the type's derived base — its
          required fields and the definitions they reach — and offers its optional fields as
          atoms and its `@assign` layer groups as layers. The tuple carries the order a union
          type cannot. A reply is decoded with `#read` to `Contact | ContactV1`, for the
          caller to match on; a writer of an older format is read as the most preferred
          format whose schema its document's is a subtype of.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          val accepting = Tel.Acceptance[(Contact, ContactV1)]()
          send(accepting.acceptance.framed)

          accepting.read(reply) match
            case contact: Contact   => contact.name
            case contact: ContactV1 => contact.name
          ```
      entry #acceptance   the derived `Tel.Acceptance`, to send
      entry #lineages   the derived lineage of each format, in order
      entry #selfContained   whether every alternative accepts a self-contained document
      entry #anyPublished   whether every alternative accepts any published layer
      entry #otherwise   a further, less preferred format: `accepting.otherwise[ContactV0]`
      entry #read   decodes a framed reply as the member whose alternative was served, typed as the tuple's union
          Takes an optional `SchemaSignature.Library` for compositions beyond the derived
          lineages; raises `Acceptance.Error(Unread)` when no alternative's schema is a
          supertype of the document's.
      entry #reading   the position of the alternative served and the document presented under its composed schema
    entry .nameOf   the name a type's derived schema carries: the type's simple name in kebab-case
    entry .lineage   the `SchemaSignature.Lineage` a type's derived schema forms: required fields as the base, optional ones as atoms
    entry .alternative   one alternative for a lineage: its base alone required, every layer and atom offered by shortest prefix
    entry .lineages   the lineages of a tuple of types, in order
    entry .decoders   the decoders of a tuple of types, in order, each from a presented document
    entry .Alternative   one composition a reader accepts: its signature, the two flags and the components it would like
      detail
          `Alternative(schema, selfContained = false, anyPublished = false, components =
          Nil)`. The flags say whether the reader accepts a document which embeds its own
          schema, and whether the writer may include any published layer of the lineage
          without being asked. Member order on the `accept` line is load-bearing: the schema
          atom first, then the flags, then every component — a flag written after a component
          would be read as a component and rejected by its codec.
      entry #schema   the invocation schema's signature
      entry #selfContained   whether a self-contained document is accepted
      entry #anyPublished   whether any published layer may be included
      entry #components   the further components the reader can resolve and would like included
      entry #compound   the `accept` compound of the acceptance document's text form
    entry .Signature   a schema signature: bytes which are structurally a signature, naming component hashes
      detail
          A palimpsest of component hashes at the BinTEL-pinned parameters: 33 bytes for a
          base alone, or `37 + 2·(n − 2)` for `n` components, XORing to the cadence byte
          `0x79`. Which hashes it names is a question for a library holding them
          (`SchemaSignature.decode`), not for the value. Two signatures are equal when their
          bytes are.
      entry .parse   a signature from bytes, or `Unset` if they are not structurally one
      entry .apply   a signature from bytes, raising `Acceptance.Error(MalformedSignature)` otherwise
      entry #data   the bytes
      entry #count   how many components the signature names
      entry #basePrefix   the first four bytes, the base component's hash prefix, uncontested
      entry #text   the signature as a BASE-256 word
    entry .Component   a further component of a lineage, named by its 32-byte hash or a prefix of at least four bytes
      detail
          A prefix denotes the one component of the writer's lineage whose hash begins with
          it; a prefix matching none or several denotes nothing. `Tel.Acceptance.alternative`
          names each offered component by the shortest prefix which identifies it.
      entry .parse   a component from four to thirty-two bytes, or `Unset`
      entry .apply   a component from bytes, raising `Acceptance.Error(ComponentLength)` outside four to thirty-two
      entry #prefix   the hash or hash prefix
      entry #full   whether this names a component by its full 32-byte hash
      entry #text   the prefix as a BASE-256 word
    entry .Error   raised when an acceptance, or a document answering one, is invalid
      detail
          Error 612, sub-numbered by reason: _the acceptance is invalid because …_:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `MalformedSignature(length)`  1  _a `length`-byte value is not structurally a schema signature_
          row  `ComponentLength(length)`  2  _a component must be four to thirty-two bytes long, not `length`_
          row  `WrongSchema(identifier)`  3  _the document identifies its schema as `identifier`, not the acceptance schema_
          row  `Malformed`  4  _the semantic model does not have the shape of an acceptance_
          row  `Unread`  5  _no alternative's schema is a supertype of the document's composed schema_  a reply
          ```
      entry .Reason   the five reasons, each with its number and message
      entry #reason   why the acceptance is invalid
    entry #alternatives   the alternatives, in order of preference
    entry #encode   the text form: a TEL document with the pinned pragma and one `accept` line per alternative
        A member rather than only the `Encodable`'s extension, because within `Tel`'s
        implicit scope the derived `Tel.Encodable`'s `encode` would shadow it.
    entry #tel   the text form as a `Tel`
    entry #element   the semantic model under the built-in schema, validated with the two codecs bound
        A signature which is not structurally one is reported here as E312, and a component
        outside four to thirty-two bytes as E315.
    entry #bintel   the bare binary form: the document root alone, not self-framing
    entry #framed   the framed binary form, 39 bytes longer than the bare form and self-framing
    entry #text   the bare form as one BASE-256 word
  entry .Element   the semantic model: a document typed against its schema, as nodes and values
    detail
        TEL §18.2, the result of `Tel.Type.assign` and of `Bintel.decode`. A `Node` is a
        struct, flag or document root, with its member's keyword index, its type and its
        children in member order; a `Value` is a scalar, with its keyword index, its
        `Tels.Scalar` type and its text. Reference types never appear: assignment resolves
        them. An element encodes to BinTEL with `bintel(schema)` and hashes with
        `valueHash(schema)`, and `Bintel.present` renders it back to a `Tel`.
    entry .Node   a struct, flag or root: a keyword index (`Unset` at the root), its type and its children
      entry #keywordIndex   the index of the member's keyword in its parent, or `Unset` for the document root
      entry #elementType   the `Tels.Type` assigned: a `Tels.Struct`, or `Tels.Flag`
      entry #children   the child elements, in member order, then document order within a member
    entry .Value   a scalar: a keyword index, its scalar type and its text
      entry #keywordIndex   the index of the member's keyword in its parent
      entry #scalarType   the `Tels.Scalar` assigned, with its validators, patterns and encoding
      entry #text   the scalar's text
  entry .Pointer   a logical address in a document: a sequence of keyword steps, each optionally indexed
    detail
        `Tel.Pointer.of(t"person", t"name")` names the `name` child of the first `person`
        compound; a step with an index, `pointer / (t"item", 2)`, selects among siblings
        sharing a keyword, and `Unset` means the first. The pointer is what `Mutation.Op`s,
        `Revision.at` and `Tel.Handle`'s operations are addressed by; `Pointer.Empty` is the
        document root. A `Telp`, by contrast, is the textual path language, used by foci and
        `locate`.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label Parse a document and read a field
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document = t"# the cast\nname Percy  # lead\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
              document.field(t"name").let(_.primaryAtom)   // t"Percy"
        frame
          label Address the field with a pointer and rewrite its atom
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document = t"# the cast\nname Percy  # lead\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
              val pointer = Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")
              val edited = Tel.Mutation(document, Tel.Mutation.Op.UpdateAtom(pointer, 0, t"Hal"))
              edited.show   // t"# the cast\nname Hal  # lead\nage 30\n": comment and remark intact
        frame
          label Insert a sibling after it
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document = t"# the cast\nname Percy  # lead\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
              val pointer = Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")
              val edited = Tel.Mutation(document, Tel.Mutation.Op.UpdateAtom(pointer, 0, t"Hal"))
              val email = Tel.Compound(t"email", Array(Tel.Atom.Inline(t"hal@example.com", 1)), Unset, Array.empty)
              Tel.Mutation(edited, Tel.Mutation.Op.InsertAfter(pointer, email)).show
              // t"# the cast\nname Hal  # lead\nemail hal@example.com\nage 30\n"
        ##
        ```
    entry .Step   one step: a keyword, and an optional occurrence index among siblings sharing it
    entry .Empty   the pointer to the document root
    entry .of   a pointer from keywords, each selecting the first occurrence
    entry #steps   the steps, root first
    entry #/   `pointer / keyword` or `pointer / (keyword, index)`: the pointer extended by one step
    entry #isEmpty   whether this is the root pointer
  entry .LineEndings   a document's line endings: `Lf` or `Crlf`, kept through a round trip
  entry .Pragma   the `tel 1.0 …` line: version, schema reference, layer selections, signature and sigil
    detail
        The pragma's positional parameters (TEL §8), each classified by form: the version
        pair, an optional `Reference` to the schema, the layers selected in the schema's
        declaration order, an optional BASE-256 schema signature, and an optional comment
        sigil replacing `#`. `Tel.Metadata` carries a loaded document's pragma, and
        `Tel.Document#pragma` holds it on the tree.
    entry .Reference   a schema coordinate, `domain/name[:selector]`: a published release by version or tag, or a local handle
      detail
          A LIRA schema reference (§8.1). The domain is a DNS name, the module name is
          kebab-case segments joined by `/` or `.`, and a selector beginning with a digit is
          exactly `x.y.z` while one beginning with a letter is a tag. A reference with no
          selector is a local-only development handle. `.tels` and `.acceptance` are the
          pinned coordinates of the two built-in schemas, which `SchemaResolver` recognises
          without network access.
      entry .Selector   `Version(major, minor, patch)` or `Tag(name)`, each rendering as `#text`
      entry .tels   `specification.tel/tels:2.1.0`, the built-in meta-schema's coordinate
      entry .telsEarlier   `specification.tel/tels:2.0.0`, the earlier pin, which the built-in still answers
      entry .acceptance   `specification.tel/acceptance:1.0.0`, the built-in acceptance schema's coordinate
      entry .parse   a reference from its text, or `Unset` for any grammar violation
      entry #domain   the DNS domain
      entry #name   the module name
      entry #selector   the version or tag, or `Unset` for a local handle
      entry #text   the reference as written: `domain/name:selector`
      entry #isTels   whether this is the built-in meta-schema's coordinate, with or without its pin
      entry #is   whether this names the same module as another, ignoring selectors
    entry #version   the TEL version, as a `(major, minor)` pair
    entry #reference   the schema reference, if the pragma names one
    entry #layers   the layer selections, `+name` each, in declaration order
    entry #signature   the schema signature as a BASE-256 word, if given
    entry #sigil   the comment sigil, if one other than `#` is declared
  entry .Metadata   a loaded document's prologue: interpreter directive, pragma, line endings and continuation
    detail
        What `text.load[Tel]` pairs with the value, and what `Tel.Handle#metadata` reports,
        so a caller can read the schema identifier and the line endings without walking the
        tree. `#continuation` is the §6.1 continuation: the 1-indexed source line on which the
        content after a `##` separator begins, or `Unset` when the document ended at the end
        of its input. It is how a caller reaches content a TEL header prefixes, since the
        separator is the only framing and the parser does not read past it.
    entry #interpreterDirective   the `#!` line's payload, if any
    entry #pragma   the pragma, if any
    entry #lineEndings   `Lf` or `Crlf`
    entry #continuation   the source line after the document separator, or `Unset`
  entry .Subtree   what a `Tel` wraps: a `Document` or a `Compound`, each with child blocks
    entry #children   the node's blocks
  entry .Document   the root of a parsed document: prologue, margin, blocks and continuation
    detail
        TEL §17. The `#margin` is the document margin (§7), re-emitted on every line when the
        document is serialized. A `Document` renders with `show` (`.documentShowable`). A
        document built by hand for an encoded value, as `Tel.compound` and `show` do, has no
        directive, no pragma, `Lf` endings and a zero margin.
    entry #interpreterDirective   the `#!` line's payload, if any
    entry #pragma   the pragma, if any
    entry #lineEndings   the line endings the source used
    entry #margin   the document margin, in spaces
    entry #children   the top-level blocks
    entry #continuation   the source line after the `##` separator, or `Unset` at end of input
  entry .Block   a run of comments, an optional tabulation header, compounds, and trailing blank lines
    detail
        Blocks are how blank lines and comments survive: a document's children are blocks,
        each a group of compounds preceded by its comments and followed by
        `#trailingBlankLines` empty lines. The document's final newline is the last block's
        single trailing blank line — for a compound with children, the innermost last child
        block's — and every mutation conserves these counts, so a document neither gains nor
        loses its final newline through an edit.
    entry #comments   the comment lines preceding the compounds
    entry #tabulation   the tabulation header, if the block's compounds are tabulated rows
    entry #compounds   the compounds, in order
    entry #trailingBlankLines   how many blank lines follow the block
  entry .Comment   a comment line's text, without its sigil
    entry #text   the comment's text
  entry .Tabulation   a tabulation header: the column marker offsets and the heading of each column
    entry #markerOffsets   the column at which each sigil marker sits
    entry #headings   the heading text after each marker
  entry .Compound   one keyword line: the keyword, its atoms, an optional remark and its child blocks
    detail
        The unit of a TEL document. `Tel.Compound(t"name", Array(Tel.Atom.Inline(t"Percy",
        1)), Unset, Array.empty)` is the line `name Percy`; a remark is the `# …` after two
        spaces at the end of the line; children are the indented blocks beneath. A compound
        is wrapped as a `Tel` with `Tel.make`, and `Tel.compound` builds one from a keyword,
        atoms and child compounds in one call.
    entry #keyword   the keyword
    entry #atoms   the atoms: inline ones, then a source or literal payload if one follows the line
    entry #remark   the end-of-line remark, if any
    entry #children   the child blocks
  entry .Atom   a value on or after a compound line: `Inline` on the line, `Source` indented beneath, `Literal` between delimiters
    detail
        TEL has three atom forms. An `Inline` atom is a phrase on the keyword's line,
        separated from the previous one by `precedingSpaces` — one space in the ordinary
        case, two or more for a *hard* space, which lets a phrase contain single spaces. A
        `Source` atom (§14) is a multi-line payload indented two levels beneath the compound,
        with its trailing blank lines discarded. A `Literal` atom (§15) is a payload between
        two delimiter lines, taken verbatim. An encoder chooses the form which reads back as
        written (`Mutation.chooseAtomForm`): inline unless the text has a line break or a
        space at either edge. Once any inline atom's value contains a space, that atom and
        every later one on the line are hard-space-preceded, so a constructed line stays
        round-trippable.
    entry .Inline   an atom on the keyword line, with the spaces which precede it
      detail
          `Tel.Atom.Inline(text, precedingSpaces)` builds one, and the same shape is the
          extractor. The text of a parsed atom is decoded from the parser's byte arena only
          when first read, so a consumer which never inspects an atom pays nothing for it;
          two atoms are equal when their texts and spacing are.
      entry .apply   an inline atom from its text and preceding-space count
      entry .unapply   the extractor, `case Tel.Atom.Inline(text, spaces)`
      entry #text   the atom's text
      entry #precedingSpaces   how many spaces precede it on the line: one, or two or more for a hard space
    entry .Source   a multi-line payload indented beneath the compound
      entry #text   the payload, its lines joined by line feeds
    entry .Literal   a verbatim payload between two delimiter lines
      entry #delimiter   the delimiter line's text
      entry #text   the payload
  entry .PositionIndex   the packed source positions a document parsed under `parsing.trackPositions` carries
      An opaque array of descriptors, one per compound, recording the keyword's line, column
      and length and the inline-atom run's column and length, laid out so that any subtree's
      slice is itself an index. It is built only when `parsing.trackPositions` is in scope,
      so the throughput path is untouched, and it is what `tel.locate`, `tel.locateKey` and
      `tel.enclosing` read. It has no public constructor.
  entry .positionable   resolves a `Telp` to a source position in a tracked document: `tel.locate`, `tel.locateKey`
      The `Positionable` given, in implicit scope. `tel.locate(path)` is the position of the
      compound's value — its inline-atom run, falling back to the keyword when it has none,
      as for a parent, a flag or a payload on later lines — and `tel.locateKey(path)` the
      keyword's. An all-digit segment after a keyword is a zero-based occurrence index among
      siblings sharing it, so `telp"/item/1/name"` is the second item's name. Both are
      `Unset` without a position index, for an unresolvable path, and for `locateKey` at the
      root, which has no keyword.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import parsing.trackPositions

      val tel = t"person\n  name Percy\n".read[Tel]
      tel.locate(telp"/person/name")      // line 2, column 8, length 5
      tel.locateKey(telp"/person/name")   // line 2, column 3, length 4
      ```
  entry .Enclosure   the result of `tel.enclosing`: the path to the innermost compound at a coordinate, and its region
    entry #path   the `Telp` reaching the compound
    entry #position   the region to highlight: the inline-atom run when the coordinate is in it, else the keyword
  entry .aggregable   `source.read[Tel]`: parses any byte source as one document
      The `Aggregable by Data` given, in implicit scope. A stream is parsed as it arrives,
      rather than gathered first, and the parser recovers from faults as TEL §19.5
      prescribes — an odd indentation, trailing spaces, a misplaced comment — so a document
      with recoverable mistakes still yields a tree, and raises `Tel.Error` for the fault
      with its line and column. Single-document parsing stops at the first `##` separator;
      content after it is ignored (`read[List[Tel]]`, `read[Chain[Tel]]` or `documents` read
      a multi-document source). The metadata is not surfaced: `load[Tel]` recovers it
      alongside the value. Needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]` and a `Buffering`; with
      `parsing.trackPositions` in scope the result carries a `Tel.PositionIndex`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
      ```
  entry .aggregableParsed   `source.read[T in Tel]` for a type with a `Tel.Parsable`: direct parsing, no tree
      In implicit scope, and chosen over `Tel.aggregableIn` whenever the type has a
      `Tel.Parsable`. The value is parsed straight from the bytes through a `TelReader`.
      Without a position index to resolve against, `parsing.trackPositions` instead makes
      each focus and error take its span from the parser as it goes.
  entry .readableParsed   `data.read[T in Tel]` of bytes already in hand, through a `Tel.Parsable`
      Concrete in `Data`, so it beats the composed stream pipeline when the whole content is
      present: the bytes are parsed in place rather than wrapped in a one-element stream.
  entry .textParsable   direct parsing of a `Text` scalar: the entry's atom
      A missing atom raises `Absent` (401); the field's value is the atom text.
  entry .stringParsable   direct parsing of a `String` scalar
  entry .intParsable   direct parsing of an `Int`, read from the atom's bytes without building its text
      A missing atom raises `Absent` and an unparseable one `NotScalar(text, t"Int")` (402),
      so the direct and tree paths report the same errors.
  entry .longParsable   direct parsing of a `Long`, read from the atom's bytes
  entry .doubleParsable   direct parsing of a `Double`, through the atom's text
  entry .booleanParsable   direct parsing of a `Boolean` from an explicit `true` or `false` atom
  entry .telParsable   `Tel` read directly, through the tree bridge over `Tel.telDecodable`
  entry .iterableParsable   a collection of directly-parsable elements, for any `Iterable` with a `Factory`
      Nominal: the element type must itself have a `Tel.Parsable`, so the read trigger stays
      opt-in. Every same-keyword occurrence is gathered.
  entry .listParsable   a `List` of directly-parsable elements
  entry .setParsable   a `Set` of directly-parsable elements
  entry .seriesParsable   a `Sequence` of directly-parsable elements
  entry .listAggregable   `source.read[List[Tel]]`: every document of a multi-document source, parsed eagerly
      TEL §6.1: documents are separated by `##` lines. The whole source is parsed before the
      list is returned; `read[Chain[Tel]]` and the `documents` extension parse lazily.
  entry .streamAggregable   `source.read[Chain[Tel]]`: the documents of a multi-document source, parsed on demand
      More specific than turbulence's generic `Chain` aggregable, which would otherwise wrap
      the whole source as a single element.
  entry .loadable   `text.load[Tel]`: a document with its `Tel.Metadata`, parsed from text as it arrives
      The text is transcoded to UTF-8, the parser's own input, and parsed as it arrives; the
      result is a `Document[Tel]` pairing the value with its interpreter directive, pragma,
      line endings and continuation. Under `parsing.trackPositions` the loaded document keeps
      its position index.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val loaded = t"tel 1.0 example.com/contact\n\nname Percy\n".load[Tel]
      loaded.metadata.pragma.let(_.reference).let(_.text)   // t"example.com/contact"
      ```
  entry .loadableData   `bytes.load[Tel]`: as `.loadable`, from UTF-8 bytes with no transcoding
  entry .documentShowable   renders a `Tel.Document` back to text, reversing the parser
      The single TEL serializer: each line is written with the document's line ending between
      lines and never after the last, trailing blank-line counts realised as that many empty
      lines, comments and tabulation headers re-emitted at their columns, and source and
      literal payloads re-indented. A document which was parsed renders byte-for-byte as it
      was read.
  entry .showable   `tel.show`: renders any `Tel` as text, wrapping a compound-rooted value in a document first
      A `Tel` from a parse is document-rooted and renders presentation-preservingly; one from
      `in[Tel]` is compound-rooted, so its children are wrapped in a document, otherwise an
      encoded value would render as empty text. An empty inline atom serializes as no atom at
      all: `Tel.scalar(t"")` under a keyword renders as the bare keyword.
  entry .streamable   a `Tel` as a stream of text blocks, rendered on a fiber: `tel.stream[Text]`
      Built on `.emit`, so it needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate` in scope.
  entry .emit   serializes a document in blocks: pulled through an iterator from a fiber, or pushed to a function
      Two forms. `Tel.emit(tel)` serializes on a fiber and hands out text through an
      `Iterator[Text]` as it is produced, so a large document can be written to a socket
      before it is fully rendered; it needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate`, as under
      `supervise`. `Tel.emit[Text](tel, deliver)` and `Tel.emit[Data](tel, deliver)` are the
      push form: serialized on the caller's thread, each block handed to `deliver` as it
      fills — as text, or as UTF-8 bytes written straight from the serializer with no
      intermediate text — which is the shape for a file, a socket or an `OutputStream`, with
      no thread handoff. Both need a `Buffering`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Tel.emit[Data](document, chunk => socket.write(chunk))

      supervise:
        Tel.emit(document).each(chunk => out.write(chunk))
      ```
  entry .Emitter   how the push form of `emit` reaches its consumer: as text, or as UTF-8 bytes
    detail
        `Emitter[Text]` delivers text blocks through a `Producer[Text]`, the same route
        `show` renders by; `Emitter[Data]` writes UTF-8 directly, copying a parsed atom's
        bytes from the parser's arena without decoding them. Both are givens in the
        companion, chosen by `emit`'s type argument.
    entry .text   the `Emitter[Text]`
    entry .data   the `Emitter[Data]`
    entry #emit   serializes a document, delivering each block as it fills
  entry .lend   serializes to UTF-8 on the caller's thread, lending each filled block without copying
      `Tel.lend(tel)(region => interval => …)`: each block is lent as a `Region[Data]` with
      its branded extent, valid only for the duration of the call, the discipline of
      `Stream.lend`. The borrowing form of the push `emit`; `emit[Data]` is this with each
      block materialized.
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering: the document on one line as `tel"…"`, line breaks escaped
      TEL's block syntax is multi-line and an inspection is not, so the rendered document has
      its line breaks escaped, visibly distinct from the `Text` holding the same document.
  entry .make   wraps a `Tel.Subtree` — a `Document` or a `Compound` — as a `Tel`
      The constructor the `tel"…"` interpolator and the mutation operations use, since the
      class's own constructor is not public.
  entry .Error   raised by parsing, schema validation and decoding: a TEL error code and a source span
    detail
        Error 605, sub-numbered by the reason's TEL code: _the TEL document is invalid at line
        2, columns 5-8 because …_, or without the position when the span is empty. The
        reasons are the specification's codes, tabulated under `Tel.Error.Reason`: E1xx for
        parse faults, each with a §19.5 `Recovery` the parser applies so that it can
        continue; E2xx for schema validity; E3xx for a document against its schema; E4xx for
        decoding into a Scala type; and the implementation-reserved E501.

        A parse error carries its `#span`; a validation or decode error leaves the span
        empty and carries its location on the accrued `Tel.Focus` instead, as a `Telp` and,
        for a document parsed under `parsing.trackPositions`, the span filled in at the end
        of the walk. Under a `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary the E3xx and E4xx reasons accrue
        — the offending node is discarded and its siblings still checked — while the E2xx
        reasons abort, since a malformed schema offers no document-level recovery.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        safely(t"name Percy\n  age 30\n".read[Tel])   // Unset: E111, over-indentation
        ```
    entry .Position   a 1-indexed source position: line, column, and optionally an offset and a token length
      detail
          Where the parser raised an error, and the `Positionable` result of `tel.locate` and
          `tel.locateKey`. `column = 1` is the first character of the line, leading spaces
          included; an error before the body, such as a byte-order mark, reports `(1, 1)`.
          `#length` is the located token's length in characters, for a precise editor range.
          `#span` is the same position as a 0-based `Span`. Aliased as `Tel.Position`.
      entry .communicable   renders a position as _line 2, column 5_
      entry #line   the 1-indexed line
      entry #column   the 1-indexed column
      entry #offset   the character offset, if known
      entry #length   the token's length in characters, if known
      entry #describe   _line 2, column 5_
      entry #span   the position as a 0-based `Span`
    entry .describe   renders a span in the parser's 1-indexed terms: _line 2, column 5_ or _line 2, columns 5-8_
    entry .spanAt   a `Line`-mode `Span` from a 1-indexed line, column and length, clamped rather than wrapped
    entry .Recovery   the §19.5 recovery strategy the parser applies after each kind of fault
        `SkipBom`, `RestartFromPragma`, `AllowOversize`, `IgnoreVersion`, `UseDefaultSigil`,
        `AdjustMargin`, `ShallowerIndent`, `StripTrailing`, `AttachComment`,
        `SkipOverIndented`, `IgnoreDuplicateAtom`, `PayloadToEof`,
        `SuppressColumnAlignment`, `CollapseLineEndings`, `IgnorePhrase`,
        `IgnoreMisplacedPhrase`, `ReorderLayerSelections` and, for every schema and
        validation fault, `IgnoreErroneousNode`. `Reason.recoveryOf` gives a reason's
        strategy.
    entry .Reason   the error codes, E101 to E501, each with its number and message
      detail
          An enum with the TEL code as its `number`. `Reason.recoveryOf(reason)` is the
          parser's §19.5 strategy for a parse or validation reason, and `Unset` for a decode
          reason (`Absent`, `NotScalar`) or `NestingLimitExceeded`, which have no parser-level
          recovery.

          E1xx are parse faults, E2xx faults in a schema, E3xx faults of a document against its
          schema, E4xx faults in decoding to a Scala type, and E501 is implementation-reserved:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          row  `BomPresent`  101  _the document begins with a byte-order mark_
          row  `PragmaNotFirst`  102  _the pragma is not the first non-blank line_
          row  `PragmaTooLong`  103  _the pragma extends beyond the first 4096 bytes_
          row  `BadVersion`  104  _the pragma version is not of the form x.y_
          row  `BadSigil`  105  _the pragma sigil is not a permitted symbolic character_
          row  `LessThanMargin`  106  _the line begins with fewer spaces than the document margin_
          row  `OddIndentation`  107  _the relative indentation after the margin is odd_
          row  `TrailingSpaces`  108  _the non-blank line has trailing spaces_
          row  `CommentNotPreceded`  109  _the comment is not preceded by a blank line, comment, start, or lesser indent_
          row  `UnmatchedDedent`  110  _the line indent does not match any open compound_
          row  `OverIndentation`  111  _the line is indented more than one level deeper than the previous compound_
          row  `ChildOfNonCompound`  112  _the line is indented as a child of a tabulated row_
          row  `DuplicateSource`  113  _the compound already has a source or literal atom_
          row  `DuplicateLiteral`  114  _the compound already has a source or literal atom_
          row  `UnclosedLiteral`  115  _the literal atom reaches end of file before its closing delimiter_
          row  `RowWrongIndent`  116  _the tabulated row has a different indent from its tabulation line_
          row  `HardSpaceWrongPosition`  117  _a hard space or consecutive-space run does not end at a column boundary_
          row  `ColumnValueTooWide`  118  _the column value or keyword portion overflows into the next column's separator_
          row  `BadTabulationHeading`  119  _the tabulation line heading is malformed_
          row  `BadLineEnding`  120  _line endings are inconsistent or a CR is not followed by an LF_
          row  `BadPragmaPhrase`  121  _the pragma phrase matches no pragma form_
          row  `MisplacedPragmaPhrase`  122  _the pragma phrase violates the pragma's phrase order or multiplicity_
          row  `LayerOrderMismatch`  124  _the layer selections are not in the schema's declaration order_
          row  `DuplicateKeywordInStruct`  201  _the same keyword appears more than once in a struct_
          row  `EmptySelectVariants`  202  _the SelectDefinition has an empty variants list_
          row  `DefaultOnOptional`  203  _a non-required member must not specify a default_
          row  `DuplicateLayerName`  204  _two or more layers share the same name_
          row  `LayerKeywordCollision`  205  _the layer introduces a keyword colliding with the base_
          row  `LayerFieldTypeMismatch`  206  _a layer Field's declared type conflicts with the base_
          row  `BadSchemaSigil`  207  _the schema sigil is not a permitted symbolic character_
          row  `TelKeywordReserved`  208  _the keyword `tel` is reserved in user schemas_
          row  `UnresolvedReference`  209  _a Reference or SelectRef names an undeclared TypeName_
          row  `DuplicateDefinition`  210  _two or more Definitions share the same name_
          row  `ExcludeMissingVariant`  211  _Exclude names a variant absent from the base_
          row  `ExcludeEmptiesRequired`  212  _Exclude would empty a SelectDefinition that a required SelectRef references_
          row  `LayerVariantAddition`  213  _a layer SelectDefinition introduces a variant absent from the base_
          row  `LayerLoosenRequired`  214  _a layer cannot loosen the `required` axis_
          row  `LayerLoosenRepeatable`  215  _a layer cannot loosen the `repeatable` axis_
          row  `ExcludeOutsideSelect`  216  _Exclude appears outside a SelectDefinition body_
          row  `ReferenceKindMismatch`  217  _a Reference / SelectRef resolves to a Definition of the wrong kind_
          row  `EncodingConflict`  218  _a layer declares an encoding conflicting with the base ScalarDefinition's_
          row  `KeyOnNonScalar`  219  _a key field's type does not resolve to a Scalar_
          row  `KeyOnLooseMember`  220  _a key field must be effectively required and non-repeatable_
          row  `MultipleKeyFields`  221  _more than one member of the Struct is key-flagged_
          row  `InvalidPattern`  222  _a `pattern` value is not a valid RE2 pattern_
          row  `PatternNotContained`  223  _a layer's patterns are not contained in the patterns they replace_
          row  `WidthNotDecimal`  225  _a `width` is not a canonical positive decimal_
          row  `WidthConflict`  226  _a `width` conflicts with the width already declared, or with its codec's fixed width_
          row  `WidthWithoutEncoding`  227  _a scalar declares a `width` but no `encoding`_
          row  `IndexNotApplicable`  228  _an `index` is declared on a field which is not repeatable, or not a Struct or Scalar_
          row  `IndexPathInvalid`  229  _an `index` path is not a valid TELP, or is the root path on a Struct-typed field_
          row  `IndexPathUnresolved`  230  _an `index` path does not resolve to a Scalar within the field's type_
          row  `WidthWithIntern`  231  _a scalar declares both a `width` and `intern`_
          row  `NonStructCompound`  301  _the compound's type is not a Struct_
          row  `TooManyAtoms`  302  _more atoms than assignable member positions_
          row  `AtomAtNonAssignablePos`  303  _the atom is at a non-atom-assignable member position_
          row  `AtomVariantUnmatched`  304  _the atom text matches no variant keyword of the SelectRef_
          row  `AtomFlagKeywordMismatch`  305  _the atom text does not match the Flag member's keyword_
          row  `UnknownKeyword`  306  _the compound keyword is not recognised for the parent_
          row  `RequiredMemberAbsent`  307  _a required member is absent and has no default_
          row  `NonRepeatableTooMany`  308  _a non-repeatable member is filled more than once_
          row  `MembersNonContiguous`  309  _compound children of the same member are not contiguous_
          row  `ValidatorRejected(message)`  310  _a scalar value or struct failed a named validator: `message`_
          row  `FlagWithContent`  311  _the Flag-typed compound has atoms or compound children_
          row  `EncodingRejected`  312  _a scalar value was rejected by its encoding's codec_
          row  `EncodingUnresolved`  313  _a scalar's declared encoding is not bound to a codec_
          row  `DuplicateKeyValue`  314  _two keyed children of the same parent have equal key values_
          row  `PatternRejected`  315  _a scalar value does not match a declared pattern_
          row  `Absent`  401  _a required value was absent_
          row  `NotScalar(value, expected)`  402  _the value `value` could not be parsed as `expected`_
          row  `NestingLimitExceeded`  501  _the document nesting exceeds the supported limit of 256_
          ```
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message: _the line is indented more than one level deeper …_
      entry .recoveryOf   the `Recovery` a reason prescribes, or `Unset` for a decode reason
    entry #reason   the error code and its detail
    entry #span   where in the source, as a `Line`-mode `Span`; empty for validation and decode errors
  entry .Flag   the flags of `open[Tel]`: `Force`, to write back even when nothing was mutated
      `source.open[Tel](Read & Write, Tel.Flag.Force)` re-serializes and writes the document
      on scope close whether or not an operation was applied: a canonicalizing rewrite.
  entry .Handle   the scoped handle to a TEL document under edit, inside `source.open[Tel](…)`
    detail
        `source.open[Tel](Read & Write) { handle ?=> … }` parses the source on entry and
        provides a `Handle` for the block. With the `Read` grant the handle offers `.current`,
        an immutable `Tel` snapshot which may outlive the block, and `.metadata`; with the
        `Write` grant it offers the TEL §22.2 machine operations — `.update`, `.insert`,
        `.remove`, `.replace`, `.attachRemark`, `.setFlag`, `.reorderGroups`, … — each
        addressed by a `Tel.Pointer` and applied eagerly: a rejected operation aborts
        `Mutation.Error` at its call site and leaves the document unchanged, so the block can
        carry on editing. On normal completion of a writable open the document is serialized,
        preserving its directive, pragma and line endings, and written back to the source; an
        abort or an exception skips the write-back, and so does an unmutated document unless
        `Tel.Flag.Force` was passed. The operations are extensions on the companion, gated by
        the grant.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        t"name Percy\n".open[Tel]() { handle ?=> handle.current.show }   // t"name Percy\n"
        ```

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        file.open[Tel](Read & Write): handle ?=>
          handle.update(Tel.Pointer.of(t"name"), t"Hal")
          handle.insertAfter(Tel.Pointer.of(t"name"), email)
          handle.attachRemark(Tel.Pointer.of(t"age"), t"checked")
        ```
    entry .current   the current document, as an immutable `Tel` snapshot (needs the `Read` grant)
        Named `current` because `tel` is the generic encoding extension and `snapshot` is
        claimed elsewhere.
    entry .metadata   the document's `Tel.Metadata`, or `Unset` for a compound-rooted document
    entry .mutate   applies any `Mutation.Op` to the document (needs the `Write` grant)
    entry .revise   applies a composed `Revision` to the document
    entry .update   §22.2 `update-value`: rewrites the primary atom, or the `atomIndex`-th, at a pointer
    entry .insert   §22.2 `insert`: a compound at its natural position under the parent at a pointer
    entry .insertBefore   inserts a compound before the one at a pointer
    entry .insertAfter   inserts a compound after the one at a pointer
    entry .insertIntoBlock   §22.2 `insert-into-block`: appends a row to the `blockIndex`-th child block of a parent
    entry .remove   §22.2 `delete`: removes the compound at a pointer (named `remove`, as `delete` is galilei's)
    entry .replace   replaces the compound at a pointer
    entry .attachRemark   attaches an end-of-line remark to the compound at a pointer
    entry .removeRemark   removes the remark from the compound at a pointer; a no-op success if it has none
    entry .setFlag   sets a flag keyword under the parent at a pointer
    entry .unsetFlag   unsets a flag keyword under the parent at a pointer; a no-op success if absent
    entry .reorderWithinGroup   moves one of the siblings sharing a keyword from one index to another
    entry .reorderGroups   places the group of one keyword before or after the group of another, per `Mutation.Placement`
    entry .resizeTabulation   re-plans a tabulated block's rows at a pointer and block index
    entry #current0   the document the handle holds; read through `.current`
    entry #dirty0   whether an operation has been applied; decides the write-back
    entry #metadata0   the document's metadata; read through `.metadata`
    entry #mutate0   applies an operation; the seam `.mutate` and the named operations go through
    entry #revise0   applies a revision; the seam `.revise` goes through
  entry .Openable   the `Openable` class behind `.telOpenable`: parse on entry, write back on normal close
    detail
        A named class rather than an anonymous given instance, so that the handle's
        capability type is not freshened in the inferred `Result`. Its `Form` is `Tel`, its
        `Operand` `Tel.Flag` and its `Result` `Tel.Handle`. TEL text is UTF-8 by
        specification, so the write-back encodes without an ambient encoder.
    entry #open   parses the source, runs the block with a granted handle, and writes back if mutated or forced
  entry .ViewOpenable   the read-only `Openable` behind `Tel.telViewOpenable`: a `Write` mode is refused at open time
    entry #open   parses the source and runs the block; aborts `Mutation.Error(WriteUnsupported)` for a `Write` mode

  entry .Mutation   the primitive presentation-preserving edit operations of §22.2, and their interpreter
    detail
        Each `.Op` is a local rewrite addressed by a `Tel.Pointer`: surrounding atoms, comments,
        blank lines and unrelated children are untouched by construction, which is what lets a
        program change one value in a file a person wrote and write it back intact.
        `Mutation(tel, op)` applies one operation and `Mutation(tel, ops)` a list in order,
        returning a new `Tel`; `Revision` is the composable DSL over the same operations.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val document: Tel = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
        val pointer: Tel.Pointer = Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")
        Tel.Mutation(document, Tel.Mutation.Op.UpdateAtom(pointer, 0, t"Hal")).show   // t"name Hal\nage 30\n"
        ```

        A pointer step is a keyword plus an occurrence index, resolved against the document an
        operation is applied to, so a pointer stays meaningful across unrelated edits; but an
        occurrence-indexed pointer must not be reused across operations which move, add or
        remove compounds of the same keyword. Schema-dependent preconditions — that a deleted
        member was not required, that a set flag is declared, that a replacement is well-typed
        — are out of scope, as in the reference implementation: a schema-aware layer checks
        them before delegating to these primitives.
    refer stratiform.Tel.Revision
    refer stratiform.Tel.Pointer
    entry .Placement   which side of the other group `ReorderGroups` places a group: `Before` or `After`
    entry .Member   a schema-free description of one struct member, for `.construct`
        `Value(keyword, occurrences)` is a scalar member with its present occurrences (one for a
        single member, several for a repeatable one, none for an absent member which ends the
        inline run); `Flag(keyword)` a flag member; `Child(compound)` a ready-made child; and
        `Break` an absent member which contributes nothing but ends the inline run, as an absent
        scalar member does because the atom phase never skips a scalar position.
    entry .Op   one primitive operation, addressed by a `Tel.Pointer`
        The target-addressed operations, whose pointer names the compound they change:
        `UpdateAtom(pointer, atomIndex, text)` rewrites one atom, keeping its form while the
        new value is safe for it and otherwise escalating inline → source → literal, never
        back; `Delete(pointer)` removes the compound, and an emptied block with its comments;
        `Replace(pointer, compound)` substitutes a compound, keeping the original's remark;
        `AttachRemark(pointer, text)` and `RemoveRemark(pointer)` set and clear the remark;
        `SetFlag(pointer, keyword)` adds a flag inline when the compound has no children and
        no source or literal atom, else as a child, and fails with `FlagAlreadySet` if present;
        `UnsetFlag(pointer, keyword)` removes an inline or bare-child flag, as the identity when
        absent; `InsertBefore(pointer, compound)` and `InsertAfter(pointer, compound)` place a
        sibling next to the target, in a new block when the target's block is tabulated so the
        new compound is not read as a row.

        The parent-addressed operations, whose pointer names the container (`Tel.Pointer.Empty`
        for the root): `Insert(pointer, compound)` appends at the member's natural position —
        after the last compound of the same keyword, or in a fresh trailing block;
        `InsertIntoBlock(parentPointer, blockIndex, compound)` appends a row to a block,
        re-padded to its tabulation, failing with `TabulationOverflow` when a column value does
        not fit; `ReorderWithinGroup(parentPointer, keyword, oldIndex, newIndex)` moves one
        occurrence within its keyword's group; `ReorderGroups(parentPointer, keyword,
        otherKeyword, placement)` moves a whole member group before or after another, whole
        blocks moving where the groups' blocks are disjoint; and `ResizeTabulation(parentPointer,
        blockIndex, plannedRows)` recomputes a tabulation's marker offsets to fit its rows and
        any planned ones, re-padding every row. Column geometry is measured in code points.
    entry .apply   applies one operation, or a list in order, to a document
        `Mutation(tel, op)` and `Mutation(tel, ops)`, each raising `Mutation.Error`. The
        document's own sigil (from its pragma, `#` by default) feeds the inline-safety test, so
        an updated value which would re-parse as a remark under a custom sigil escalates its
        form.
    entry .construct   a fresh compound with each atom's form chosen by the §22.3 safety rules
        `construct(keyword, atoms*)` makes one atom per non-empty value — inline if the value
        has no line break, no leading, trailing or doubled space and does not begin with the
        sigil and a space; else a source atom if it has no empty line, no line ending in
        whitespace and no leading space; else a literal atom under the shortest `---` delimiter
        which collides with no line. An empty value yields no atom, since a leading space would
        be a trailing space on the keyword line.

        `construct(keyword, members)` and `construct(keyword, members, sigil)` run the canonical
        presentation algorithm over a `Member` list: leading single-occurrence inline-safe
        scalars and flags become inline atoms; a repeatable scalar goes all inline when every
        occurrence is inline-safe and otherwise every occurrence becomes a child; and every
        remaining member is a child in one block with no comments, tabulation or blank lines.
        Inline atoms are soft-space-preceded up to the first value containing a space, and
        hard-space-preceded from there on, so the construction re-parses to the same atoms.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Tel.Mutation.construct(t"name", t"Nick Bottom").atoms.length            // 1, an inline atom
        Tel.Mutation.construct(t"note", t"first line\nsecond line")            // a source atom
        Tel.Mutation.construct(t"note", t"first\n\nsecond\n")                  // a literal atom
        Tel.Mutation.construct(t"flag", t"").atoms.length                      // 0
        ```
    entry .Error   raised when a pointer does not resolve or an operation's invariant fails
      detail
          Error 606: _the mutation failed because …_. `PointerNotFound` is a pointer, block
          index or group which does not resolve to a compound, including a reorder whose groups
          are interleaved or whose indices are out of range, and a `ResizeTabulation` on a block
          with no tabulation; `AtomIndexOutOfRange` an `UpdateAtom` beyond the target's atoms;
          `FlagAlreadySet` a `SetFlag` of a flag the target already has; `TabulationOverflow` an
          `InsertIntoBlock` whose column value exceeds its column's capacity; and
          `WriteUnsupported`, raised by `open[Tel]` with `Write` when the document's source
          cannot be written back.
      entry .Reason   the five reasons, each with a number
        entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #reason   why the mutation failed
  entry .Revision   a composable log of edits, applied to a document one operation at a time
    detail
        A `Revision` is an ordered sequence of `Mutation.Op`s. Revisions compose with `#++`,
        and apply to a `Tel` with `revision(tel)` or the `Tel#edited` extension; each operation
        in the sequence addresses the document the preceding operations produced, since
        pointers resolve at apply time. The edit DSL starts at a `Tel.Pointer` with `.at`, whose
        `.Cursor` has one method per operation, and builds the compounds an insertion or
        replacement needs with `.compound` or `.construct`. Every operation preserves the
        presentation it does not name — comments, blank lines, layout and unrelated children —
        so a configuration file edited by a program comes back as its author wrote it, with one
        value changed.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label The document as read
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document: Tel = t"# contacts\nname Percy\n".read[Tel]
              document.show   // t"# contacts\nname Percy\n"
        frame
          label Update one atom, leaving the comment in place
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document: Tel = t"# contacts\nname Percy\n".read[Tel]
              val renamed: Tel.Revision = Tel.Revision.at(Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")).update(t"Hal")
              document.edited(renamed).show   // t"# contacts\nname Hal\n"
        frame
          label Then insert a member at the root
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document: Tel = t"# contacts\nname Percy\n".read[Tel]
              val renamed: Tel.Revision = Tel.Revision.at(Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")).update(t"Hal")
              val emailed: Tel.Revision = Tel.Revision.at(Tel.Pointer.Empty).insert(Tel.Revision.compound(t"email", t"hal@example.com"))
              document.edited(renamed ++ emailed).show   // t"# contacts\nname Hal\nemail hal@example.com\n"
        frame
          label And attach a remark to the renamed line
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val document: Tel = t"# contacts\nname Percy\n".read[Tel]
              val renamed: Tel.Revision = Tel.Revision.at(Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")).update(t"Hal")
              val emailed: Tel.Revision = Tel.Revision.at(Tel.Pointer.Empty).insert(Tel.Revision.compound(t"email", t"hal@example.com"))
              val noted: Tel.Revision = Tel.Revision.at(Tel.Pointer.of(t"name")).attachRemark(t"renamed")
              document.edited(renamed ++ emailed ++ noted).show   // t"# contacts\nname Hal  # renamed\nemail hal@example.com\n"
        ##
        ```

        Applying a revision raises `Mutation.Error` when a pointer does not resolve or an
        operation's precondition fails; the conventions for what each operation's pointer
        addresses — the target compound, or its parent — are those of `Mutation.Op`.
    refer stratiform.Tel.Mutation
    refer stratiform.Tel.Pointer
    entry .noop   the empty revision: applying it returns the document unchanged
    entry .at   begins an edit at a pointer: `Revision.at(pointer)` is a `Cursor`
    entry .compound   a fresh compound from a keyword and inline atom texts
        `Revision.compound(t"email", t"hal@example.com")` builds the payload of an insertion or
        replacement with every atom inline, each single-space-preceded, and no remark or
        children. For atoms chosen by the safety rules of §22.3 — a multi-line value becoming a
        source or literal atom — use `.construct`.
    entry .construct   a fresh compound with each atom's form chosen by the §22.3 rules
        `construct(keyword, atoms*)` and `construct(keyword, members)` are `Mutation.construct`:
        the first picks inline, source or literal form per value, dropping empty values; the
        second runs the canonical-presentation algorithm over a `Mutation.Member` list.
    entry .Cursor   a pointer bound to the next operation: one method per `Mutation.Op`
      detail
          Each method returns a single-operation `Revision`, to be chained with `++`. The
          pointer addresses the *target* compound for `#update`, `#delete`, `#replace`,
          `#insertBefore`, `#insertAfter`, `#attachRemark`, `#removeRemark`, `#setFlag` and
          `#unsetFlag`, and the *parent* for `#insert`, `#insertIntoBlock`, `#reorderWithinGroup`,
          `#reorderGroups` and `#resizeTabulation`; `Tel.Pointer.Empty` is the document root.
      entry #pointer   the pointer the operation is anchored at
      entry #update   replaces the text of one of the target's atoms; the first, by default
          `update(text)` rewrites atom 0 and `update(atomIndex, text)` any atom, counting every
          presentation form. The atom's form is kept while the new value is still safe for it,
          and otherwise escalates inline → source → literal, never back; preceding spaces are
          kept, so an identity update leaves tabulation padding intact.
      entry #delete   removes the target compound; an emptied block goes with its comments
      entry #replace   replaces the target with a compound, keeping the target's remark
      entry #insert   appends a compound at its natural position among the parent's children
          Immediately after the last compound with the same keyword, in the same block when it
          is untabulated and in a new block after it when it is; or in a fresh trailing block
          when no member of that keyword exists yet.
      entry #insertBefore   inserts a compound immediately before the target
          In the target's block, or in a new block before it when the block is tabulated.
      entry #insertAfter   inserts a compound immediately after the target
      entry #insertIntoBlock   appends a compound as a row of the parent's `blockIndex`-th child block
          The way to add a row to a tabulated block: each inline atom is one column value,
          re-padded to the block's marker offsets. A value too wide for its column is
          `TabulationOverflow`; `#resizeTabulation` first.
      entry #attachRemark   sets the target's remark, replacing any it had
      entry #removeRemark   removes the target's remark; an absent remark is the identity
      entry #setFlag   sets a flag on the target, inline when it can be, else as a child
          Inline — hard-space-preceded if the line is already in hard-space mode — when the
          target has no children and no source or literal atom; otherwise as a bare-keyword
          child. A flag already present is `FlagAlreadySet`.
      entry #unsetFlag   removes an inline or child flag from the target; an absent flag is the identity
      entry #reorderWithinGroup   moves the `oldIndex`-th occurrence of a keyword to `newIndex` among the parent's children
          Zero-based within the keyword's group; block boundaries and surrounding comments are
          preserved.
      entry #reorderGroups   moves one keyword's member group before (the default) or after another's
          Whole blocks move where the groups occupy disjoint blocks, so attached comments,
          tabulations and blank lines travel with their group; both groups must be contiguous
          before and after. `Mutation.Placement` chooses the side.
      entry #resizeTabulation   recomputes the marker offsets of the parent's `blockIndex`-th tabulated block
          By the minimal-offsets algorithm, accommodating any `plannedRows` about to be
          inserted, and re-pads every existing row.
    entry #ops   the operations, in order
    entry #++   the revision followed by another
    entry #apply   applies the operations to a document in order; `revision(tel)`
        Raises `Mutation.Error`. `Tel#edited(revision)` is the same call as an extension.

entry .optionalityOptions   how an `Optional` field treats absence and faults, each a given to import by name
  detail
      An `Optional` field reads as `Unset` when its keyword is missing or its compound has
      neither an atom nor a child — TEL's two spellings of absence — and a value the inner
      decoder rejects is an error. These givens vary each policy for a scope, and are
      imported by name since `import soundness.*` does not import givens. Absence is lenient
      by default and faults are strict. The direct parser polices absence only, since a fault
      surfaces mid-entry.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Flags(active: Boolean, verbose: Optional[Boolean])

      locally:
        import optionalityOptions.lenientTelFaults
        t"active false\nverbose maybe\n".read[Tel].as[Flags]   // Flags(false, Unset)
      ```
  entry .strictTelAbsence   a missing or empty `Optional` field is `Tel.Error(Absent)`, and required in the derived schema
  entry .lenientTelAbsence   a missing or empty `Optional` field is `Unset` (the default)
  entry .strictTelFaults   a rejected value in an `Optional` field is an error (the default)
  entry .lenientTelFaults   a rejected value in an `Optional` field reads as `Unset`

entry .tel   `value.tel`, a value's `Tel`; and the `tel"…"` interpolator and extractor
    Two extensions share the name. On any value with an `Encodable in Tel`, `value.tel` is
    `value.encode`: the value as a `Tel`, mirroring jacinta's `.json`. On `StringContext`,
    `tel"…"` is the interpolator, a document parsed as the code compiles whose `$`
    substitutions are encoded through their own types, and the same syntax is an extractor
    binding the atoms a pattern captures (`Tel.interpolator`, `Tel.extrapolator`).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    Person(t"Percy", 30).tel.show   // t"name Percy\nage 30\n"

    val name = t"Percy"
    tel"name $name" match
      case tel"name $who" => who.primaryAtom
      case _              => t"anonymous"
    ```

entry .telp   the `telp"…"` literal: a `Telp` path checked as the code compiles
    `telp"/office/city"` is a TEL path whose first character chooses the delimiter and whose
    further components name keywords or select an occurrence by key value or index. A
    literal which is not syntactically a TELP is a compile error — `telp"office/city"`, with
    no leading delimiter, does not compile — and substitutions are rejected, since a path is
    a constant. A path is the same whichever delimiter writes it: `telp"/office/city" ==
    telp".office.city"`.

entry .bintel   a document, element or value as BinTEL body bytes, guided by a schema
    Three extensions. `tel.bintel(schema)` type-assigns the document against the schema
    (`Tel.Type.assign`) and encodes the result; `element.bintel(schema)` encodes an already
    typed `Tel.Element`; and `value.bintel`, for a value with a `Tel.Encodable` and a
    `TelSchematic`, encodes it under the schema derived from its type, with an internal name
    a BinTEL body never embeds, so a decoder deriving the schema from the same type agrees
    on the layout. Each has an overload taking `Tel.Codec.Bindings`, which writes a scalar
    with a declared encoding as its codec's bytes. The body is TEL §7.1's document root
    alone — no magic number, no signature — which is what `valueHash` hashes;
    `bintelDocument` and `Bintel.frame` make it a self-framing document. Raises `Tel.Error`
    for a document which does not conform and `Bintel.Error` for an encoding fault.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    val schema: Tels = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")
    val document: Tel = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
    val body: Data = document.bintel(schema)
    Person(t"Percy", 30).bintel == body   // true
    ```

entry .ratel   a document, element or value as a RaTEL image, under a schema which the image carries; or the reader an `open[Ratel]` block has open
    Three extensions, as `bintel` has. `tel.ratel(schema)` composes the schema, type-assigns
    the document against it and writes the image; `element.ratel(schema)` writes an already
    typed `Tel.Element`; and `value.ratel` writes a value under the schema derived from its
    type. The first two have an overload taking `Tel.Codec.Bindings`; without it the codecs
    are `Tel.Codec.Bindings.all`. Raises `Tel.Error` for a document which does not conform and
    `Ratel.Error` for a schema or a value which cannot be laid out. With no receiver, inside an
    `open[Ratel]` block, `ratel` is the `Ratel.Reader` which the block was given.

entry .valueHash   the BLAKE3 digest of a document's, element's or acceptance's BinTEL body: a hash of content, not bytes
    `tel.valueHash(schema)`, `element.valueHash(schema)` (each with a `Tel.Codec.Bindings`
    overload) and `acceptance.valueHash` digest the §7.1 body alone, so the hash is a
    function of the semantic model and the schema and independent of presentation: two
    documents differing only in comments, whitespace or member order hash identically,
    while documents whose values differ do not. It serves as a cache key or a change
    detector where hashing the file would report a change whenever someone reflowed it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
    val schema: Tels = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")

    t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel].valueHash(schema)
      == t"# a person\nage 30\n\nname Percy\n".read[Tel].valueHash(schema)   // true
    ```

entry .bintelDocument   a document as a complete, self-framing BinTEL byte sequence under a schema signature
    `tel.bintelDocument(schema, signature)` is magic number, document length, signature
    length, signature and body (TEL §6): what `Bintel.frame` builds from `tel.bintel(schema)`.
    The signature must be a valid length, else `Bintel.Error(BadSignatureLength)`.

entry .fulfil   a writer's answer to an acceptance from a value: the first alternative its derived schema can serve
    `value.fulfil(acceptance)` holds the value under its type's derived lineage — its
    required fields as the base, its optional fields as atoms and its `@assign` groups as
    layers, less any layer which `@retract`s a case the value uses, so the document stays
    valid under the composition it names — and answers the first alternative that
    composition can serve with the richest composition the alternative permits, as a
    `Tel.Acceptance.Served` holding the framed document. `Unset` when no alternative can be
    served. An optional `Tel.Codec.Bindings` writes encoded scalars; the default binds the
    built-ins. Needs tactics for `Tel.Error`, `Bintel.Error`, `Tels.Resolution.Error` and
    `Tels.Renderer.Error`. Named `fulfil` because `serve` is urticose's.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    val accepting = Tel.Acceptance[(Contact, ContactV1)]()

    Contact(t"Nym", t"nym@example.com", Unset).fulfil(accepting.acceptance).let: served =>
      send(served.document)
    ```

entry .documents   the documents of a byte stream, one at a time, each parsed only when it is reached
    An extension on a consumed `Stream[Data]`: the streaming counterpart of
    `read[List[Tel]]`, which parses every document of a `##`-separated source first. The
    result is a single-consumer iterator; it needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]` and a `Buffering`.

entry .Stratiform   the macros behind a verified `Tel`'s compile-checked field and index access
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Tel#selectDynamic` and `Tel#applyDynamic` are `transparent inline`
      and delegate here. When the receiver is a `Tel of P from R` and the field name is a
      literal, the macro looks the field up among `P`'s case fields and types the result as
      `Tel of <field type> from R`; otherwise it falls back to the plain, `Dynamical`-gated
      runtime access.
  entry .select   the `tel.field` macro: schema-typed lookup, or gated dynamic access
  entry .applied   the `tel.field(index)` macro: an element of a collection field, or gated dynamic access

entry .Tel2   the trait holding `Tel`'s derived codecs, primitive and collection instances, and the interpolator
  hidden
  detail
      `object Tel extends Tel2`, so every member declared here is reached through the
      companion, as `Tel.interpolator` or `Tel.scalar`, and is in the implicit scope of `Tel`'s
      nested typeclasses: `interpolator`, `extrapolator`, the blanket `decodable` and
      `encodable`, the `field…` instances for collections and `Optional`, `encodableOver`,
      `aggregableIn`, the derivation objects, the primitive, `Optional`, collection and `Map`
      codecs, the read-only `telViewOpenable`, and the constructors `scalar`, `compound` and
      `empty`. Not for direct use.
  entry #telViewOpenable   the read-only `Openable` for any source parseable as `Tel`
      Any source which is `Readable to Tel` may be opened; a `Write` mode is refused at open
      time with `Tel.Mutation.Error(WriteUnsupported)`, so a `Tactic[Tel.Mutation.Error]` is needed.
      `t"name Percy\n".open[Tel]() { handle ?=> handle.current }` reads a text this way.
  entry #interpolator   the `tel"…"` interpolator: a document parsed as the code compiles, with typed substitutions
      Each `$value` is encoded through its own `Tel.Encodable`, so `tel"name $name"` with a
      `Text` is the compound `name Percy`. A malformed literal is a compile error positioned
      at the offending range of the Scala source. The `tel` extension on `StringContext`,
      in the `stratiform` package, is the entry point; this given does the work.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val name = t"Percy"
      val contact: Tel = tel"name $name"
      contact.field(t"name").let(_.primaryAtom)   // t"Percy"
      ```
  entry #extrapolator   the `tel"…"` extractor: a pattern parsed as the code compiles, binding atom captures
      `case tel"name $name" =>` binds `name` to a `Tel` holding the captured atom, so
      `name.primaryAtom` is its text; several captures may split one atom on a literal
      separator, `case tel"version $major.$minor.$patch" =>`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      tel"contact Percy percy@example.com" match
        case tel"contact $name $email" => (name.primaryAtom, email.primaryAtom)
        case _                         => (t"", t"")
      ```
  entry #decodable   the blanket `Tel.Decodable`: a text codec's scalar, or a derived product or sum
      In implicit scope for every type. A type with a `Decodable in Text` decodes its primary
      atom through it, as a `Scalar`; otherwise a product derives field by field, and a sum
      as a single child compound keyed by the variant's name in kebab-case (an all-singleton
      enum decodes through its text codec). Field keywords are the field names in kebab-case
      unless renamed with `@name[Tel](…)` or a bare `@name(…)`. Recursive types resolve: a
      `Tree(value: Text, children: List[Tree])` derives.
  entry #encodable   the blanket `Tel.Encodable`: a text codec's scalar, or a derived product or sum
      The mirror of `#decodable`. A product encodes as one child compound per field; a sum
      as a compound keyed by the variant's name holding the variant's fields. Within `Tel`'s
      implicit scope this given's `encode` extension can shadow another library's
      `Encodable in Text` for a type which has both, which is why `Tel.Acceptance#encode` is
      a member.
  entry #fieldCollection   the field parser for a collection: elements through the `Tel.Field` chain, gathered
      Resolves before the `Tel.field` fallback, so a `List` is never mis-derived as a sum from
      its own `Mirror`. Every same-keyword occurrence accumulates, as the tree decoder
      gathers them.
  entry #fieldList   the field parser for a `List`
  entry #fieldSet   the field parser for a `Set`
  entry #fieldSeries   the field parser for a `Sequence`
  entry #fieldOptional   the field parser for an `Optional`: `Unset` for a missing keyword or an empty entry
      Honours `optionalityOptions`. The inner parser comes from the field chain by name, so
      a type recursive through `Optional` resolves.
  entry #encodableOver   `value.in[Text over Tel]`: the value encoded to a tree and rendered as text
      The write-side mirror of `read[T in Tel]`: the text `show` would render for
      `value.in[Tel]`.
  entry #encodableOverData   `value.in[Data over Tel]`: the same text as bytes, in the `Codepage` in scope
      Needs a `Codepage`, such as `codepages.utf8Codepage`.
  entry #aggregableIn   `source.read[T in Tel]` for a type with no `Tel.Parsable`: parse the tree, then decode
      Shorthand for `source.read[Tel].as[T]`, with the same position tracking and foci. Lower
      priority than `.aggregableParsed`, which wins when the type parses directly.
  entry #ParsableDerivation   the Wisteria derivation of direct field parsers, behind `Tel.Parsable.derived`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use. A product's parser collects each field's wire keyword, parser and
        default and hands them to `Tel.Parsable.product`; a sum always takes the tree bridge
        over its `Tel.Decodable`, since a variant's keyword cannot be known before the entry
        is read.
    entry .conjunction   the product parser
    entry .disjunction   the sum parser, through the tree bridge
  entry #DecodableDerivation   the Wisteria derivation of `Tel.Decodable`, behind the blanket `#decodable`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use. The product decoder fills fields positionally from the entry
        line's atoms, then by keyword, keeps the first of a repeated non-repeatable field,
        falls back to a declared default, and accrues every missing field under a
        `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary; the sum decoder dispatches on the single child's
        keyword.
    entry .conjunction   the product decoder
    entry .disjunction   the sum decoder
  entry #EncodableDerivation   the Wisteria derivation of `Tel.Encodable`, behind the blanket `#encodable`
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use. The product encoder writes one compound per field under its wire
        keyword, flattening a collection field's document into repeated compounds and
        omitting an empty `Optional`; the sum encoder writes the variant's compound under
        its kebab-case name.
    entry .conjunction   the product encoder
    entry .disjunction   the sum encoder
  entry #textDecodable   `Text` from a compound's primary atom; a bare keyword with no atom is the empty text
      A present compound with a keyword and no atom is the empty string, TEL's spelling of
      it; the keywordless empty node still raises `Absent`.
  entry #stringDecodable   `String` from a compound's primary atom
  entry #intDecodable   `Int` from a compound's primary atom; `NotScalar` for text which is not one
  entry #longDecodable   `Long` from a compound's primary atom
  entry #doubleDecodable   `Double` from a compound's primary atom
  entry #booleanDecodable   `Boolean` from an explicit `true` or `false` atom, never from a bare flag
      A Scala `Boolean` is a value, not a TEL flag: `active true` decodes, `active` alone
      raises `Absent`, and anything else `NotScalar`.
  entry #telDecodable   `Tel` decodes as itself
  entry #telsDecodable   a schema from its TEL document: `tel.as[Tels]`
      The §20.5 schema-of-schemas surface, through `Tels.Reconstructor`. It lives here, beside
      the blanket `#decodable`, so that a file which imports `Tel.given` still finds it rather
      than deriving `Tels` as a record.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val schema: Tels = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel].as[Tels]
      ```
  entry #textEncodable   `Text` as a one-atom scalar
  entry #stringEncodable   `String` as a one-atom scalar
  entry #intEncodable   `Int` as a one-atom scalar
  entry #longEncodable   `Long` as a one-atom scalar
  entry #doubleEncodable   `Double` as a one-atom scalar
  entry #booleanEncodable   `Boolean` as an explicit `true` or `false` atom
  entry #telEncodable   `Tel` encodes as itself
  entry #optionalEncodable   an `Optional` as its value, or as nothing at all when `Unset`
      An `Unset` field contributes no compound to its record, so the text and binary forms
      both read it back as `Unset`.
  entry #optionalDecodable   an `Optional` as `Unset` for a missing keyword or an empty entry, else the inner value
      Captures the `optionalityOptions` in scope: under `strictTelAbsence` absence raises
      `Tel.Error(Absent)` and the field is required in the derived schema; under
      `lenientTelFaults` a value the inner decoder rejects reads as `Unset` instead of
      raising. TEL has no null, so there is no nullity option.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Flags(active: Boolean, verbose: Optional[Boolean])

      t"active false\n".read[Tel].as[Flags]                  // Flags(false, Unset)
      safely(t"active false\nverbose maybe\n".read[Tel].as[Flags])   // Unset: `maybe` is a fault

      locally:
        import optionalityOptions.lenientTelFaults
        t"active false\nverbose maybe\n".read[Tel].as[Flags]   // Flags(false, Unset)
      ```
  entry #listEncodable   a `List` as repeated compounds, one per element, re-keyed to the field's keyword
      A collection encodes to a document of its elements' compounds, which the product
      encoder flattens into siblings sharing the field's keyword: `items 1`, `items 2`,
      `items 3`.
  entry #setEncodable   a `Set` as repeated compounds
  entry #seriesEncodable   a `Sequence` as repeated compounds
  entry #collectionDecodable   any `Iterable` with a `Factory`, from the gathered same-keyword compounds
      Repeatable: the product decoder gathers every sibling sharing the field's keyword into
      a document and hands it here, where each child decodes as an element. A lone compound
      is a one-element collection, and no compounds an empty one.
  entry #listDecodable   a `List` from the gathered same-keyword compounds
  entry #setDecodable   a `Set` from the gathered same-keyword compounds
  entry #seriesDecodable   a `Sequence` from the gathered same-keyword compounds
  entry #mapEncodable   a `Map` as `entries` compounds, each with a `key` and a `value` child
  entry #mapDecodable   a `Map` from `entries` compounds, each child decoded through the key and value codecs
      A missing `key` or `value` child reads through the codec's `absent()`, so a
      flag-natured value reports its absent form rather than misreading emptiness.
  entry #scalar   a `Tel` holding one atom: the form a scalar encodes to
      `Tel.scalar(t"Hal")` is a keywordless compound with one atom, in whichever form reads
      back as written (inline, source or literal). An empty text stays an empty inline atom,
      which serializes as no atom but is distinguishable, at the value level, from absence.
      What `modify` and the `telLens` setter take.
  entry #compound   a `Tel` holding a compound built from a keyword, atoms and child compounds
      `Tel.compound(t"address", Array.empty, Array(street, city))` puts the children in one
      block; no children means no blocks.
  entry #empty   the empty `Tel`: a keywordless compound with no atoms and no children
      What `field` navigation yields for a missing field and what a decoder's `absent()`
      decodes by default; a primitive raises `Absent` from it, an `Optional` reads `Unset`.

entry .Tel3   the trait holding the universal `Tel.Field` fallback for direct parsing
  hidden
  detail
      The lowest-priority layer of `Tel`'s companion chain, so that `Tel2`'s element-wise
      field instances match collections and `Optional` first. Its one member, `field`, is
      reached as `Tel.field`. Not for direct use.
  entry #field   the universal `Tel.Field` fallback: a `Parsable`, a text codec, derivation, or the tree bridge
      The lowest-priority layer of direct parsing, resolved per field of a derived parser in
      that order, so a field's wire format is identical on the direct and tree paths. A case
      class with both a `Reflection` and a hand-written `Tel.Decodable` derives structurally
      here and so diverges from its decoder; give it `Tel.Parsable.fromDecodable(…)` to make
      the decoder win.

entry .Tel4   the trait holding `Tel.Provider`, the type provider over a TEL schema
  detail
      Its own layer in `Tel`'s companion chain, contributing no givens for `Tel` itself.
      `Provider` is reached as `Tel.Provider`. Not for direct use.
  entry #Provider   a type provider over a TEL schema: typed records whose fields the schema declares
    detail
        `object ContactRecords extends Tel.Provider(schema)`, declared in a file of its own,
        reads its `Tels` as the code compiles and gives `ContactRecords.record(tel)` a
        structural type with one accessor per field of the schema's document struct, so the
        shape of the data is taken from the schema and checked by the compiler with no Scala
        type restating it. A field the schema does not declare is a compile error. Each field
        reads at the type its validator names: `string`, `identifier` and `type-name` as
        `Text`, `sigil` as `Char` (failing on more than one character), a flag as `Boolean`
        (`true` where present), and a custom validator through a given of the same label in
        scope where `record` is called, such as `given ("handle" is Intensional in
        Tel.Provider from Tel to Handle) = Intensional(…)`. An optional field, or one a layer
        introduced, reads as `Optional`; a repeatable one reads every occurrence as a `List`;
        a struct or a reference to a record definition reads as a nested record; a select
        reads as one optional field per variant. A required field which is absent fails as it
        is read, with `Tel.Provider.Error`, through the `Tactic` of the code reading it.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        object ContactRecords extends Tel.Provider(Tels.tels[Person](t"person"))

        val record = ContactRecords.record(t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel])
        record.name   // t"Percy"
        record.age    // 30
        ```

        A provider also speaks acceptances: `#acceptance` is what a reader of these records
        sends, and `#receive` presents a reply for `record` with every layer the writer
        included.
    entry .string   the `string` validator reads as `Text`: the primary atom
    entry .identifier   the `identifier` validator reads as `Text`
    entry .typeName   the `type-name` validator reads as `Text`
    entry .sigil   the `sigil` validator reads as `Char`, failing with `Tel.Provider.Error(NotSigil)` on a longer atom
    entry .flag   a flag reads as `Boolean`: `true` where the keyword is present, `false` where not
    entry .tel   the `tel` validator reads the field as a `Tel`
    entry .Error   raised as a record's field is read from a document which does not conform
      detail
          Error 614, sub-numbered by reason: _the TEL did not conform to its schema because
          …_. `Absent(name)` (1), _the field `name` is required_, for a required field with no
          compound; `NotSigil(value)` (2), _the value `value` is not a single character_, for
          a `sigil` field.
      entry .Reason   the two reasons, `Absent(name)` and `NotSigil(value)`
      entry #reason   why the document did not conform
    entry .fieldsOf   the polyvinyl members of a `Tels.Struct`: one per field, with nested records and select variants
        Takes the struct, the schema it resolves in, and optionally the schema's base before
        any layer and the struct as the base declares it: a member the base lacks was
        introduced by a layer and is optional whatever the layer declares.
    entry #tels   the schema this provider reads by
    entry #fields   the record's members, from the composed schema; a schema which does not compose aborts `Specification.Error`
    entry #layersOf   the names of the layers whose root members a document carries
        The layers a value built from the document was composed with, for a writer serving
        an acceptance.
    entry #acceptance   the acceptance a reader of these records sends: the base required, every layer offered, self-contained as fallback
    entry #receive   a document received in reply to the acceptance, presented for `record` with every layer included; `Unset` if unreadable
    entry #access   the compound of a named field, or `Tel.empty` when absent
    entry #absent   whether a field's `Tel` is the absent sentinel (an empty keyword)
    entry #required   a required field's compound, or `Tel.Provider.Error(Absent)`; a flag is never absent
    entry #repeated   every compound of a repeatable field, as a `List`
    entry #Origin   what records are read from: `Tel`
    entry #Form   the specification's form: `Tel.Provider`
    entry #Failure   the error a field read raises: `Tel.Provider.Error`

entry .Tels   a TEL schema: a root struct, named record, scalar and select definitions, and layers
  detail
      A `Tels` is the schema data model of §20 of the TEL specification, as a value: the
      schema's `#name`, the `#document` struct a document root must match, its `#records`,
      `#scalars` and `#selects` — one namespace of definitions, each named by a PascalCase
      `TypeName` — the `#layers` which refine it, and an optional `#sigil` for remarks. Every
      part is an immutable case class under the companion (`Tels.Field`, `Tels.Struct`,
      `Tels.RecordDefinition`, `Tels.Layer`, …), so a schema can be built in Scala, compared
      structurally, rendered back to a schema document, hashed and signed.

      A schema comes from one of three places: it is **derived** from a Scala type with
      `Tels.tels[T](name)`; it is **read** from a TEL document — the schema language is itself
      TEL — with `tel.as[Tels]`, and then checked with `Tels.Validation.validate`; or it is
      **reconstructed** from the semantic element a BinTEL frame embeds, with
      `element.as[Tels]` (`.elementDecodable`). Once in hand, a schema types a document with
      `Tel.Type.assign`, which validates it and reports every fault as a `Tel.Error` with a
      `Telp` path to the offending element.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val derived: Tels = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")

      val source: Text =
        t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier key\n  field email String optional repeatable\n"

      val written: Tels = Tels.Validation.validate(source.read[Tel].as[Tels])
      val element: Tel.Element = Tel.Type.assign(t"name percy\nemail percy@example.com\n".read[Tel], written)
      ```

      The companion holds the schema machinery as nested objects, each a page of its own:
      `.Layers` composes a base with its layers (§20.3); `.Validation` checks the constraints
      of §20.1 before and after composition; `.Reconstructor` and `.SemanticReconstructor`
      read a schema back from its document or its typed element, and `.Renderer` writes one
      out; `.Atoms` decomposes a schema into the smallest components an acceptance can name;
      `.Subtyping` decides whether a document written under one composed schema can be read
      under another, and `.Projection` restricts an element to what the supertype can
      address; `.Resolution` is the schema-resolution protocol of BinTEL §8.2; `.Decoder`
      validates a document before decoding it; and `.Axiom` holds the two schemas every
      implementation has built in, the schema of schemas and the acceptance schema.

      The `Tels2` mixin contributes the `TelSchematic` givens by which a type derives its
      schema — `text`, `int`, `optional`, `list`, `map`, `schematic`, … — and the
      `tels` method which assembles them into a schema. `Tels` inherits them, so they are in
      the companion's implicit scope and a derived schema needs no import beyond
      `soundness.*`.

      Field polarity is a tristate, `Tels.Polarity`: a member whose `required` is not `Loose`
      is required, and one whose `repeatable` is `Loose` repeats. A definition's name must be
      unique across all three kinds (E210), a struct's keywords unique across its fields and
      the variants of its selects (E201), and `tel` is reserved as a keyword (E208).
  refer stratiform.Tel
  refer stratiform.Telp
  refer stratiform.TelSchematic
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  refer stratiform.SchemaSignature
  entry #name   the schema's kebab-case name, as the `name` line of its document
  entry #document   the struct a document root must match
      The root `Tels.Struct`: the members a document's top-level compounds fill, with any
      struct-level validators. A derived schema puts the type's required and optional fields
      here; a sum at the root is a struct with one `Tels.SelectRef` to its select.
  entry #layers   the layers which refine the base, in declaration order
      Each `Tels.Layer` adds or refines definitions and may overlay members onto the root. A
      composed schema (`Tels.Layers.compose`) has none: its layers have been folded in.
  entry #sigil   the remark sigil the schema declares, if any; `#` is the default
      A single character which is not whitespace, a letter, a digit, a bracket or `+`
      (E207). `Tels.Validation.validate` checks it.
  entry #records   the schema's `record` definitions: named structs
  entry #scalars   the schema's `scalar` definitions, including the four built-ins
      A schema read from a document or an element always lists `Identifier`, `TypeName`,
      `Sigil` and `String` first, as every schema document declares them implicitly; a schema
      built by hand or derived from a type need not, since `Tels.Reference` resolves the
      built-in names anyway (`Tels.Builtin`).
  entry #selects   the schema's `select` definitions: named sums of variants
  entry .elementDecodable   reads a schema back from the semantic element a BinTEL frame embeds
      The given which makes `element.as[Tels]` work, for a `Tel.Element` produced by decoding
      an embedded schema body (BinTEL §6.2) under the `tels` axiom. It is the inverse of
      `Tels.Renderer.element`, and the counterpart of `Tel.telsDecodable`, which reads a
      schema from a TEL document as `tel.as[Tels]`. It needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]`, raised
      (`RequiredMemberAbsent`, E307) when the element lacks its `name` or `document`. A value
      typed as the subtype `Tel.Element.Node` must be ascribed, `(node: Tel.Element).as[Tels]`.
  entry .Polarity   whether a member's flag was loosened, tightened or left to the default
      The tristate of §20 for each of a member's two axes. `Implicit` means no flag was
      written; `Loose` means a loosening flag — `optional` on the required axis, `repeatable`
      on the repeatable axis; `Tight` a tightening one — `required` or `irrepeatable`. The
      effective booleans are `required = (polarity != Loose)` and `repeatable = (polarity ==
      Loose)`, so an unflagged member is required and single. A layer may tighten a member
      but never loosen one (E214, E215). The tristate rather than two booleans is what lets a
      derived schema hash as the schema its author would have written: a `field` with no
      flags is `Implicit`, not `Tight`.
  entry .Member   one member of a struct: a field, a select reference, or a layer's exclude
      The sealed trait of `Tels.Field`, `Tels.SelectRef` and `Tels.Exclude`. A struct's
      members are ordered, and the order is the positional order atoms fill (§20.2) and the
      flat keyword order BinTEL indexes.
  entry .Field   a keyword with a type, at a member position
    detail
        A `field` line of a schema: its `#keyword`, `#fieldType`, the two polarities, an
        optional `#default` (only on a required scalar member, E203), an optional
        `#description`, and whether it is the struct's `#key`. `key` is declared last, after
        `description`, so positional construction written before keys existed still compiles;
        the keyword order of the schema language puts `key` before `default`, which is why a
        trailing `key` atom on a `field` line is read as the flag and never as a default.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val name: Tels.Field =
          Tels.Field(Tels.Polarity.Implicit, Tels.Polarity.Implicit, t"name", Tels.Reference(t"Identifier"), Unset, key = true)

        val email: Tels.Field =
          Tels.Field(Tels.Polarity.Loose, Tels.Polarity.Loose, t"email", Tels.Reference(t"String"), Unset)
        ```
    entry #required   `Loose` for `optional`, `Tight` for `required`, `Implicit` for neither
    entry #repeatable   `Loose` for `repeatable`, `Tight` for `irrepeatable`, `Implicit` for neither
    entry #keyword   the field's kebab-case keyword
    entry #fieldType   the field's type: a reference, a scalar, a flag or an inline struct
    entry #default   the value a required scalar field takes when elided, if any
    entry #description   the field's free-text description, if any; never validated
    entry #key   whether this field identifies occurrences of the enclosing struct
        At most one field of a struct is a key (E221); it must be scalar-typed (E219) and
        required and non-repeatable (E220). A key is what a `Telp` key selector such as
        `/contact/nym` matches, and two occurrences with the same key value are rejected at
        assignment (E314). A layer may mark a field as key; nothing can clear it.
    entry #indexes   the secondary indexes declared over a repeatable field, for RaTEL
        Each `index` child of a `field` line is one `Tels.Index`. The field must be repeatable,
        and of a struct or scalar type (E228). A layer's indexes are appended to the base's,
        save any already present. Indexes are a storage-layout annotation: they constrain no
        document, and take no part in subtyping.
  entry .Index   a secondary index over a repeatable field: an ordered tuple of paths
      Written `index /country /city` beneath a `field` line. Each of its `#paths` is a TELP
      (E229), which resolves within the field's type through non-repeatable fields to a scalar
      (E230); over a scalar-typed field the only path is the root, `/`.
    entry #paths   the paths, as they were written
    entry #same   whether another index has the same paths, each compared as a TELP
    entry .merge   a base's indexes followed by each of a layer's which is not already present
  entry .SelectRef   a reference to a named select, at a member position
    detail
        A `select` line inside a struct body: the member whose keyword space is every variant
        keyword of the `Tels.SelectDefinition` it `#reference`s. Its polarities govern whether
        some variant must be present and whether several may be. A select at a member
        position can only be reached this way: a `Tels.Field` whose reference names a select
        is a kind mismatch (E217).
    entry #required   `Loose` for `optional`, `Tight` for `required`, `Implicit` for neither
    entry #repeatable   `Loose` for `repeatable`, `Tight` for `irrepeatable`, `Implicit` for neither
    entry #reference   the name of the select definition referenced
  entry .Exclude   a layer's removal of a variant from a select
    detail
        The layer-only member (§20.3) which removes the variant with `#keyword` from the
        select a layer restates. It is valid only inside a layer's `select` body; one in a
        struct body is E216, and one naming no variant of the original select is E211.
        Composition consumes excludes, so a composed schema has none.
    entry #keyword   the variant keyword excluded
  entry .Variant   one alternative of a select: a keyword and its type
    detail
        A `variant` line of a select body. The `#keyword` is what a document writes; the
        `#variantType` is what follows it, typically a `Tels.Reference` to a record.
    entry #keyword   the variant's kebab-case keyword
    entry #variantType   the type of the variant's value
    entry #description   the variant's free-text description, if any
  entry .Type   a member's type: struct, scalar, flag or reference
      The sealed trait of the four kinds of §20: `Tels.Struct`, an ordered member list;
      `Tels.Scalar`, atom text under validators and patterns; `Tels.Flag`, a value-less
      keyword; and `Tels.Reference`, an indirection to a named definition. Only a reference,
      `Flag` or a built-in scalar can be written in a schema document, so a derived schema's
      inline struct or constrained inline scalar cannot be rendered (`Tels.Renderer.Error`).
  entry .Struct   an ordered list of members with struct-level validators
    detail
        The shape of a document root, a record definition's body and a layer's overlay. Its
        `#members` are filled in order by a compound's atoms and children (§20.2), and its
        `#validators` are applied to the whole instance.
    entry #members   the members, in declaration order
    entry #validators   validator names applied to the whole struct
  entry .Scalar   atom text constrained by validators, patterns and an encoding
    detail
        A scalar type inline at a member position. Its `#validators` are names resolved
        through a `Tel.Validator.Registry`; its `#patterns` are RE2 expressions (§21.8) which
        each match the whole value and intersect; and `#encoding`, when present, names the
        codec which gives the scalar its BinTEL representation (§21.7). `Tels.Scalar(Array.empty)`
        accepts anything, like the built-in `String`.
    entry #validators   the validator names applied to the value
    entry #encoding   the codec name of the scalar's binary form, or `Unset` for UTF-8 text
    entry #patterns   RE2 patterns the whole value must match, in declaration order
    entry #width   the byte count every value's encoded form is asserted to have, for RaTEL
    entry #intern   whether a RaTEL writer should store equal values once
  entry .Flag   the value-less type: a keyword whose presence is its value
      A member of type `Flag` is written as its bare keyword, inline among a compound's atoms
      or as a child with no content; one carrying content is E311. A derived `Boolean` field
      is not a flag but a scalar holding `true` or `false`.
  entry .Reference   an indirection to a definition by its `TypeName`
    detail
        The `#name` resolves, through the composed namespace and then the built-ins of
        `Tels.Builtin`, to a record or a scalar when used as a field type; a name which
        resolves to nothing is E209, and one naming a select from a field is E217.
    entry #name   the definition's `TypeName`
  entry .RecordDefinition   a named struct in the schema's namespace
    detail
        A `record` declaration: `#name`, `#members`, `#validators` and an optional
        `#description`. A field refers to it by `Tels.Reference(name)`; a schema never nests a
        record's struct inline.
    entry #name   the record's `TypeName`
    entry #members   the members, in declaration order
    entry #validators   validator names applied to every instance
    entry #description   free-text description, if any; carried through BinTEL, never validated
  entry .ScalarDefinition   a named scalar in the schema's namespace
    detail
        A `scalar` declaration. `#validators` and `#patterns` are each optional, and a
        declaration carrying neither accepts every value, like the built-in `String`, while
        remaining a distinct named type for generated code and later tightening. A layer may
        add validators and may replace the patterns only with a language contained in the old
        one (E223); it may add an `#encoding` where there was none but never change one
        (E218).
    entry #name   the scalar's `TypeName`
    entry #validators   the validator names applied to the value
    entry #description   free-text description, if any
    entry #encoding   the codec name of the scalar's binary form, or `Unset` for UTF-8 text
    entry #patterns   RE2 patterns the whole value must match, in declaration order
    entry #width   the byte count every value's encoded form is asserted to have, for RaTEL
    entry #intern   whether a RaTEL writer should store equal values once
        A `width` needs an `#encoding` (E227), must agree with a standard codec's fixed width
        and cannot be changed by a layer (E226), and excludes `intern` (E231).
    entry #scalar   the scalar type which a reference to this definition denotes
  entry .SelectDefinition   a named sum of variants in the schema's namespace
    detail
        A `select` declaration: `#name`, its `#variants`, `#validators`, an optional
        `#description` and, in a layer only, the `#excludes` it removes from the base's
        variants. A base-side select must have at least one variant (E202) and no excludes
        (E216); a layer's select may only restate variants, never add one (E213), and its
        excludes must not empty a select that a required member references (E212).
    entry #name   the select's `TypeName`
    entry #variants   the variants, in declaration order
    entry #validators   validator names applied to the chosen variant
    entry #description   free-text description, if any
    entry #excludes   in a layer, the variant keywords removed; empty once composed
  entry .Layer   a named refinement of a schema: an overlay for the root and definitions to merge
    detail
        A `layer` declaration of §20.3. Its `#overlay` is merged into the document root, and
        its `#records`, `#scalars` and `#selects` into the namespace, by `Tels.Layers.compose`:
        a definition of a name the base already has refines it, and a new name is added.
        Layers are what let a schema shared between programs be extended by one of them
        without forking it, and what an acceptance selects by name.
    entry #name   the layer's kebab-case name
    entry #overlay   members and validators merged into the document root; may be empty
    entry #records   record definitions added or refined by this layer
    entry #scalars   scalar definitions added or refined by this layer
    entry #selects   select definitions refined by this layer, possibly with excludes
  entry .Builtin   the names of the predefined types of §20.5
    detail
        Text constants for the four built-in scalars — `String`, `Identifier`, `TypeName`,
        `Sigil` — and `Flag`, which a `Tels.Reference` may name without the schema declaring
        them. `Flag` parses to the `Tels.Flag` type directly, never to a reference.
    entry .String   `String`: any text
    entry .Identifier   `Identifier`: a kebab-case name, not beginning with a hyphen
    entry .TypeName   `TypeName`: a PascalCase definition name
    entry .Sigil   `Sigil`: a single sigil-valid character
    entry .Flag   `Flag`: the value-less type
  entry .Axiom   the two built-in schemas: the schema of schemas and the acceptance schema
    detail
        Hand-encoded `Tels` values mirroring the canonical `tels.tel` (§20.5) and
        `acceptance.tel` (BinTEL §8.4) documents verbatim, so that an implementation can type
        a schema document, or an acceptance, before any schema has been exchanged. Every
        schema document is assigned under `.tels`; `Tels.Renderer` and
        `Tels.SemanticReconstructor` read and write the typed element under it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val schemaDocument: Tel = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel]
        Tel.Type.assign(schemaDocument, Tels.Axiom.tels)
        Tels.Reconstructor.equivalent(schemaDocument.as[Tels], Tels.Axiom.tels)   // false
        ```
    entry .tels   the schema of schemas: `Field`, `SelectRef`, `Variant`, `Record`, `Scalar`, `Select`, `Body`, `Layer`
        The schema a schema document is typed under. Its root takes `name`, an optional
        `sigil`, repeatable `record`, `scalar`, `select` and `layer` members, and the
        `document` body; a body's members are the `Member` select of `field`, `select` and
        `validate`. The `Field` record's member order puts `key` before `default`, which is
        load-bearing for positional assignment of a `field` line's atoms.
    entry .acceptance   the schema an acceptance message is typed under (BinTEL §8.4)
        A root of repeatable `accept` members, each an `Alternative` with a `schema`
        signature, the `self-contained` and `any-published` flags and repeatable `component`
        prefixes. Its `Signature` and `Component` scalars carry the `schema-signature` and
        `base-256` encodings, bound by `Tel.Codec.Bindings.builtins`.
  entry .Decoder   validate a document against the schema in scope, then decode it
    detail
        Extension methods on `Tel` which run `Tel.Type.assign` against a `given Tels` before
        returning the document or decoding it, so that a document is checked against its
        schema and then read as a case class in one expression. They are imported by name:
        `import Tels.Decoder.asValidated`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        given Tels = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")

        import Tels.Decoder.asValidated
        t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel].asValidated[Person]   // Person(t"Percy", 30)
        ```

        Under the default fail-fast tactic the first E2xx/E3xx violation aborts with a
        `Tel.Error`; under a `validate[Tel.Focus]` boundary the document-level violations
        accrue, each with the `Telp` of its element.
    entry .validate   checks a document against the schema in scope and returns it unchanged
        Three overloads: with the `Tels` alone; with a `Tel.Validator.Registry` too, so that
        named validators are applied; and with `Tel.Codec.Bindings` as well, so that declared
        encodings (§21.7) are checked against the codecs in scope. Each raises `Tel.Error` and
        tracks `Tel.Focus`.
    entry .asValidated   validates, then decodes as `tel.as[T]`
        `tel.asValidated[T]` with a `given Tels`: `Tel.Type.assign` first, then `tel.as[T]` through
        the type's `Decodable in Tel`.
  entry .Resolution   the schema-resolution protocol of BinTEL §8.2: steps, stores, delegate and error
    detail
        A document arriving as BinTEL names its schema by signature or by reference, and the
        reader resolves it through five steps in order: `Embedded` (the frame carries it),
        `Builtin` (`Tels.Axiom`), `Cache` and `Library` (a content-addressed `.Store`), and
        `Lira` (a `.Delegate` which fetches a published release). The engine which runs the
        steps is `SchemaResolver`, in the binary component; this object holds the vocabulary:
        the `.Step` taxonomy, the `.Store` abstraction with its in-memory `Memory`, the
        step-4 `.Delegate` SPI, the `.Body` wrapper for a resolved schema's bytes, and
        `.Error`, which sits outside the E-code taxonomy and names the failing step.
    refer stratiform.SchemaResolver
    entry .Step   the five resolution steps, in order: `Embedded`, `Builtin`, `Cache`, `Library`, `Lira`
        A hash lookup is order-independent — any store's answer for a signature is the right
        answer — so the order is one of cost, not authority. `Resolution.Error.Reason.Unresolved`
        reports the step at which an identifier was given up on.
    entry .Store   a content-addressed store of schema bodies, serving steps 2 and 3
      detail
          Any store may answer `#apply` for a signature and `#cache` a body under one; bare
          development references (a domain and name with no version) resolve only through
          `#reference`, against local state. `Memory` is the in-memory implementation used as
          the process-lifetime cache and as a test double.
      entry .Memory   an in-memory store, keyed by the signature's BASE-256 rendering
        detail
            Signatures are keyed by their BASE-256 text, since `Data` has no structural
            equality. Thread-safe. `#install` seeds a development reference with a local
            working copy, as the `tel schema add` command does for a developer's cache.
        entry #apply   the body cached under a signature, if any
        entry #reference   the body installed under a domain and name, if any
        entry #cache   stores a body under its signature
        entry #install   seeds a bare development reference with a body
      entry #apply   the body cached under a signature, if any
      entry #reference   the body a bare development reference names, if any
      entry #cache   stores a body under its signature
    entry .Body   the bytes of a resolved schema, wrapped so a delegate's result stays pure
      detail
          A bare `Data` result from an effectful delegate would carry a fresh capability
          under capture checking; the wrapper keeps the SPI's results plain values.
      entry #data   the schema body's bytes
    entry .Delegate   the step-4 SPI: resolve a schema from a published LIRA release
      detail
          Implemented by the LIRA format (in its own repository); an implementation without
          network access answers from local state and `Unset` otherwise. Both methods raise
          `Resolution.Error`.
      entry #bySignature   resolves a signature's components by exact identity, with an optional reference
          The signature is authoritative: if a `Tel.Pragma.Reference` is given too, the lineage
          it resolves to must serve the signature, else `ReferenceDisagrees`.
      entry #bySelector   resolves a `:version` or `:tag` selector to a signed release
          The resolver verifies the release's manifest signature rather than trusting a store
          index; a failure is `Unverified`.
    entry .Error   raised when a schema cannot be resolved; names the failing step
      detail
          Error 611: _the schema does not resolve because …_. Raised by `SchemaResolver`,
          `Tels.Layers.select` (an `UnknownLayer`) and acceptance handling. The reasons are
          `UnknownLayer(layer)`, a selected layer the schema does not declare;
          `ComponentCount(expected, found)`, a signature with the wrong number of components;
          `BaseMismatch` and `LayerMismatch(layer)`, a component hash which disagrees with the
          schema's; `ReferenceDisagrees`, a reference whose lineage does not serve the
          signature; `Unresolved(step, identifier)`, given up at the named step;
          `NotSchema(detail)`, a resolved body which is not a schema document; and
          `Unverified(detail)`, a failed signature check.
      entry .Reason   the failure kinds of schema resolution
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #reason   why resolution failed
  entry .Layers   composes a base schema with its layers (§20.3)
    detail
        Layer composition folds a schema's `layers` into its base, producing a flat `Tels`
        whose `layers` is empty. A layer's record, scalar and select definitions are merged
        into the namespace — a same-named definition refines the base's, a new name is added —
        and its overlay is merged into the document root. The merge rules are what keep a
        layer from loosening what the base accepted: a restated member may tighten a polarity
        but not loosen it (E214, E215); its type must equal the base's or both must be structs,
        which merge recursively (E206); a layer may add validators but never remove one; a
        select may lose variants through excludes but never gain one (E213); an encoding once
        declared cannot change (E218); and a scalar's patterns may be replaced only by a
        language contained in the old one, decided by RE2 containment and failing closed when
        the analysis cannot prove it (E223).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val source: Text =
          t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n\nlayer with-email\n  overlay\n    field email String optional\n"

        val schema: Tels = source.read[Tel].as[Tels]
        val everything: Tels = Tels.Layers.compose(schema)
        val selected: Tels = Tels.Layers.compose(schema, List(t"with-email"))
        everything.document.members.length   // 2
        ```

        A selection of layers, as a document's pragma names them, must be a subsequence of
        the declaration order (E124), so each selected set has one canonical spelling.
    entry .compose   the base with every layer applied, or with just the selected layers
        `compose(schema)` applies every layer in `schema.layers` in order, raising
        `DuplicateLayerName` (E204) for a name declared twice. `compose(schema, selection)`
        applies only the named layers, in declaration order, after `.select` has checked
        them; it raises `Resolution.Error` for an unknown name and `Tel.Error` for an
        out-of-order or duplicate selection. Either result has an empty `layers`.
    entry .select   the `Layer` values a selection names, checked for existence and order
        Each name must be declared by the schema (else `Resolution.Error.Reason.UnknownLayer`),
        and the selection must follow the declaration order (else `LayerOrderMismatch`, E124).
    entry .composeComponents   composes a base with an ordered sequence of layers, named or virtual
        As a composition of components in the sense of §20.3: each layer is applied in turn,
        with no name check — the virtual single-atom layers of `Tels.Atoms` have no name, and
        one listed twice applies twice, harmlessly — and the composed schema is then checked
        once as a whole (`Tels.Validation`'s post-composition checks). This is how an
        acceptance's chosen composition is built.
  entry .Validation   checks a schema's well-formedness, before and after composition (§20.1)
    detail
        Some constraints hold of each declaration site — unique definition names (E210), a
        sigil-valid sigil (E207), a declared select with at least one variant (E202) and no
        excludes (E216), every pattern valid RE2 (E222) — and are checked on the base and on
        each layer before composition. Others hold only of the composed result — unique
        keywords across a struct's fields and the variants of its selects (E201), `tel`
        reserved (E208), references which resolve to the right kind (E209, E217), a default
        only on a required scalar (E203), the key-field rules (E219–E221) and a required
        select left with no variants (E212) — and are checked after. Checking patterns before
        composing is what guarantees that containment (E223) is never asked about a pattern
        which does not compile.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val source: Text = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n"
        val schema: Tels = Tels.Validation.validate(source.read[Tel].as[Tels])
        ```
    entry .validate   checks the base, composes the layers, checks the result, and returns the composed schema
        `validate(schema)` composes every layer; `validate(schema, selection)` composes only the
        named ones, as a document's pragma selects them, and may also raise `Resolution.Error`
        for an unknown layer. Both raise `Tel.Error` with the E2xx reason of the first
        constraint that fails. `Tels.tels` runs the base checks itself, so a derived schema is
        checked as a hand-written one is.
  entry .Reconstructor   structural equality of schemas, and the reader behind `tel.as[Tels]`
    detail
        The inverse of the schema of schemas: given a TEL document whose surface uses the
        `tels` vocabulary (`name`, `record`, `scalar`, `select`, `document`, `layer`), it
        rebuilds a `Tels` value, prepending the four built-in scalars. The reading itself is
        reached through the given `Tel.telsDecodable`, as `tel.as[Tels]`; what remains public
        here is `.equivalent`. A definition's name may be written as the first atom
        (`record Contact`) or as a `name` child; a `field` line's atoms are assigned
        positionally, so a trailing `key` is the flag and the first other atom the default.
    entry .equivalent   deep structural equality of two schemas
        Compares name, sigil, the document struct, and the record, scalar, select and layer
        lists element by element and in order; patterns compare textually. A hand-written
        `Tels` is `equivalent` to the one reconstructed from its own rendering only if it
        lists the four built-in scalars first, as `Tels.Axiom.tels` does.
  entry .SemanticReconstructor   the reader behind `element.as[Tels]`: a schema from its typed element
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use: the inverse of `Tel.Type.assign` for schema documents, which
        rebuilds a `Tels` from the semantic element produced by decoding an embedded BinTEL
        schema body under `Tels.Axiom.tels`. It is reached through `Tels.elementDecodable`;
        its members are package-private.
  entry .Renderer   writes a schema value as the typed element of its schema document
    detail
        The inverse of `Tels.SemanticReconstructor`: a `Tels` built in Scala — derived,
        composed or decomposed into atoms — becomes the `Tel.Element.Node` a parsed schema
        document would assign to under the `tels` axiom, so that it is encoded, hashed and
        signed exactly as a parsed document is. `SchemaSignature` and the BinTEL encoder work
        from this element. Only a reference, `Flag` or a built-in scalar can be written in a
        schema document, so a member whose type is an inline struct or a constrained inline
        scalar — which a derived `Map` field produces — cannot be rendered and raises
        `.Error`. The four built-in scalars are omitted, as no document declares them.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        val element: Tel.Element.Node = Tels.Renderer.element(Tels.tels[Person](t"person"))
        ```
    entry .element   the schema document's root element: name, sigil, definitions, body and layers
        Under `axiom` (`Tels.Axiom.tels` unless another is given). Raises `Renderer.Error`
        for a member type with no textual form.
    entry .layer   a layer as a document root of its own, under the axiom's `Layer` definition
        The form BinTEL §8.1 hashes for a layer component and — with an empty name and a single
        atom — for an atom of `Tels.Atoms`. An overlay with no members and no validators is
        not written.
    entry .Error   raised when a schema has a member type no document can name
      detail
          Error 613: _the schema cannot be rendered because …_. `InlineStruct(keyword)` is a
          member whose type is an inline struct, such as the `entries` member a derived `Map`
          field produces; `InlineScalar(keyword)` is a member whose inline scalar carries an
          encoding, patterns or a validator other than the four built-in ones. A derived
          schema with such a member can still type and validate documents, but cannot be
          written out, hashed or signed.
      entry .Reason   the two reasons: `InlineStruct(keyword)` and `InlineScalar(keyword)`
        entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #reason   why rendering failed
  entry .Atoms   decomposes a schema into the smallest components a composition can name (§20.3)
    detail
        An *atom* is the smallest modification which can appear on its own in a composition:
        one member or validator of a record, one validator, the patterns, the encoding or the
        description of a scalar, the variants, one exclude or one validator of a select, or
        one member or validator of the body. Every atom but the head — the base's `name` and
        `sigil` — is a virtual `Tels.Layer` with an empty name holding exactly that atom under
        its `.Path`, which is both how BinTEL §8.1 hashes it and how `Tels.Layers.composeComponents`
        applies it. A `.Group` is the base or a layer with its atoms in canonical order.

        `.split` is the use an acceptance makes of this: a derived schema's required root
        members, with the definitions they reach, form the base a reader requires, and each
        optional member becomes an atom it can accept, so two types which agree on their
        required fields interoperate however their optional fields differ.
    refer stratiform.SchemaSignature
    entry .Path   where an atom applies: `Body`, `Record(name)`, `Scalar(name)` or `Select(name)`
    entry .Atom   one atom: the `Head(name, sigil)`, or a `Part(path, layer)` holding one modification
      detail
          The head is the schema document with every definition removed and an empty body;
          a part is its virtual layer.
      entry #element   the atom as the element it renders as: a definition-less schema, or a layer
          `Tels.Renderer.element` for the head and `Tels.Renderer.layer` for a part, under
          the given axiom; raises `Tels.Renderer.Error`.
    entry .Group   the base (name absent) or a layer, with its atoms in canonical order
      detail
          Canonical order is the head (base only); the atoms of each record, scalar and
          select in source order; then the body's members and validators in source order.
      entry #name   the layer's name, or `Unset` for the base
      entry #atoms   the atoms, in canonical order
    entry .decompose   the schema's groups: the base, then each layer in declaration order
    entry .base   the base's group: the head followed by its definitions' and body's atoms
    entry .layer   a layer's group: its definitions' and overlay's atoms
    entry .expansion   a sequence of groups flattened to its atoms
    entry .split   a schema's required base, and its optional members as atoms it can accept
        Returns `(base, atoms)`: the base keeps the root's required members and every
        definition reachable from them or from a layer; the atoms are the definitions left
        over, then each optional root member, in that order so that a composition adding them
        one at a time resolves each reference when it reaches it.
  entry .Subtyping   whether a document written under one composed schema can be read under another (§24.3)
    detail
        `sub <: sup` when a consumer expecting `sup` can read any document composed under
        `sub`. Records are subtyped by extension — every required member of the supertype is
        present in the subtype, in the same relative order and of the same kind; a member the
        supertype requires is required, one it does not repeat does not repeat, one it keys is
        keyed; selects by narrowing — every variant of the subtype's select is a variant of
        the supertype's; and scalars by tightening — at least the supertype's validators, a
        pattern language within its, and its encoding kept. References unfold coinductively,
        so the decision terminates over recursive definitions. BinTEL §8.2 makes this the
        compatibility relation acceptances are decided by.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val source: Text =
          t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n\nlayer with-email\n  overlay\n    field email String optional\n"

        val schema: Tels = source.read[Tel].as[Tels]
        val plain: Tels = Tels.Layers.compose(schema, List())
        val extended: Tels = Tels.Layers.compose(schema, List(t"with-email"))

        Tels.Subtyping.subtype(extended, plain)   // true: the extension can be read as the base
        Tels.Subtyping.check(plain, extended)     // Unset: optional members need not be present
        ```

        Both arguments should be composed schemas: an uncomposed schema's layers are ignored.
    refer stratiform.Tel.Acceptance
    entry .Failure   the first premise of the subtype relation which fails, with the path to it
      detail
          Each case carries the `path` of struct keywords from the root (innermost first; the
          empty list is the document root), and most a `keyword`: `MissingRequired`, a required
          member of the supertype the subtype lacks; `MemberOrder`, a member out of relative
          order; `MemberKind`, a field where a select was expected or the reverse;
          `Validators`, a validator of the supertype the subtype lacks; `Patterns`, a pattern
          language not within the supertype's; `Encoding`, an encoding not kept; `Required`,
          `Repeatable` and `Key`, a polarity or key the subtype does not match; `VariantMissing`,
          a variant the supertype's select lacks; `TypeKind`, types of different kinds; and
          `Unresolved(path, name)`, a reference which does not resolve.
      entry .communicable   renders each failure as a message naming the path
    entry .subtype   whether `sub <: sup`
    entry .check   the first failing premise of `sub <: sup`, or `Unset` when it holds
  entry .Projection   restricts an element valid under a subtype to the members its supertype can address (§24.5)
    detail
        At every struct, children whose keyword the supertype's struct does not declare are
        dropped, and the rest are re-indexed to the supertype's flat keyword positions —
        BinTEL keyword indices are positional, so keeping the subtype's indices would encode a
        different document — and typed by the supertype's member. Scalars and flags pass
        through. That `from <: to` holds is the caller's responsibility (`Tels.Subtyping`); a
        member whose kinds disagree is dropped rather than mistyped. This is how a writer
        serves a reader which accepts less than the writer holds.
    entry .project   an element typed under `from`, projected onto `to`
        Raises `Bintel.Error` from the keyword flattening of the two schemas.

entry .Tels2   the trait which holds the schema-derivation givens and `tels`
  hidden
  detail
      Mixed into the `Tels` companion so that its givens are in the implicit scope of
      `Tels.Type`; not for direct use. `Tels` inherits its members, which are reached as
      `Tels.tels` and so on: the givens `encoded`, `text`, `string`, `int`, `long`,
      `double`, `boolean`, `optional`, `list`, `set`, `sequence`, `map` and `schematic`, and
      the method `tels`.
  entry #encoded   derives the schema of a type with a declared BinTEL encoding
      A type marked `Tel.Encoded[name]` derives as a scalar whose `encoding` is `name`, so the
      AST and the staged BinTEL paths agree on its wire form by construction. In implicit
      scope through the `Tels` companion.
  entry #text   `Text` derives as an unconstrained scalar
  entry #string   `String` derives as an unconstrained scalar
  entry #int   `Int` derives as an unconstrained scalar
  entry #long   `Long` derives as an unconstrained scalar
  entry #double   `Double` derives as an unconstrained scalar
  entry #boolean   `Boolean` derives as an unconstrained scalar holding `true` or `false`
      A `Boolean` field is a scalar, not a `Flag`: the document writes `active true`, and a
      bare keyword is not a value.
  entry #optional   an `Optional[T]` field derives as `T`'s schema with `Loose` required polarity
      The field is written `optional`; its definitions, layers and identities are `T`'s.
  entry #list   a `List[T]` field derives as a repeatable, optional field of `T`'s schema
      TEL repeats a field rather than wrapping it in a collection: `item 1`, `item 2`, `item 3`
      are three occurrences of one `item` member, declared `optional repeatable`.
  entry #set   a `Set[T]` field derives as a repeatable, optional field of `T`'s schema
  entry #sequence   a `Sequence[T]` field derives as a repeatable, optional field of `T`'s schema
  entry #map   a `Map[K, V]` field derives as repeatable `entries` of `key` and `value`
      The `entries` struct is inline — it has no record of its own to reference — so a schema
      with a `Map` field types documents but cannot be rendered by `Tels.Renderer`
      (`InlineStruct`), hashed or signed.
  entry #schematic   derives the schema of any product or sum type, through `TelsDerivation`
      A case class derives as a record of its fields, each keyword the field's name in
      kebab-case (or its `@name`), `Optional` and collection fields loosened as `#optional`
      and `#list` say, and `@assign(layer)` fields grouped into layers. An enum or sealed
      trait derives as a select of its cases, with `@retract(layer)` cases excluded by the
      named layer; at a member position a sum is reached through a record named
      `<Sum>Value` holding one `select` of it, since a field cannot reference a select
      directly (E217).
  entry #tels   assembles the schema of a type: `Tels.tels[T](name)`
      Collects the identities of every definition the type and the types it references
      contribute, names them — a type keeps its simple name unless another type reached from
      the root shares it, in which case each is qualified by its enclosing types (`Phrase.Code`
      and `Element.Code` become `PhraseCode` and `ElementCode`), deepening until they differ —
      registers each record and select once, forms the `@assign` and `@retract` layers, and
      runs the base checks of `Tels.Validation`. It raises `Tel.Error`: `DuplicateDefinition`
      (E210) when two types cannot be told apart by qualification, and whatever the base
      checks find. The root type's own record is registered only when some member references
      it, which is the recursive case.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Address(street: Text, city: Text)
      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int, address: Optional[Address], tags: List[Text])

      val schema: Tels = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")
      schema.records.map(_.name).to[List]   // List(t"Address")
      ```

      A product with a field of a sum type derives, but a value of it cannot yet be written
      under the derived schema: the codec and the schema disagree on the shape of such a
      field, and the write fails with `ReferenceKindMismatch` (E217).

entry .telSchematics   fused codec-plus-schema instances built from a type's `Tel.Encodable` or `Tel.Decodable`
  detail
      Two methods — deliberately not givens, since a fused instance is `<: Schematic` and two
      would be ambiguous for a bare `Schematic` summon — which pair a type's TEL codec with
      the schema of exactly what it reads or writes. The schema is taken from the codec's own
      carried `Morphology` and reified into a `Tels.Type`, never resolved independently, so a
      gated or text-branch codec always pairs with the shape it actually uses. It mirrors
      jacinta's `jsonSchematics`. Optionality and repeatability are recorded on the field, as
      TEL does: an `Opt` field becomes `Loose`, an `Arr` field a repeatable `Loose` member of
      the element's schema, a `Dict` repeatable `entries` of `key` and `value`, and a sum a
      permissive empty struct — precise schemas of sums come from `Tels.tels`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val fused = telSchematics.encodable[Person]
      fused.schema()   // a Tels.Struct of `name` and `age`
      ```
  entry .encodable   `T is Encodable & Schematic in Tel over Tels.Type`, from `T is Tel.Encodable`
  entry .decodable   `T is Decodable & Schematic in Tel over Tels.Type`, from `T is Tel.Decodable`

entry .TelSchematic   the TEL refinement of `Schematic`: a type's schema with field-level polarity and definitions
  detail
      `Schematic over Tels.Type` says how a type's schema is represented; `TelSchematic`
      adds what TEL records on the field rather than the type — `#polarity` and
      `#repeatable`, so that an `Optional` field is `Loose` and a `List` field repeatable —
      and what a type contributes to the schema's namespace: its `#identities`, the
      `#definitions` and `#layers` it and the types it references declare, and the
      `#fieldType` a member of its type is declared with (a reference to its record, for a
      product, since a schema names definitions and never nests structs). `Tels.tels` collects
      these from the root type's instance; the givens in the `Tels` companion (`Tels.text`,
      `Tels.optional`, `Tels.list`, `Tels.schematic`, …) supply the instances, and a type
      may hand-write one, `() => Tels.Scalar(Array(t"identifier"))`, to constrain how it
      derives.

      Definitions are named by the companion's `.Naming`, keyed on a type's `.Identity`
      rather than its name: a type keeps its simple name unless another type reached from the
      root shares it, in which case each of the group is qualified by its enclosing types to
      the shallowest depth which tells them apart, so a name depends only on which types the
      schema reaches, never on the order it reaches them.
  refer stratiform.Tels
  entry .Definitions   the record and select definitions a type contributes
    entry #records   record definitions, the product's own first
    entry #selects   select definitions, the sum's own first
  entry .Identity   a derived definition's identity: a type's simple name and its enclosing names
    detail
        `enclosing` lists the packages, objects and classes around the type, innermost first,
        with compiler-generated `$` suffixes stripped. Keying on the type rather than the name
        is what tells `Phrase.Code` from `Element.Code` while still recognising one type
        reached by several paths.
    entry #name   the type's simple name
    entry #enclosing   the enclosing scopes' names, innermost first
    entry #qualified   the name qualified by the innermost `depth - 1` enclosing names
        Each enclosing name contributes its ASCII letters and digits, capitalised, as part of
        a `TypeName`: `PhraseCode` for `Phrase.Code` at depth 2, `PkgPhraseCode` at depth 3. An
        anonymous scope contributes nothing.
  entry .Naming   how a schema's definitions are named, given every identity it reaches
    detail
        Built with `Naming(identities)`: types whose simple names clash are grouped, and each
        group is qualified (`Identity#qualified`) at increasing depth until the names differ
        or stop changing. Names which coincide even so are `#ambiguous`, and `Tels.tels`
        rejects the schema (E210) rather than silently collapse two types into one
        definition.
    entry .simple   every type by its simple name
    entry .apply   the naming of a list of identities, qualifying only the clashing groups
    entry #apply   the name of a definition, by its identity
    entry #ambiguous   the names which two distinct types still share after qualification
  entry .schemaOf   a member's schema under a naming, from a `Schematic` which may or may not be a `TelSchematic`
      A plain `Schematic` has no naming to thread, so its `schema()` is used as is.
  entry .fieldTypeOf   the type a member is declared with, under a naming
  entry .identitiesOf   the identities a member's schematic contributes; none for a plain `Schematic`
  entry .definitionsOf   the definitions a member's schematic contributes; none for a plain `Schematic`
  entry .layersOf   the layers a member's schematic contributes; none for a plain `Schematic`
  entry .merge   layers of the same name, contributed by several fields or nested records, merged into one
      In order of first appearance. Overlay members and validators, records and scalars are
      concatenated; selects of the same name are combined so that a sum reached through
      several members contributes its excludes once each.
  entry .distinct   the first definition of each name
  entry #Transport   the schema representation: `Tels.Type`
  entry #polarity   the required-axis polarity of a field of this type; `Implicit` unless overridden
      `Loose` for `Optional[T]` and for collections. `Implicit` rather than `Tight` by
      default, so a derived schema hashes as the schema its author would write, with no
      `required` flag.
  entry #repeatable   the repeatable-axis polarity of a field of this type; `Loose` for collections
  entry #schema   the type's schema, with its definitions named by a `Naming`
      The no-argument `schema()` names each definition by its simple name, as a schema without
      clashes does; `schema(naming)` is what `Tels.tels` calls.
  entry #fieldType   the type a member of this type is declared with
      The schema itself for a scalar or flag; a `Tels.Reference` to the registered record for
      a product; a reference to the `<Sum>Value` wrapper record for a sum.
  entry #selectDefinitions   the select definitions a sum contributes; empty for scalars and products
  entry #identities   every definition identity this type and the types it references contribute
      `seen` cuts off the recursion through a recursive type. `Tels.tels` names all of them
      together, so two types sharing a simple name are told apart before any is built.
  entry #definitions   every definition this type and the types it references contribute, under a naming
  entry #layers   the layers a product's `@assign` fields form, as a nested record contributes them
      Each layer refines the record by the fields it groups; a sum contributes the layers its
      `@retract` cases name, each a select of the sum's name excluding those cases.
  entry #rootLayers   the same layers as the root document contributes them: its own fields in the overlay

entry .TelsDerivation   the `Derivable` behind `Tels.schematic`: products to records, sums to selects
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the derivation home `Tels.schematic` delegates to. `conjunction`
      derives a product as a `TelSchematic` whose `schema()` is the struct of its unlayered
      fields, whose `fieldType` is a reference to its record, and whose `@assign`-annotated
      fields form layers; `disjunction` derives a sum as a named select of its cases, with
      `@retract` annotations forming layers which exclude cases, and a `<Sum>Value` wrapper
      record for use at a member position. Field keywords are the labels in kebab-case, or
      their `@name`. Members are built lazily so that a recursive type can summon its own
      instance.
  entry .conjunction   derives a product type's `TelSchematic`
  entry .disjunction   derives a sum type's `TelSchematic`

entry .TelReader   the restricted rim of the TEL parser handed to a `Tel.Parsable` instance
  detail
      A `Tel.Parsable` instance consumes compound entries straight off the input, without an
      intermediate document tree, through a `TelReader`. An entry is a keyword line plus its
      subtree: `#keyword(indent)` steps to the next entry at the given indentation
      (transparently consuming blank lines, comments and tabulation headers), and exactly one
      of the consumers — `#atom`, `#int`, `#long`, `#boolean`, `#finishLine`, `#lineAtoms`,
      `#skipEntry` or `#value` — must then take that entry in full, leaving the reader at the
      next sibling. The reader carries its own `Tactic[Tel.Error]`, so an instance's `parse`
      body needs no error vocabulary: malformed input aborts through the read call's ambient
      tactic, and `#fault` and `#absentFault` reject a well-formed entry's content the same way.

      It is an exclusive, stateful capability, like the parser it wraps: owned by one
      `parse` call for the duration of that call, and nothing of it may be retained
      afterwards. Only the library's read path constructs one. The packed-keyword methods
      (`#keywordWord`, `#keywordText`) and `.reveal` are public because staged parsers are
      generated into user modules, not because an instance should call them by hand.
  refer stratiform.Tel
  entry .KeywordEnd   `-1`: the `keywordWord` sentinel for the end of the entry region
  entry .KeywordOpaque   `-2`: the `keywordWord` sentinel for a keyword which cannot pack into eight bytes
  entry .reveal   the wrapped parser, for the inlined forwarders of generated parsers
      Not for use by hand: it exists so that a capture-erased reader spliced into a staged
      parser can reassert the parser's type at the rim.
  entry #keyword   the next entry's keyword at exactly `indent`, or `Unset` when the region ends
      The reader is left positioned right after the keyword. The region ends at a shallower
      line, a document separator or the end of the input.
  entry #keywordWord   the next keyword packed into a `Long`, for comparison against literal constants
      The keyword's bytes packed least-significant first (at most eight printable-ASCII
      bytes); `KeywordEnd` once the region ends, or `KeywordOpaque` for a keyword which cannot
      pack, which is still consumed and identified by `#keywordText`.
  entry #keywordText   the keyword most recently stepped to, as text
  entry #hasSubstance   whether the current entry has an inline atom or a child, without consuming it
      The test an optional wrapper uses to map a bare keyword to an absent value.
  entry #atom   consumes the entry and gives its primary atom's text, or `Unset` if the line carries none
      Mirrors `Tel#primaryAtom`; the primitive parsers are built on it.
  entry #int   consumes the entry and parses its primary atom as an `Int`, or `Unset`
      Parsed straight from the arena bytes, without materialising the text. After an `Unset`,
      `#primaryPresent` says whether an atom was there and `#primaryText` gives its text for
      the error, so a direct-path failure reports exactly what the tree path would.
  entry #long   consumes the entry and parses its primary atom as a `Long`, or `Unset`
  entry #boolean   consumes the entry and parses its primary atom as a `Boolean`, or `Unset`
  entry #primaryPresent   whether the last numeric or boolean read found an atom at all
  entry #primaryText   the text of the atom the last numeric or boolean read could not parse
  entry #finishLine   consumes the entry's own line, leaving its children for the caller
      Including any source or literal continuation: the step before a nested record's field
      loop one level deeper.
  entry #lineAtoms   as `#finishLine`, but returning the line's atoms in every presentation form
      For a derived record parser's positional pre-pass (§19.2).
  entry #skipEntry   skips the entry entirely: an unknown keyword, or a repeated non-repeatable field
  entry #value   materialises one entry at `indent` as a `Tel`, for a field type which only has a `Tel.Decodable`
  entry #entryOrdinal   the ordinal of the current entry, for telling spans apart
      A direct parse builds no tree, so a focus takes its span from the reader while the
      parser is still on the entry; the ordinal says whether the extent on record still
      belongs to the entry of interest.
  entry #keywordSpan   the span of the current entry's keyword
  entry #valueSpan   the inline-atom run of the entry with the given ordinal, or empty
  entry #fault   raises a `Tel.Error` for the current entry's content, located at its value
      For a leaf instance rejecting a well-formed entry: continues with a sentinel under an
      accruing boundary, exactly like the tree primitives, located at the entry's value or
      its keyword when it has none.
  entry #absentFault   raises a `Tel.Error` for a field whose keyword never arrived
      There is no entry to point at, so the error carries no span, as an unresolvable
      pointer does on the tree path.

entry .Telp   a TEL path: a delimiter-first sequence of keywords and selectors over the semantic model
  detail
      TELP is the TEL Path companion specification: a schema-aware textual path. The first
      character of a path selects its delimiter, and there is no escaping — a component
      containing one candidate delimiter is written under another — so `.a.b` and `/a/b`
      are the same path, and a path's identity is its `#components` alone. Each component
      names a keyword of the current struct, or, after a repeatable keyword, selects one of
      its occurrences: an all-digit component is always a zero-based index, and any other
      selects the first occurrence whose key field (`Tels.Field#key`) has that value, code
      point for code point. A component may contain spaces.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val path: Telp = telp"/contact/nym/email/1"
      path == Telp.parse(t".contact.nym.email.1")   // true
      path.components                               // List(t"contact", t"nym", t"email", t"1")
      path.encode                                   // t"/contact/nym/email/1"
      ```

      A `telp"…"` literal is parsed as the code compiles, so `telp"contact/nym"` — no leading
      delimiter — is a compile error. `Telp` is also the pointer of `Tel.Focus`, the focus
      under which decoding errors accrue: a rendered focus such as `/person/name` is a valid,
      resolvable TELP, since keywords are kebab-case and so never all digits. It is a query
      mechanism and defines no edit operations; those address a document by `Tel.Pointer`.

      Resolution (`#resolve`) runs over the typed element `Tel.Type.assign` produces, with
      the composed `Tels` it was assigned under in scope, and answers with a `.Resolution`:
      one element, or a repeatable keyword's whole occurrence sequence when the path ends on
      it. Each way a path can fail is a `.Error` naming the component at which it did.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val source: Text =
        t"name book\n\nrecord Contact\n  field name Identifier key\n  field email String optional repeatable\n\ndocument\n  field owner Contact\n  field contact Contact optional repeatable\n"

      given schema: Tels = Tels.Validation.validate(source.read[Tel].as[Tels])

      val root: Tel.Element =
        Tel.Type.assign(t"owner hal\ncontact nym\n  email nym@example.com\n  email nym@example.org\ncontact pistol\n".read[Tel], schema)
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A non-repeatable member needs no selector
        code
            telp"/owner/name".resolve(root)          // One(Value "hal")
      frame
        label A repeatable keyword at the end of the path is its occurrence sequence
        code
            telp"/contact".resolve(root)             // Occurrences(nym, pistol)
      frame
        label An occurrence is selected by its key value
        code
            telp"/contact/nym/name".resolve(root)    // One(Value "nym")
      frame
        label An unkeyed repeatable member is selected by index
        code
            telp"/contact/nym/email/1".resolve(root) // One(Value "nym@example.org")
      ##
      ```

      Here `contact` is an optional, repeatable `Contact` whose `name` is its key, so `nym`
      selects the first occurrence keyed `nym`, while `email`, which has no key, is selected
      by index alone.
  refer stratiform.telp
  refer stratiform.Tel.Focus
  refer stratiform.Tel.Pointer
  entry .Root   the empty path: the delimiter alone, resolving to the context element
  entry .delimiters   the twenty-two characters a path may begin with
      `!"#$%&*+,./:;=?@\^_`|~`: the sigil-valid set minus `-` and `'`, which may appear inside
      a kebab-case identifier, plus `+`. `/` and `.` are the conventional choices.
  entry .Error   raised when a path does not parse or does not resolve
    detail
        Error 610: _the TELP does not resolve because …_. The `#index` is the zero-based
        component at which parsing or resolution failed. The reasons are outcomes of the
        resolving API, not document errors, and carry no E-codes: `Syntax`, a path which is
        empty, begins with no delimiter, has an empty component, or holds a line break;
        `UnknownKeyword`, a component matching no keyword of the current struct (a select's
        name is not a keyword; its variants' are); `NonStructDescent`, descending below a
        value or a flag; `AbsentMember`, a non-repeatable optional member not present;
        `IndexOutOfRange`, an index beyond the occurrence sequence; `TypeNotKeyed`, a key
        selector on a type with no key field; and `KeyNotFound`, no occurrence with that key.
    entry .Reason   the seven failure kinds of TELP §7
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   why the path failed
    entry #index   the zero-based index of the component at which it failed
  entry .Resolution   what a path resolves to: one element, or a keyword's occurrence sequence
    detail
        `One(element)` when the path ends on a non-repeatable member, a selected occurrence
        or the root; `Occurrences(elements)` — possibly empty — when it ends on a repeatable
        keyword with no selector.
    entry .One   a single element
      entry #element   the element
    entry .Occurrences   one keyword's ordered occurrence sequence
      entry #elements   the occurrences, in document order
  entry .parse   parses a path's text, raising `Telp.Error` with `Syntax` at the failing component
      The first character selects the delimiter; the rest splits at every further occurrence.
      The delimiter alone is `.Root`. Text-to-path decoding, `text.as[Telp]`, goes through
      `.decodable`, and the compile-time `telp"…"` literal through `.interpolable`.
  entry .unsafeParse   parses a literal the `telp"…"` macro has already checked
      Takes an `Unsafe` token: the gate asserts that the text was parsed at expansion, so the
      runtime re-parse cannot fail. Not for text of unknown provenance.
  entry .decodable   `text.as[Telp]`, needing a `Tactic[Telp.Error]`
  entry .interpolable   the `telp"…"` literal, parsed as the code compiles
      A syntax error is reported at the offending component, and substitutions are rejected,
      as `jp"…"` and `yp"…"` reject them.
  entry .encodable   renders a path under the first delimiter no component contains
      `/` is preferred, then `.`, then the rest of `.delimiters` in order; a path whose
      components exhaust all twenty-two is unaddressable, and rendering falls back to `/`
      rather than failing. `Telp(List(t"contact", t"a.b/c")).encode` is `!contact!a.b/c`.
  entry #components   the path's components, root first; the path's whole identity
  entry #prepend   a path with a component pushed onto the root end
      Decoding builds a focus from the inside out — an outer `focus` block runs after the
      inner ones — so each level prepends its own keyword, giving `/person/name`.
  entry #/   `path / component`: the path extended by one component at the leaf end
  entry #resolve   the element or occurrences the path selects in a typed element, under the `Tels` in scope
      `resolve(element)` walks an element `Tel.Type.assign` produced, raising `Telp.Error`
      at the first component which fails. `resolve(tel)` is the convenience which assigns the
      document under the schema first, and so also needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]` and
      `Foci[Tel.Focus]`. Defaults are materialised in the assigned element, so a key supplied
      by a default still selects; index and key selection count same-keyword occurrences
      only, so a `dog` between two `cat`s does not disturb the cat sequence.

entry .Patterns   compiled RE2 pattern constraints, memoised by pattern text
  detail
      A schema holds its patterns (§21.8) as text, so that a `Tels` stays a pure,
      structurally comparable, BinTEL-round-trippable value; but compiling one is a full
      parse and program compilation, and validation asks for the same handful once per scalar
      value. This process-global cache stores each pattern's outcome — including that it is
      not valid RE2, so an invalid pattern is not re-parsed on every attempt — keyed on the
      pattern's text. It is unbounded, which is acceptable because the keys are schema text:
      few and long-lived.
  entry .compile   the compiled `Motif` of a pattern, or `Unset` if it is not valid RE2
      The caller decides what `Unset` means: E222 at schema-validity time, and fail-closed
      everywhere else — a value is never treated as matching a pattern which did not compile.

entry .assign   `@assign(layer)`: declares a case-class field in a schema layer
    An annotation on a field of a case class, naming the layer object the field belongs to
    (§20.3): the derived schema's base omits the field, and the layer declares it, refining
    the record the field belongs to, or the document root's overlay, so that the field is a
    component a reader can accept or decline by the layer's hash. Fields of one product
    assigned to the same layer form one layer, and the annotation composes with `@name`. The
    layer is a singleton object extending `Tel.Layer("…")`, whose name is part of its type,
    which the derivation checks.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    object withPhone extends Tel.Layer("with-phone")

    case class Contact(name: Text, email: Optional[Text], @assign(withPhone) phone: Optional[Text])
    ```

    In the `soundness` umbrella `assign` is exported as a type alone, so that its
    constructor proxy does not hide anamnesis's `assign` method; the annotation `@assign(…)`
    is unaffected.

entry .retract   `@retract(layer)`: removes a case of a sum from a schema layer
    An annotation on a case of an `enum`, or a case class or object extending a sealed trait
    (§20.3): the case remains a variant of the base's `select`, and the named layer's select
    of the same name excludes it, so a reader accepting the layer says it will never receive
    that case. A case may be retracted from several layers, and several cases from one,
    though never every case of a sum (E212), which the derivation checks. A layer cannot add
    a variant (E213), so this is the only way a case of a sum is associated with a layer. A
    writer's `fulfil` leaves out any layer which retracts a case the value uses.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    object lean extends Tel.Layer("lean")

    enum Message:
      @retract(lean) case Ping
      @retract(lean) case Data(payload: Text)
      case Stop
    ```

entry .conversions   a given to import by name: any TEL-encodable value where a `Tel` is wanted
  detail
      Importing `conversions.encodableToTel` brings a `Conversion` into lexical scope which
      lets any `Encodable in Tel` value be supplied directly at a position expecting a `Tel`,
      such as a lens assignment `tel.lens(_.field = value)`, without writing `.encode`. It is
      imported by name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens, and the package
      merges with the umbrella's own `conversions`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import conversions.encodableToTel

      val value: Tel = t"Percy"
      ```
  entry .encodableToTel   `Conversion[T, Tel]` for any `T: Encodable in Tel`

entry .dynamicAccess   a given to import by name: unchecked `tel.field` access to a document
  detail
      Importing `dynamicAccess.dynamicTel` grants `Tel is Dynamical`, the erased permission
      which unlocks the dynamic syntax — `tel.name`, `tel.address.city` — for the rest of the
      scope, for reaching into a document whose shape is only informally known. The
      alternative, for one block, is `dynamically[Tel]:` (or `dynamically:`, for every
      format). Verified access through `tel.verify[T]` needs no permission, and the two styles
      stay visibly distinct.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel

      val document: Tel = t"name Percy\nage 30\n".read[Tel]
      document.name.as[Text]   // t"Percy"
      ```
  entry .dynamicTel   `Tel is Dynamical`: the permission for `tel.field` syntax

entry .Bintel   the BinTEL binary form of a typed TEL document: framing, encoding, decoding and direct parsing
  detail
      BinTEL is TEL's compact binary encoding: the same data as the text form, but guided by a
      schema (a `Tels`), so that the stream carries no keywords, only varint-encoded *keyword
      indices* resolved against the schema, and values typed by the schema rather than tagged
      in the stream. `Bintel` is the object which does all of it: `encode` and `decode` turn a
      type-assigned `Tel.Element` tree (what `Tel.Type.assign` produces) into body bytes and
      back; `frame`, `unframe`, `documentExtent` and the `decodeDocument` family handle the
      document header; `selfContained` and the `…SelfContained` decoders carry the schema with
      the data; `read` and `parse` go straight from bytes to a typed value.

      Most programs never call `encode` directly: `value.bintel` (the extension on any
      `Tel.Encodable` value, deriving the schema from its type), `tel.bintel(schema)` and
      `element.bintel(schema)` do, and `Bintel.read[T](bytes)` is their inverse.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val bytes: Data = Person(t"Percy", 30).bintel
      Bintel.read[Person](bytes)   // Person(t"Percy", 30)
      ```

      A *body* is what `encode` emits: the document root alone, with no magic number and no
      signature; it can only be read by a reader which already holds the schema. A *document*
      wraps a body in the §6 header — the magic number (`.magic` or `.magicSelfContained`), a
      varint *document length* counting every byte that follows it, the schema's signature
      (`SchemaSignature`), and in self-contained mode the schema's own BinTEL body under the
      `tels` axiom — so a reader can delimit, skip or forward documents without their schema,
      and several documents may share one stream.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A body: the root alone, readable only with the schema in hand
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val schemaDoc = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel]
            val schema = schemaDoc.as[Tels]
            val greeting = t"name Percy\n".read[Tel]
            val body: Data = greeting.bintel(schema)
            Bintel.decode(body, schema)   // the Tel.Element root
      frame
        label A document: the body framed under the schema's signature
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val schemaDoc = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel]
            val schema = schemaDoc.as[Tels]
            val greeting = t"name Percy\n".read[Tel]
            val body: Data = greeting.bintel(schema)
            val signature: Data = SchemaSignature.fromDocument(schemaDoc, Tels.Axiom.tels)
            val document: Data = Bintel.frame(body, signature)
            Bintel.unframe(document).signature.length   // 33
            Bintel.decodeWholeDocument(document, schema).root
      frame
        label A stream: documents back to back, each delimited by its declared length
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val schemaDoc = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel]
            val schema = schemaDoc.as[Tels]
            val greeting = t"name Percy\n".read[Tel]
            val body: Data = greeting.bintel(schema)
            val signature: Data = SchemaSignature.fromDocument(schemaDoc, Tels.Axiom.tels)
            val document: Data = Bintel.frame(body, signature)
            val stream: Data = Array.concat(document, document)
            Bintel.documentExtent(stream) == document.length      // where the second begins
            Bintel.decodeStream(stream, schema).map(_.continuation)   // List(document.length, stream.length)
      ##
      ```

      Every failure of the bytes is a `Bintel.Error` (error 609) whose `Reason` is one of the
      specification's B-codes, B01 to B16, plus the decoder's own nesting limit; the error
      entry lists them. A scalar whose schema declares an *encoding* (TEL §21.7) is written and
      read through a `Tel.Codec.Bindings`, which the codec-aware overloads take; with no
      binding, meeting such a scalar is B13 (`CodecUnresolved`), and `checkCanonical = true`
      additionally insists that an encoded scalar's bytes are the re-encoding of their decoded
      text (B15).

      Every decoder treats its counts as adversarial: a child count larger than the bytes
      remaining is rejected before anything is allocated, a declared length is checked against
      the input before it is acted on and never sizes a buffer, and structs nested more than
      256 deep (matching the type-assignment limit of TEL §20.2) are `NestingLimitExceeded`. A
      reader consuming an untrusted stream should still bound the number and size of the
      documents it accepts.
  refer stratiform.SchemaSignature
  refer stratiform.SchemaResolver
  refer stratiform.Varint
  refer stratiform.Base256
  refer stratiform.BintelInlinable
  refer stratiform.Framed
  entry .magic   the four bytes which begin an external-schema document: `B2 C4 B5 BB`
      Read as BASE-256 text these are `βτελ`, so a document's text form (`.text`) begins with
      the word. The document length varint follows them. A document whose first four bytes
      match neither this nor `.magicSelfContained` is `BadMagic` (B01).
  entry .magicSelfContained   the four bytes which begin a self-contained document: `B2 C4 B5 BC`
      `βτεμ` in BASE-256 — the trailing μ, for *monolithic*, is what tells self-contained mode
      from external mode's `βτελ`. After the length and the signature, a self-contained
      document carries its schema's BinTEL body (under the `tels` axiom) before the root.
  entry .Framed   one unframed external-schema document: its signature bytes, its body bytes and its continuation
    detail
        What `.unframe` returns: the header has been read and validated, but the body has not
        been decoded, so the schema is not needed yet. This is the shape a router works with
        when it chooses a schema per signature before decoding.
    entry #signature   the schema signature the document was written under, as raw palimpsest bytes
        Render it with `Base256.encode` to compare it with a pragma's signature text or with
        `SchemaResolver.telsSignature`.
    entry #body   the document root's body bytes, exactly what `Bintel.encode` emitted
    entry #continuation   the offset one past the document's last byte, where the next document (or anything else) begins
  entry .Document   one decoded document: its signature bytes, its `Tel.Element` root and its continuation
    detail
        What the `decodeDocument` family returns. The continuation is one past the document's
        last byte as fixed by the declared length; the bytes from there are the caller's to
        interpret — a further document, content in another format, or nothing — and
        `decodeWholeDocument` is the reader which insists on nothing.
    entry #signature   the schema signature carried in the header
    entry #root   the decoded document root, a `Tel.Element.Node` typed by the schema
        Re-decode it to a typed value with `Bintel.read`'s machinery, or present it as a `Tel`
        and decode with `as[T]`; a match on `Tel.Element.Node(_, _,
        children)` reads its children directly.
    entry #continuation   the offset at which the document's continuation begins
  entry .encode   a type-assigned `Tel.Element` root as BinTEL body bytes
      `Bintel.encode(element, schema)` writes the root `Tel.Type.assign` produced — a `Node`
      with no keyword index and the schema's `document` struct as its type — as body bytes: a
      child count, then per child its flat keyword index and its payload (a scalar's byte
      length and UTF-8 bytes; a struct's own count and children; nothing for a flag). Children
      are emitted in the canonical order of TEL §7.2 — member by member in declaration order,
      source order preserved within a member, and a `SelectRef`'s variant-filling children
      kept together — so two documents with the same values encode to the same bytes, which is
      what makes the value hash (`valueHash`) well-defined.

      It raises `Bintel.Error`: `ReferenceUnresolved` (B10) when a `SelectRef` in the schema
      names no select definition, and `CodecUnresolved` (B13) when a scalar's schema declares
      an encoding and no binding was given, in which case nothing is emitted. The three-argument
      overload `encode(element, schema, codecs)` takes a `Tel.Codec.Bindings`, writes an encoded
      scalar as its codec's bytes, and additionally needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]`, since a value
      the codec rejects is `Tel.Error(EncodingRejected)` (E312): the document is invalid under
      its schema.

      The extensions `tel.bintel(schema)`, `element.bintel(schema)` and `value.bintel` call this.
  entry .decode   BinTEL body bytes back to a `Tel.Element` root under the schema they were written with
      `Bintel.decode(data, schema)` reads exactly what `.encode` emits — no magic, no signature
      — and recovers the type-assigned tree. The schema must be the same *composed* schema the
      writer used: BinTEL carries keyword indices, not keywords, so a different layer selection
      shifts every index and decodes the document against the wrong members. Bytes left over
      after the root are `TrailingBytes` (B08).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val schema = Tels.tels[Person](t"person")
      val element: Tel.Element = Bintel.decode(Person(t"Percy", 30).bintel, schema)
      ```

      The failures are `Bintel.Error`s: `VarintError` (B02) for a truncated, overwide or
      overlong varint, including one with no bytes at all (an empty input is B02, not B09);
      `UnexpectedEoi` (B09) for a child count the remaining bytes cannot satisfy;
      `BadKeywordIndex` (B05) for an index beyond the struct's flat keyword count;
      `ValueTruncated` (B06) and `BadUtf8` (B07) for a scalar payload; `ReferenceUnresolved`
      (B10); and `NestingLimitExceeded` beyond 256 nested structs.

      `decode(data, schema, codecs, checkCanonical = false)` reads an encoded scalar's bytes
      through the `Tel.Codec.Bindings`: `CodecUnresolved` (B13) when the name does not resolve
      or no binding is configured, `CodecDecodeFailed` (B14) when the codec rejects the bytes,
      and with `checkCanonical`, `CodecNoncanonical` (B15) when re-encoding the decoded text
      does not reproduce the bytes.
  entry .read   BinTEL body bytes straight to a typed value, deriving the schema from the type
      `Bintel.read[Person](bytes)` is the inverse of `person.bintel`: it derives the schema with
      `Tels.tels[Person](t"root")`, decodes the body under it, presents the tree as a `Tel`,
      and decodes that with the type's `Tel.Decodable`. The type needs a `TelSchematic` (every
      case class and sealed sum derives one) and the call a `Tactic[Bintel.Error]` for the
      bytes and a `Tactic[Tel.Error]` for the values, which accrue through the usual `Foci`.
      The overload with a `Tel.Codec.Bindings` and `checkCanonical` reads encoded scalars
      through the binding, as `.decode` does.

      This is the AST path: the element tree, its `Tel` presentation and the text-format
      decode are all materialized. `.parse`, with a `Bintel.Parsable` generated by
      `BintelInlinable.parsable`, reads the same bytes with none of them.
  entry .parse   BinTEL body bytes straight to a typed value through its `Bintel.Parsable`, with no intermediate tree
      `Bintel.parse[Person](bytes)` hands a `BintelReader` over the bytes to the
      `Person is Bintel.Parsable` in scope, which consumes the struct body element by element,
      and rejects trailing bytes exactly as `.decode` does (B08). It needs only a
      `Tactic[Bintel.Error]`; the parser's own value faults go through the `Tactic[Tel.Error]`
      the parser was generated against.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      given (Person is Bintel.Parsable) = BintelInlinable.parsable[Person]

      Bintel.parse[Person](Person(t"Percy", 30).bintel) == Bintel.read[Person](Person(t"Percy", 30).bintel)
      ```

      The overload `parse[T](data, codecs, checkCanonical = false)` reads leaves whose type
      declares an encoding (the `Tel.Encoded` marker) through the `Tel.Codec.Bindings`, with
      B13, B14 and, under `checkCanonical`, B15, matching `.decode`'s rules exactly. BinTEL has
      no per-subtree bridge back to the AST path, so a shape the generator does not support —
      a sum in field position, or a `Map` — stays on `.read`.
  entry .Parsable   the typeclass of a value which reads itself straight off BinTEL body bytes
    detail
        `T is Bintel.Parsable` has one method, `#parse`, which is called positioned at a struct
        body — a child count, then that many index-prefixed elements — and must consume it in
        full through the `BintelReader` it is given. Instances are generated, not hand-written:
        `BintelInlinable.parsable[T]` compiles one whose keyword-index dispatch is a positional
        table over `T`'s declaration order, and `Bintel.parse` is the entry point which uses
        it. The reader is an exclusive, stateful capability owned by the one `parse` call, so
        nothing of it may be retained afterwards.
    refer stratiform.BintelInlinable
    refer stratiform.BintelReader
    entry .Direct   the base of generated parsers: takes the reader as a neutral carrier and asserts the capability at the rim
      detail
          `Bintel.Parsable.Direct[T]` is what `BintelInlinable.parsable[T]` extends. Generated
          code is capture-erased, so it implements the protected `parseCarrier(reader: AnyRef)`,
          and `Direct#parse` casts the `BintelReader^` to the carrier at the audited point. A
          generated override of `parse` itself would narrow the trait's capability-typed
          parameter, which capture checking rejects at the instantiation site. Not for
          hand-written code.
      entry #parse   consumes one struct body through the reader, delegating to the generated carrier method
    entry #parse   consumes one struct body from the `BintelReader` and returns the value
  entry .frame   wraps body bytes as an external-schema document under a schema signature
      `Bintel.frame(body, signature)` writes `.magic`, the document length (a varint counting
      every byte after it), the signature's length, the signature bytes and the body. The
      signature must be a syntactically valid palimpsest: its byte length must agree with the
      cadence recovered from its trailing byte (33 bytes for a layerless schema, two more per
      layer), or `BadSignatureLength` (B03) is raised. The signature is not verified against
      any schema here; the body is not inspected.

      Together with `.unframe` this is the framing a transport uses when the schema is agreed
      separately; `tel.bintelDocument(schema, signature)` combines the encode and the frame.
  entry .unframe   splits an external-schema document into its signature and body without decoding the body
      `Bintel.unframe(data)` (or `unframe(data, offset)` for a document which does not begin
      the input) checks the magic number (`BadMagic`, B01, which a self-contained document also
      triggers here — it has a different magic), reads the declared length (`UnexpectedEoi`,
      B09, when it overruns the input), checks the signature's length pattern
      (`BadSignatureLength`, B03), and returns a `Bintel.Framed`. The body's structural extent
      is not checked against the declared length — that is B16, raised by the decoders — and
      the continuation is returned, not judged.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val document: Data = Tel.Acceptance(List()).framed
      Base256.encode(Bintel.unframe(document).signature) == Tel.Acceptance.signature
      ```
  entry .documentExtent   where the document beginning at an offset ends, read from its header alone
      `Bintel.documentExtent(data)` and `documentExtent(data, offset)` read the magic number, in
      either mode, and the declared length, and return the offset at which the continuation
      begins. No schema is resolved and nothing proportional to the document is allocated, so
      a reader may count, skip, split or forward the documents of a stream at constant cost
      each. `BadMagic` (B01) and `UnexpectedEoi` (B09) are the only failures.
  entry .decodeDocument   decodes one external-schema document and reports where its continuation begins
      `Bintel.decodeDocument(data, schema)` unframes the document at the start of the input,
      decodes its body under `schema`, and returns a `Bintel.Document` with the signature,
      the root and the continuation. Bytes beyond the document are neither decoded nor
      validated, and need not be BinTEL. The declared length must agree with the structural
      extent of the root: a forged length which conceals bytes inside the document or exposes
      bytes of the next is `DeclaredLengthMismatch` (B16). The signature is *not* verified
      against the schema; `SchemaResolver` is what does that.

      The overloads are `decodeDocument(data, schema, offset)` for a document which does not
      begin the input, and `decodeDocument(data, schema, codecs, checkCanonical = false)` and
      `decodeDocument(data, schema, codecs, checkCanonical, offset)` with a
      `Tel.Codec.Bindings` for encoded scalars. A self-contained document at the offset is
      `BadMagic` here: it is read by `.decodeDocumentSelfContained`, or dispatched there by
      `.decodeStream`.
  entry .decodeWholeDocument   decodes an external-schema document which must be the whole input
      As `.decodeDocument`, and `TrailingBytes` (B08) if the continuation is not the end of
      the input. That is a property of this reader's contract, not of the bytes: the same
      bytes are a well-formed stream to `.decodeStream`. The overload with a
      `Tel.Codec.Bindings` and `checkCanonical` reads encoded scalars through the binding.
  entry .decodeStream   decodes every document of a stream, in order, each by its declared length
      `Bintel.decodeStream(data, schema)` follows the continuations from offset zero to the end
      of the input and returns the `Bintel.Document`s in order; an empty input is an empty
      list. Every document is independent: an external-schema one is decoded under `schema`
      and a self-contained one under its embedded schema, so the two modes may be interleaved.
      A continuation which begins with neither magic number is `BadMagic` (B01) at that
      position, and decoding stops at the first error.

      A stream whose external-schema documents are typed by *several* schemas is driven by the
      caller instead, with `.documentExtent` and `.unframe`, choosing a schema per signature.
      The overload with a `Tel.Codec.Bindings` and `checkCanonical` reads encoded scalars
      through the binding.
  entry .frameSelfContained   wraps a body, its schema's body and the signature as a self-contained document
      `Bintel.frameSelfContained(signature, schemaBody, body)` writes `.magicSelfContained`,
      the document length, the signature (length and bytes), the embedded schema body (length
      and bytes — the schema document's own BinTEL body under the `tels` axiom) and the
      document body. `BadSignatureLength` (B03) if the signature's length is not a valid
      palimpsest length. `.selfContained` computes all three parts from two `Tel` documents.
  entry .selfContained   a TEL document and its schema document as one self-contained BinTEL document
      `Bintel.selfContained(tel, schemaDoc)` composes the schema from `schemaDoc` (a document
      parseable under the `tels` axiom), signs it with `SchemaSignature.fromDocument`, encodes
      the schema document under the axiom and `tel` under the schema, and frames the three
      with `.frameSelfContained`. A receiver holding only the axiom decodes the result with
      `.decodeDocumentSelfContained`, with no schema resolution at all. It needs a
      `Tactic[Tel.Error]` (the document may be invalid under the schema) and a
      `Tactic[Bintel.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val greeting = t"name Percy\n".read[Tel]
      val greetingSchema = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel]

      val document: Data = Bintel.selfContained(greeting, greetingSchema)
      Bintel.decodeWholeDocumentSelfContained(document).root
      ```
  entry .decodeDocumentSelfContained   decodes one self-contained document under the schema it carries
      `Bintel.decodeDocumentSelfContained(data)` (or with an `offset`) reads the header, the
      signature and the embedded schema body; decodes the schema body under the `tels` axiom
      and composes the schema (`EmbeddedSchemaUndecodable`, B12, on any failure); recomputes
      its signature and compares it byte for byte with the carried one
      (`EmbeddedSignatureMismatch`, B11); and only then decodes the document root under the
      reconstructed schema, returning a `Bintel.Document`. An external-schema document here is
      `BadMagic`.

      Verification proves only that the body is internally consistent with its signature: a
      receiver which requires a *trusted* schema must compare the signature against those it
      knows. The overloads with a `Tel.Codec.Bindings` and `checkCanonical` apply the binding
      to the outer document root only; the embedded schema is governed by `tels`, which
      declares no encodings, so the bootstrap never needs a codec.
  entry .decodeWholeDocumentSelfContained   decodes a self-contained document which must be the whole input
      As `.decodeDocumentSelfContained`, and `TrailingBytes` (B08) if anything follows the
      document. The overload with a `Tel.Codec.Bindings` and `checkCanonical` reads encoded
      scalars of the outer root through the binding.
  entry .text   a BinTEL document as BASE-256 text, one character per byte
      `Bintel.text(data)` is `Base256.encode(data)`: the text has as many characters as the
      document has bytes, begins with `βτελ` (or `βτεμ`), and round-trips losslessly through
      `.fromText`. This is the §9 textual form, for a document carried in a text channel.
  entry .fromText   BASE-256 text back to BinTEL bytes, permissively
      `Bintel.fromText(text)` is `Base256.decode(text)`: each character's code point mod 256
      becomes a byte, and a character outside the alphabet is not rejected. Use
      `Base256.decodeStrict` first when the text may come from an untrusted source.
  entry .Error   raised when BinTEL bytes are not a valid document, with the specification's B-code as its reason
    detail
        Error 609: _the BinTEL stream is invalid because `reason`_. Raised by every decoder,
        framer and parser in `Bintel`, by `SchemaSignature`'s encoding and decoding, and by the
        `bintel` and `valueHash` extensions. `Bintel.Error.Reason` numbers its cases by the
        B-code of the BinTEL specification:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `BadMagic`  1  _the magic number is missing or matches neither B2 C4 B5 BB (external mode) nor B2 C4 B5 BC (self-contained mode)_
        row  `VarintError`  2  _a variable-length integer in the stream is invalid_  truncated, wider than 64 bits or not minimal; a `Varint.Error` from the lower layer is always reported as this
        row  `BadSignatureLength`  3  _the schema signature length is not a valid palimpsest length under any (H, k_i, k_r)_  also raised by `SchemaSignature.encode` for an empty list or a mis-sized hash
        row  `BadSignature`  4  _the schema signature does not decode against the library_  from `SchemaSignature.decode`
        row  `BadKeywordIndex`  5  _a keyword index exceeds the parent's flat-keyword count_
        row  `ValueTruncated`  6  _a Scalar value's byte length extends beyond end of input_
        row  `BadUtf8`  7  _a Scalar value's bytes are not valid UTF-8_
        row  `TrailingBytes`  8  _a whole-document reader found bytes after the document_
        row  `UnexpectedEoi`  9  _the decoder requested bytes beyond end of input_
        row  `ReferenceUnresolved`  10  _a Reference or SelectRef in the schema does not resolve to a definition of its kind_
        row  `EmbeddedSignatureMismatch`  11  _the signature recomputed from the embedded schema body does not equal the carried signature_  in a self-contained document
        row  `EmbeddedSchemaUndecodable`  12  _the embedded schema body does not decode as a valid TEL document under tels_  in a self-contained document
        row  `CodecUnresolved`  13  _a scalar's declared encoding is not resolved by the codec binding_  or no binding was given
        row  `CodecDecodeFailed`  14  _an encoded scalar's value bytes are rejected by the bound codec_
        row  `CodecNoncanonical`  15  _an encoded scalar's value bytes are not the re-encoding of their decoded text_  checked only under `checkCanonical`
        row  `DeclaredLengthMismatch`  16  _the declared document length disagrees with the structural extent of the document_
        row  `NestingLimitExceeded`  17  _the document nests Structs more deeply than the decoder's limit_  structs more than 256 deep; not a B-code, since the specification leaves a decoder's resource limits outside the taxonomy
        ```

        The precedence between them is the specification's: truncation *inside* a varint is
        B02, not B09, so an empty input to `Bintel.decode` is `VarintError`.
    entry .Reason   the B-code: `BadMagic`, `VarintError`, … `DeclaredLengthMismatch`, and `NestingLimitExceeded`
      detail
          An enum whose `number` is the B-code; the cases are listed on `Bintel.Error`. Each
          renders as a message through `.communicable`.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message, as in _a keyword index exceeds the parent's flat-keyword count_
    entry #reason   which B-code the bytes failed

entry .Ratel   a RaTEL image: a TEL document and its schema laid out for random access from a mapped file
  detail
      RaTEL is TEL's random-access encoding. Where BinTEL is read front to back, a RaTEL image
      is read in place: every struct type has a fixed-width *row* with a *slot* for each member
      at an offset the schema determines, the instances of a record type form a *table*,
      variable-width values lie in regions addressed by offset, and sorted *indexes* over keyed
      and `index`-declared members make a lookup a binary search. A `Ratel` is the image as a
      value — its bytes, `data` — and is `Encodable in Data`, so galilei's `claim` publishes it.
      The object `Ratel` writes one (`encode`); one is read by opening whatever holds it as
      `Ratel`, with aperture's `open`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val image: Ratel = document.ratel(schema)
      file.claim[Ratel](_.publish(image))

      file.open[Ratel]():
        (ratel.root / t"role").find(t"Hamlet").let(_.text(t"play"))
      ```

      An image always carries its composed schema, with its signature, and may carry the
      document's value hash and a digest of each section. It is not byte-deterministic: the
      value hash, not the bytes, is a document's identity.
  entry .encode   a type-assigned `Tel.Element` root as a RaTEL image, by the specification's canonical writer
      `Ratel.encode(element, schema, codecs, digests)` lays the document out under `schema`,
      which is given as it is declared — with its layers — and is embedded as BinTEL beside
      its signature. `codecs` defaults to `Tel.Codec.Bindings.all`; `digests` (by default
      `true`) adds the digests section. It raises `Ratel.Error`: `SchemaUnlayoutable` (R11),
      `CodecUnresolved` (R19), `WidthMismatch` (R22) for a value whose bytes are not the width
      its scalar declares, `Unencodable` for a model the schema does not describe, and
      `Oversized` for an image of 2 GiB or more; and `Tel.Error(EncodingRejected)` for a value
      its codec rejects. The extensions `tel.ratel(schema)`, `element.ratel(schema)` and
      `value.ratel` call this.
  entry .openable   opens anything `Viewable` — a path, an image, a `Mapped` view — as `Ratel`, for reading in place
      `source.open[Ratel]()` lends a `Mapped` view of the source through its `zephyrine.Viewable`
      instance, copies and checks the header, decodes the embedded schema, recomputes its
      signature, derives the layout and checks the table and index directories against it
      (R01–R13, R19), and gives the block a `Ratel.Reader`, which the accessor `ratel` names. It
      reads nothing else until it is asked. The reader captures the view, and cannot outlive
      the block. A `Write` mode is refused with `Unwritable`: an image is replaced, by
      publishing another, and never changed. The flags are `Ratel.Flag`s.
  entry .Flag   what may be asked of `open[Ratel]`: `Verify`, and `Codecs(bindings)`
      `Verify` runs the reader's `verify` before the block, making it a verifying reader in
      the specification's sense. `Codecs(bindings)` binds codecs for encodings this module does
      not define, ahead of `Tel.Codec.Bindings.all`.
  entry .viewable   an image as a view of its bytes where they lie on the heap, so that it is opened as a file is
  entry .Reader   an open image: its schema, its root row, and whole-document operations
    entry #root   the document's root, as a `Ratel.Row`
    entry #schema   the composed schema the image carries
    entry #signature   the schema signature the image carries, which a reader requiring a trusted schema compares
    entry #valueHash   the document's value hash, if the header carries it
    entry #materialise   the semantic model the image encodes, as `Bintel.decode` would give it
    entry #verify   checks the digests, the whole document, every index against its rows, and the value hash
  entry .Row   one record, read in place: `text`, `data`, `texts`, `flag`, `count`, `record`, `/`, `has`, `holds`, `as`
      A plain value — a table and a record index — which says where a record lies. Every
      accessor takes the `Ratel.Reader` contextually, as the block of `open[Ratel]` gives it,
      so a row may be kept, collected or returned, but is read only where a reader is given;
      it should be read through the reader of the image it came from.
  entry .Rows   the records of a repeatable member: `size`, `apply`, `each`, `find`, `where`, `sorted`
  entry .Error   raised when an image cannot be read or written, with the specification's R-code as its reason
    detail
        Error 615: _the RaTEL image cannot be used because `reason`_. `Ratel.Error.Reason`
        numbers its cases 1 to 27 by the R-code of the RaTEL specification — `BadMagic`,
        `UnsupportedVersion`, `ReservedSet`, `BadLength`, `BadDirectory`, `SectionsOverlap`,
        `DigestMismatch`, `BadSignature`, `SchemaUndecodable`, `SignatureMismatch`,
        `SchemaUnlayoutable`, `BadTableDirectory`, `BadRootTable`, `BadRef`, `BadRecordIndex`,
        `RowRevisited`, `BadTag`, `BadUtf8`, `CodecUnresolved`, `CodecDecodeFailed`,
        `CodecNoncanonical`, `WidthMismatch`, `BadIndex`, `IndexUnsorted`, `IndexDisagrees`,
        `NonzeroPadding`, `ValueHashMismatch` — and continues with those outside that taxonomy:
        `NestingLimitExceeded` (28), `Unencodable` (29), `Oversized` (30), `Unindexed` (31) for
        a lookup no index answers, and `Unknown` (32), `Inapplicable` (33) and `Absent` (34)
        for a member which a row does not have, has in another kind, or has not got a value
        for, and `Unwritable` (35) for an image opened for writing. R01–R13 and R19 are raised
        as an image is opened; the others by the access which meets them, or by `verify`. `CodecNoncanonical` and `NonzeroPadding`, the specification's optional
        checks, are not yet raised.

entry .BintelReader   the restricted rim of the BinTEL body parser a `Bintel.Parsable` reads through
  detail
      A `BintelReader` is what `Bintel.parse` hands to a `Bintel.Parsable#parse` call: a bare
      offset over the body bytes, exposing the four steps of a struct body — the child
      `#count`, then per child a keyword `#index` and either a `#scalar` payload or a nested
      struct body (recursively, the same four steps). The structure is entirely count-driven
      and self-delimiting, so no lookahead and no keywords are involved: a keyword index is the
      flat position of the member it fills, matching the members' declaration order.

      The reader carries its own `Tactic[Bintel.Error]`, taken from the `Bintel.parse` call,
      so malformed bytes abort through that call's tactic; *decode* faults (a mistyped or
      absent value) go through the parser's `Tactic[Tel.Error]` and `Foci`, exactly as the AST
      path's text-format decode does. It is an exclusive, stateful capability, like the
      `BintelParser` it wraps: owned by one `parse` call for its duration, and nothing of it
      may be retained afterwards. Only stratiform constructs one; generated parsers
      (`BintelInlinable.parsable`) bind the underlying parser once per read and step through
      its direct rim without these per-step forwarders.
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  refer stratiform.BintelInlinable
  entry #count   the child count which opens a struct body
      A varint, bounded to `Int`; `VarintError` (B02) if it is malformed.
  entry #index   the flat keyword index of the next child
      A varint, bounded to `Int`; which member it names is the parser's business, since the
      reader knows no schema.
  entry #scalar   one scalar payload as text: its byte length, then that many UTF-8 bytes
      `ValueTruncated` (B06) if the length runs past the input. A BinTEL scalar is the TEL
      atom's UTF-8 bytes, so a leaf's value semantics are the text format's: an `Int` field
      parses the text as the text format would.
  entry #skipScalar   skips one scalar payload without materializing it
      For a child whose index the parser does not recognise; `ValueTruncated` (B06) if the
      length runs past the input. Framing is codec-independent, so an encoded scalar skips
      correctly too.

entry .BintelParser   the direct BinTEL body parser generated parsers step through; constructed only by `Bintel.parse`
  hidden
  detail
      The bare-offset reader behind `BintelReader`. The class is public because the parsers
      `BintelInlinable.parsable` generates, spliced into user modules, bind it once per read
      and call its `direct…` methods; its constructor and companion are package-private, so
      only stratiform's read path can make one. Each method mirrors one step of
      `Bintel.decode`'s recursive descent and fails with the same `Bintel.Error` reasons, so
      direct parsing and AST decoding agree on every malformed input. An exclusive, stateful
      capability.
  entry #directVarint   one varint as a `Long`: B02 for truncation, width over 64 bits or an overlong encoding
  entry #directCount   a child count or keyword index: a varint bounded to `Int`
  entry #directScalar   one scalar payload as a `String`: its length varint, then UTF-8 bytes (B06 if truncated)
  entry #directEncodedScalar   one scalar payload through the named codec: B13 unresolved, B14 rejected, B15 non-canonical
  entry #directScalarBytes   one scalar payload's raw bytes, undecoded, as the leaf before a codec is applied
  entry #directSkipScalar   skips one scalar payload without materializing it
  entry #directAtEnd   whether the offset has reached the end of the input

entry .Varint   the variable-length unsigned integer encoding of BinTEL §4
  detail
      Seven payload bits per byte, least-significant group first, with bit 7 set on every byte
      but the last. Two properties are normative, so that validity is a property of the bytes
      and not of the reader: the representable range is zero to 2^64 − 1, and the encoding is
      minimal — `80 00` for zero is invalid — so every integer has exactly one encoding, which
      is what makes BinTEL's byte-determinism and the value hash well-defined. Every count,
      byte length and keyword index in a BinTEL document is one of these.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Varint.encode(300L).length               // 2
      Varint.decode(Varint.encode(300L), 0)    // Varint.Decoded(300L, 2)
      ```

      `Bintel` reports every `Varint.Error` as `Bintel.Error.Reason.VarintError` (B02); the
      three reasons here distinguish what went wrong for a caller working at this layer.
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  entry .encode   a non-negative `Long` as its one minimal byte sequence
      One to ten bytes. A negative value throws an `IllegalArgumentException`, since a varint
      is unsigned: the caller's bug, not the data's.
  entry .decode   the varint at an offset of a byte array, with the offset past it
      `Varint.decode(data, offset)` returns a `Varint.Decoded`, and raises `Varint.Error` when
      the bytes end before a terminating byte (`Truncated`), encode more than 64 bits
      (`Overflow`), or end with a byte carrying no payload bits after the first (`Overlong`).
  entry .Decoded   one decoded varint: its value and the index past its last byte
    entry #value   the integer, as a `Long`
    entry #next   the offset of the first byte after the varint, where the next read begins
  entry .Error   raised when bytes are not a valid varint
    detail
        Error 608: _the varint is invalid because `reason`_. Raised by `Varint.decode` only;
        the BinTEL layer wraps it as `Bintel.Error.Reason.VarintError`.
    entry .Reason   `Truncated` (1), `Overflow` (2) or `Overlong` (3)
      detail
          `Truncated`: a continuation byte was expected and the input ended. `Overflow`: the
          value does not fit in 64 bits. `Overlong`: the encoding is not minimal — its last
          byte carries no payload bits.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   which of the three §4 rules the bytes broke

entry .SchemaSignature   a schema's identity as a palimpsest of its base and layer hashes, and the lineages it decodes against
  detail
      A schema signature is the short fingerprint by which a BinTEL document names its schema,
      and by which a TEL pragma (`tel 1.0 … ÔŀưḞ…`) identifies one: the BLAKE3-256 hash of the
      *base* schema (the document with its `layer` compounds removed, encoded as BinTEL under
      the `tels` axiom) folded with the hash of each selected layer into a *palimpsest* at
      BinTEL's pinned cadence — a 32-byte hash, a 4-byte initial cadence and a 2-byte regular
      cadence — so a schema with no layers signs in 33 bytes and each further layer adds two.
      The trailing byte carries the cadence (`0x79`), so a decoder recovers it without prior
      agreement. The value hash is over the schema's content, not its text: two schema files
      which differ only in comments, whitespace or member order sign identically.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val schemaDoc = t"name greeting\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n".read[Tel]
      val signature: Data = SchemaSignature.fromDocument(schemaDoc, Tels.Axiom.tels)
      signature.length            // 33
      Base256.encode(signature)   // the text a pragma or an acceptance carries
      ```

      Two sides use it. A *writer* signs the composition it holds: `.fromDocument` over a
      schema document, or `Lineage#signature` over a selection of its layers. A *reader*
      decodes a signature it receives back to the components it names — `.decode` against a
      list of candidate hashes, or, as the specification recommends, a `.Library` of
      `.Lineage`s which picks the base by the first four bytes and searches that base's own
      components alone — and composes the schema with `Lineage#compose`. `SchemaResolver`
      drives the whole of that for a pragma; `Tel.Acceptance` negotiates with it between peers.
  refer stratiform.SchemaResolver
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  refer stratiform.Base256
  entry .cadence   the BinTEL-pinned palimpsest cadence: a 32-byte hash, 4 initial and 2 regular bytes
      The `Cadence` given every signature here is built and decoded at. The specification
      forbids any other parameters for a schema signature, so it is pinned rather than taken
      from scope; `Cadence.pack` of it is the trailing byte `0x79`.
  entry .fromDocument   the full signature of a schema document: its base and every layer, in source order
      `SchemaSignature.fromDocument(doc, axiom)` type-assigns the document under `axiom`
      (normally `Tels.Axiom.tels`), hashes the base and each `layer` compound, and encodes the
      palimpsest of all of them. It needs a `Tactic[Tel.Error]` (the document may not be a
      schema) and a `Tactic[Bintel.Error]`. For the signature of a *selection* of layers,
      take `.componentHashes` and `.encode` the base with the chosen ones, or use a
      `.Lineage`.
  entry .fromElement   the full signature from an already type-assigned schema root
      As `.fromDocument`, starting from the `Tel.Element.Node` which `Tel.Type.assign`
      produced; this is how a self-contained BinTEL document's embedded schema is re-signed
      for verification (B11).
  entry .componentHashes   the base hash and the layer hashes a signature is built from
      `SchemaSignature.componentHashes(doc, axiom)` returns the base-schema hash and one hash
      per `layer` compound, in source order (zip them with `Tels#layers` for their names).
      `encode(base :: layers)` reproduces `.fromDocument`, and `encode(base :: chosen)` with a
      sublist of the layer hashes is the signature of the base composed with just those
      layers. The overload `componentHashes(schema: Tels, axiom)` does the same for a schema
      *value*, rendering it through `Tels.Renderer`, and needs a `Tactic[Tels.Renderer.Error]`.
  entry .encode   a palimpsest from an ordered list of 32-byte component hashes
      The first hash is the base, the rest the layers (or atoms) in order. An empty list, or
      any hash not of `.cadence`'s 32 bytes, is `Bintel.Error(BadSignatureLength)`.
  entry .componentCount   how many component hashes a signature encodes, from its trailing cadence byte
      Recovers the cadence from the XOR-fold of the signature's bytes and derives the count
      from its length; a length inconsistent with any valid cadence is
      `Bintel.Error(BadSignatureLength)`.
  entry .decode   a signature back to the ordered component hashes it names, against a library of candidates
      `SchemaSignature.decode(signature, library)` runs the palimpsest search over `library`
      — a list of candidate 32-byte hashes — and returns the ordered sequence the signature
      encodes. `Bintel.Error(BadSignature)` if no sequence of candidates reconstructs it,
      `BadSignatureLength` if it is not structurally a signature. The search is over every
      candidate, so prefer a `.Library`, which narrows it to one lineage, for anything larger
      than a handful.
  entry .decodeHinted   decodes a signature over a base and its layers, trying the selected layers first
      TEL §8.1's decomposition hints: `decodeHinted(signature, base, layers, selection)` first
      tries the decode over the base plus only the layers whose names are in `selection`, then
      falls back to the full component list, since hints are advisory. The result is the
      component sequence, or `Unset` when neither attempt decodes; nothing is raised.
  entry .hash   the value hash of a rendered schema element: BLAKE3-256 over its BinTEL encoding under the axiom
      `SchemaSignature.hash(element, axiom = Tels.Axiom.tels)` is the hash the base and each
      layer contribute; a `Tels.Renderer` element hashes identically to the document it
      renders, so a derived schema and the same schema read from a file agree.
  entry .atomHash   the value hash of an atom of a schema
      An atom (`Tels.Atoms.Atom`) is the smallest unit a layer decomposes into — one optional
      member, say — and `atomHash(atom)` is the hash of its virtual single-atom layer, or for
      a `Head`, the definition-less schema. A signature may name atoms as well as layers, and
      `Lineage#including` offers a derived schema's optional members this way.
  entry .verifySelection   checks a pragma's claimed signature against a schema document and its selected layers
      TEL §8.1: when a pragma's signature follows its layer selections it is authoritative,
      and must decompose into exactly `1 + n` components — the base hash followed by the `n`
      selected layers' hashes, in order. `verifySelection(doc, schema, axiom, selection,
      signature)` raises `Tels.Resolution.Error` with a reason naming what diverged:
      `UnknownLayer` for a selected name the schema does not declare, `ComponentCount` when
      the count is wrong, `BaseMismatch`, `LayerMismatch(name)` for a wrong or misordered layer,
      or `Unverified` when the signature does not decompose over the schema's hashes at all.
      `SchemaResolver.resolve` runs this on every candidate it retrieves.
  entry .Lineage   a base schema with every component of its lineage — layers and atoms, each with its hash
    detail
        The set a signature naming this base is decoded against (BinTEL §8.2, step 3): the
        base's hash, its layers in declaration order, and the atoms of the base and of each
        layer, each hashed once. A writer builds one over the schema it holds to sign
        compositions and to decode the signatures a reader sends; a reader's or writer's
        library is a list of them (`.Library`).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val source = t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n\nlayer\n  name with-email\n  overlay\n    field email String\n"
        val contact: SchemaSignature.Lineage = SchemaSignature.Lineage.of(source.read[Tel])

        contact.layers.map(_.name)                      // List(t"with-email")
        contact.signature(List(t"with-email")).length   // 35
        contact.prefix(t"with-email").length            // 4
        ```

        `Lineage(schema)` builds one from a `Tels` value and `Lineage.of(document)` from a
        schema document; both take the axiom (`Tels.Axiom.tels` by default) and need a
        `Tactic[Bintel.Error]` and a `Tactic[Tels.Renderer.Error]`.
    refer stratiform.SchemaSignature
    entry .apply   a lineage from a `Tels` value, under an axiom
        Hashes the base (the schema without its layers), each layer, and each atom of
        `Tels.Atoms.decompose(schema)`, deduplicating atoms by hash: an atom which occurs in
        two groups, or twice in one, is one component.
    entry .of   a lineage from a schema document, as parsed
        `Lineage.of(document)` is `Lineage(document.as[Tels])`, and so also needs a
        `Tactic[Tel.Error]`.
    entry .Component   one layer or atom of a lineage: its hash, its name if it has one, and its layer
      detail
          A layer component has the layer's name; an atom has none (`Unset`), and its `layer`
          is the layer it is a part of (or, for an atom added by `Lineage#including`, the
          virtual layer which composes it).
      entry #hash   the component's 32-byte hash
      entry #name   the layer's name, or `Unset` for an atom
      entry #layer   the `Tels.Layer` which composes this component
      entry #text   the hash as BASE-256 text
    entry #schema   the base schema the lineage was built over
    entry #base   the base's hash: the first component of every signature naming this lineage
    entry #layers   the layer components, in declaration order
    entry #atoms   the atom components, each hash once
    entry #extras   the atoms added by `#including`, which belong to no layer
    entry #components   every component but the base: the layers, then the atoms
    entry #offered   the components worth offering beyond the base: the layers, each standing for its atoms, and the extras
        What an acceptance offers: a layer names its atoms, and the base's own atoms are part
        of every composition naming it, so only the layers and the `#extras` are listed.
    entry #candidates   the hashes a signature naming this base is decoded against: the base, then every component
    entry #signature   the signature of the base composed with the named layers, in declaration order
        `lineage.signature(List(t"with-email"))`; `signature(List())` is the base alone.
        `Tels.Resolution.Error(UnknownLayer(name))` for a name the lineage does not have.
    entry #component   the component with exactly this hash, if any
    entry #matching   the components whose hash begins with a prefix: none, one, or several
        An acceptance names a component by a prefix of its hash, and treats a prefix several
        components share as naming none.
    entry #prefix   the shortest prefix, at least four bytes, which names a layer uniquely within this lineage
        `lineage.prefix(name, minimum = 4)`: the form an acceptance names a further component
        by. `Tels.Resolution.Error(UnknownLayer(name))` for an unknown layer.
    entry #prefixOf   the shortest prefix of any hash, at least `minimum` bytes, which no other component shares
        Lengthens the prefix a byte at a time until it denotes one component alone; the full
        hash always does.
    entry #including   this lineage with further atoms as components, each once by hash
        `lineage.including(atoms)` adds the atoms a derived schema's optional members form, so
        a writer can offer them; the added atoms appear in both `#atoms` and `#extras`.
    entry #compose   the composed schema a decoded hash sequence names
        The first hash must be `#base` (`Tels.Resolution.Error(BaseMismatch)` otherwise); each
        further hash must be a component (`Unresolved` at the `Library` step, naming the
        hash in BASE-256); and the components' layers must compose validly
        (`NotSchema(message)` otherwise — a signature naming atoms whose references are unmet,
        say). `Library#resolve` is `decode` followed by this.
  entry .Library   a list of lineages which decodes a signature in two phases: the base by prefix, then its components
    detail
        As BinTEL §8.2 recommends: the base is found by the first four bytes of the signature
        body, which are its hash prefix uncontested, and the further components are then
        decoded by the palimpsest search over that base's lineage alone — never over the whole
        library, which keeps the search bounded however many schemas a peer holds.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val source = t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n\nlayer\n  name with-email\n  overlay\n    field email String\n"
        val contact = SchemaSignature.Lineage.of(source.read[Tel])
        val library = SchemaSignature.Library(List(contact))

        library.resolve(contact.signature(List(t"with-email"))).let(_.document.members.length)   // 2
        library.decode(contact.signature(List())).let(_.hashes.length)                           // 1
        ```
    entry .empty   the library with no lineages
    entry .Decoded   what a library decodes a signature to: the lineage which served it and the ordered hashes
      entry #lineage   the lineage whose base the signature names
      entry #hashes   the component hashes, base first, in the signature's order
    entry #lineages   the lineages, in the order given
    entry #byBase   the lineages whose base hash begins with a prefix
    entry #decode   the lineage and hashes a signature names, or `Unset` when no lineage serves it
        Nothing is raised: a signature shorter than five bytes, one whose prefix matches no
        base, and one which decodes against no matching lineage all give `Unset`.
    entry #resolve   the composed schema a signature names, or `Unset` when no lineage serves it
        `#decode` followed by `Lineage#compose`, so a signature a lineage *does* serve but
        which composes invalidly raises `Tels.Resolution.Error`.

entry .SchemaResolver   resolves a pragma's schema identification to a composed, signature-verified schema
  detail
      The resolution engine of TEL §8.2. A document's pragma identifies its schema by a
      *reference* (`domain/name`, with an optional `:version` or `:tag` selector), a list of
      *layer* selections, and a *signature*; `SchemaResolver.resolve` turns that into the
      composed `Tels` the document should be typed against, trying in order:

      0. the embedded schema of a self-contained BinTEL container, when one is passed;
      1. the built-ins — the `tels` meta-schema and the `acceptance` schema — by their pinned
          coordinates (`specification.tel/tels:2.1.0`, or the earlier `tels:2.0.0`, with or without
          the version pin) or by
          their signatures, with no network access;
      2. every content-addressed `Tels.Resolution.Store` (a schema cache, a LIRA store), by
          signature;
      3. the *library* of schema documents already in hand, by signature, using the layer
          selections as decomposition hints;
      4. a `Tels.Resolution.Delegate` (LIRA resolution), by signature or by selector, caching
          what it returns in the first store;
      5. failure, as `Tels.Resolution.Error(Unresolved(step, identifier))` naming the deepest
          step attempted.

      Every retrieved schema body is verified: its signature over the base plus the pragma's
      selected layers, recomputed with `SchemaSignature.verifySelection`, must match the
      claimed signature byte for byte, so a tampered cache entry fails as `Unverified` rather
      than typing the document against the wrong schema. A *bare* reference — no selector and
      no signature — is a developer's working copy, and resolves only against a store's local
      reference entries: never the library, never the delegate.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A built-in, by its coordinate
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val pragma = Tel.Pragma((1, 0), Tel.Pragma.Reference.tels, List(), Unset, Unset)
            SchemaResolver.resolve(pragma).step   // Tels.Resolution.Step.Builtin
      frame
        label A built-in, by its signature alone
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val pragma = Tel.Pragma((1, 0), Unset, List(), SchemaResolver.telsSignature, Unset)
            SchemaResolver.resolve(pragma).step   // Tels.Resolution.Step.Builtin
      frame
        label A schema document in hand, found by its signature and verified
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val source = t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n\nlayer\n  name with-email\n  overlay\n    field email String\n"
            val contactDoc = source.read[Tel]
            val contact = SchemaSignature.Lineage.of(contactDoc)
            val claimed = Base256.encode(contact.signature(List(t"with-email")))
            val pragma = Tel.Pragma((1, 0), Unset, List(t"with-email"), claimed, Unset)
            val resolved = SchemaResolver.resolve(pragma, library = List(contactDoc))
            (resolved.step, resolved.schema.document.members.length)   // (Library, 2)
      frame
        label A cached body answers before the library is consulted
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val source = t"name contact\n\ndocument\n  field name Identifier\n\nlayer\n  name with-email\n  overlay\n    field email String\n"
            val contactDoc = source.read[Tel]
            val contact = SchemaSignature.Lineage.of(contactDoc)
            val signature = contact.signature(List())
            val store = Tels.Resolution.Store.Memory()
            store.cache(signature, Array.unsafeFrozen(source.s.getBytes("UTF-8").nn))
            val pragma = Tel.Pragma((1, 0), Unset, List(), Base256.encode(signature), Unset)
            SchemaResolver.resolve(pragma, stores = List(store), library = List(contactDoc)).step   // Cache
      ##
      ```

      `resolve` raises `Tel.Error` (a candidate is not a valid schema; a malformed library
      document aborts resolution rather than being skipped), `Bintel.Error` (a signature is
      not structurally one) and `Tels.Resolution.Error` (`Unresolved`, `UnknownLayer`,
      `ComponentCount`, `BaseMismatch`, `LayerMismatch`, `Unverified`). Library documents are
      fingerprinted once each — their base and layer hashes, memoised weakly by identity — so
      resolving many pragmas against the same library does not re-hash it.
  refer stratiform.SchemaSignature
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  entry .telsSignature   the pinned BASE-256 signature of the built-in `tels` meta-schema
      `ÔŀưḞ2żbτȚÆAĄſЬMẍỳϋῩJλḤӛ3ñẉḢkŻẋzǓĥ`: a golden value, recomputed from the canonical
      `tels.tel` by the test suite. A pragma claiming it resolves at the built-in step.
  entry .acceptanceSignature   the pinned signature of the built-in `acceptance` schema, `Tel.Acceptance.signature`
      Recognised at the built-in step alongside `tels`, so a peer can parse an acceptance
      before any schema has been exchanged.
  entry .builtins   the built-in table: each schema's pinned reference, its signature and the `Tels` which answers for it
      Two rows, `tels` and `acceptance`, each matched by coordinate (with or without its
      version pin) or by signature.
  entry .Resolved   a resolution outcome: the composed schema, its source document, its signature identity and the step
    entry #schema   the schema composed with the pragma's layer selection
    entry #document   the schema document it came from, or `Unset` for a built-in
    entry #signature   the signature identity it was matched under: the base plus the selected layers
        Also the key it is cached under, when a delegate's answer is stored.
    entry #step   which `Tels.Resolution.Step` answered: `Embedded`, `Builtin`, `Cache`, `Library` or `Lira`
  entry .resolve   runs the resolution steps for a pragma, over the stores, library, delegate and embedded schema given
      `SchemaResolver.resolve(pragma, stores = List(), library = List(), delegate = Unset,
      embedded = Unset, axiom = Tels.Axiom.tels)`: `stores` are consulted in order and the
      first is where a delegate's answer is cached; `library` is a list of schema `Tel`
      documents; `embedded` is the signature and schema body pair a self-contained BinTEL
      container carries, which a claimed pragma signature must match. The result is a
      `.Resolved`.

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `tel"…"`, `telp"…"`, the `tel` extractor, staged parsing and layer annotations
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home. (`object
      Tel`, the companion of `Tel`, is declared in the same source file and is documented
      under `Tel`.)
  entry .identityOf   the macro behind `TelSchematic.Identity`: a type's simple name and its enclosing scopes, innermost first
      How two types of one simple name are told apart in a derived schema, and a type reached
      twice is recognised.
  entry .assignments   reads a product's `@assign(layer)` annotations as `(field, layer)` pairs
      The layer must be named by its singleton object, declared as `object name extends
      Tel.Layer("name")`; anything else is a compile error at the argument.
  entry .retractions   reads a sum's `@retract(layer)` annotations as `(case, layer)` pairs
  entry .telp   the macro behind `telp"…"`: parses the literal with `Telp.parse` and points the caret at a bad component
  entry .interpolator   the macro behind `tel"…"`: parses the static parts at compile time and splices the holes
      Substitution is supported at the atom position of a compound (`tel"name $percy"`); a
      parse failure's span is mapped back into the literal.
  entry .extractor   the macro behind the `tel"…"` pattern: a compile-time pattern with holes as capture sites
  entry .matchDocument   the runtime matcher the extractor splices: the captures, in document order, if the input matches
  entry .stagedParsable   the macro behind `Tel.Parsable.staged`: a monomorphic parser for a case class
      Requires a top-level or object-nested case class with a single parameter list; keywords
      compile to packed-word comparisons when no `@name` annotation can rename them.

entry .Base256   the BASE-256 binary-to-text codec: one Unicode character per byte, each congruent to it mod 256
  detail
      The alphabet assigns every byte value a character from the Basic Multilingual Plane —
      Basic Latin, the Latin supplements, Greek, Cyrillic and their extensions — whose code
      point is congruent to the byte mod 256, so the digits and ASCII letters stand for
      themselves, encoding is an array index, and decoding is a modulo. A schema signature
      travels this way in a pragma (`tel 1.0 … ÔŀưḞ…`) and in an acceptance, and a whole
      BinTEL document does as `Bintel.text`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val data: Data = Array[Byte](0x41, 0x42, 0x43)
      Base256.encode(data)                   // t"ABC"
      Base256.decode(t"ABC").length          // 3
      safely(Base256.decodeStrict(t"A B"))   // Unset: the space is not in the alphabet
      ```

      The codec is also available as the `base-256` and `schema-signature` codecs of
      `Tel.Codec.Bindings.builtins`, for a scalar whose schema declares that encoding. The
      alphabet's defining property is verified when the object loads, as the specification
      requires of every implementation. It is the `stratiform.base256` component, which
      `stratiform.core` depends on.
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  refer stratiform.SchemaSignature
  entry .alphabetString   the 256 characters of the alphabet, in byte-value order, as a `String`
  entry .alphabet   the alphabet as an array of 256 characters, indexed by byte value
  entry .valid   whether a character is a member of the alphabet
      Exact membership, which the pragma parser uses to classify a phrase as a schema
      signature by its form; whether it then decodes as a palimpsest is settled at resolution
      time.
  entry .encode   bytes to text, one character per byte
      The result has as many characters as the input has bytes; the empty input is the empty
      text.
  entry .decode   text to bytes, permissively: each code point mod 256
      A character outside the alphabet is not rejected — its residue is taken as it is — and a
      supplementary character is one code point and so one byte. Use `.decodeStrict` for text
      from an untrusted source.
  entry .decodeStrict   text to bytes, rejecting any character outside the alphabet
      Raises `Base256.Error` naming *every* offending character — by its zero-based code-point
      index (not a byte or UTF-16 offset) and its code point, so an editor can locate each —
      rather than only the first. A supplementary character is always an offender. On success
      it behaves exactly as `.decode`.
  entry .Offender   a character outside the alphabet: its code-point index in the input, and its code point
    entry #position   the zero-based code-point index of the character
    entry #codepoint   the character's code point
    entry #render   the character, its `U+XXXX` code point and its position, as text
  entry .Error   raised by `decodeStrict` when text contains characters outside the alphabet
    detail
        Error 607: _the BASE-256 string is invalid because `reason`_, where the reason lists
        every offending character.
    entry .Reason   `NotInAlphabet(offenders)`, listing every offending `Base256.Offender`
      entry .communicable   renders the reason with each offender's character, code point and position
    entry #reason   the offenders, as `NotInAlphabet`

entry .BintelInlinable   compile-time generation of a `Bintel.Parsable`: a flat parser over body bytes, with no tree
  detail
      `BintelInlinable.parsable[T]` generates, at compile time, a monomorphic
      `T is Bintel.Parsable` for a case class or a sealed sum: nested records, repeated-field
      gathering, variant dispatch and leaf parsers all inline into one parser over the body
      bytes, so `Bintel.parse[T](bytes)` materializes neither the `Tel.Element` tree, nor its
      `Tel` presentation, nor the text-format decode `Bintel.read` goes through. The value
      semantics are the text format's exactly — leaf faults, absent handling, foci, sentinel
      values — because the AST path ends in the same text-format instances.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Troupe(name: Text, players: List[Person])

      given (Person is Bintel.Parsable) = BintelInlinable.parsable[Person]
      given (Troupe is Bintel.Parsable) = BintelInlinable.parsable[Troupe]

      val troupe = Troupe(t"mechanicals", List(Person(t"Quince", 40), Person(t"Bottom", 35)))
      Bintel.parse[Troupe](troupe.bintel) == troupe
      ```

      BinTEL is schema-driven: the wire carries flat keyword *indices*, and the derivation
      gives every case-class field exactly one slot in declaration order, so the generated
      dispatch is a positional table — the index *is* the field's position. A repeatable
      field's occurrences repeat its index and are gathered by the struct's parser; an
      `Optional` field absent from the body is `Unset`; a field with a default takes it. A
      sealed sum at the *top level* derives to a struct with one `SelectRef`, so the index
      chooses the variant directly; a sum in field position is rejected while the parser is
      planned, as it derives to an unresolvable reference on the AST path too, and a `Map`
      field has no BinTEL form. Shapes the generator rejects stay on `Bintel.read`.

      The trait itself is the `Expr`-level counterpart of `Bintel.Parsable`: an instance's
      `#parse` receives an `Expr` of the `BintelReader`, positioned at its value's payload, and
      returns an `Expr` of the decoded value, which the generator splices into the composed
      parser. The leaf instances in the companion (`int`, `long`, `boolean`, `double`, `text`,
      `string`) cover the primitives; a custom leaf is a user-written instance; and `case
      class Play(…) derives BintelInlinable.ForBintel` places a structural instance in `Play`'s
      companion, where the generator finds it. The instance behind each summoned given is
      obtained *live*, by running the implicit search inside an in-macro staging compiler, so
      an instance and its type must be compiled in an earlier run than the expansion which
      uses them. This is the `stratiform.binaryStaged` component.
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  refer stratiform.BintelReader
  refer stratiform.Inlinable
  entry .parsable   generates a `T is Bintel.Parsable` for a case class or a top-level sealed sum, at compile time
      `BintelInlinable.parsable[T]`, bound as a given so that `Bintel.parse[T]` finds it. A
      type the generator cannot plan — a sum in field position, a `Map`, a field with no
      instance in reach — is a compile error naming the shape.
  entry .derived   the structural instance for a case class, reflected when invoked
      No macro: `Type[Self]` arrives with the `#parse` call. It is what `ForBintel.derived`
      wraps for a `derives` clause.
  entry .ForBintel   the `derives`-clause carrier: `case class Play(…) derives BintelInlinable.ForBintel`
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass cannot appear in a `derives` clause, so this parameterised
        subtrait — which *is* a `Play is BintelInlinable` — is what the clause synthesizes into
        `Play`'s companion, where the generator's summon finds it. The resolution ladder
        unwraps the delegate, so a structural instance keeps its generator identity.
    entry .derived   the carrier over `BintelInlinable.derived[value]`
    entry #parse   delegates to the wrapped instance's generator
    entry #absent   delegates to the wrapped instance's absence handling
  entry .int   the leaf generator for `Int`: the scalar's text parsed as an integer, `NotScalar` otherwise
  entry .long   the leaf generator for `Long`
  entry .boolean   the leaf generator for `Boolean`: `true` or `false`, `NotScalar` otherwise
  entry .double   the leaf generator for `Double`
  entry .text   the leaf generator for `Text`: the scalar's text as it is
  entry .string   the leaf generator for `String`
  entry .iterable   a marker instance for a repeatable field of an inlinable element type
      A repeatable field's occurrences are gathered by its *struct's* generated parser, so
      this instance exists only to make the element's instance reachable in the ladder; a
      collection has no standalone BinTEL form, and calling its generator directly is a
      compile error saying so.
  entry #parse   the code generator: an `Expr` of the reader at the value's payload, to an `Expr` of the value
  entry #absent   the code a field of this type yields when its index never arrives in the struct body
      An abort with `Tel.Error(Absent)` unless overridden; the primitive instances raise and
      continue with a sentinel (`0`, `false`, `t""`), as the runtime instances do.

entry .Inlinable   compile-time generation of a `Tel.Parsable`: a flat text-format parser composed from instances
  detail
      `Inlinable.parsable[T]` generates, at compile time, a monomorphic `T is Tel.Parsable`
      for a case class or a sealed sum, like `Tel.Parsable.staged` but composed through
      `Inlinable` instances: nested records, collection gathering and custom leaf parsers all
      inline into one flat parser over the `TelReader`, with no runtime dispatch, no instance
      arrays and no adapter hops between composed parsers.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Tree(value: Text, children: List[Tree])
      given (Tree is Tel.Parsable) = Inlinable.parsable[Tree]

      t"value root\nchildren\n  value a\n".read[Tree in Tel]
      ```

      The trait is the `Expr`-level counterpart of `Tel.Parsable`: an instance's `#parse`
      receives an `Expr` of the reader and — because a TEL value is an *entry* whose extent
      depends on its indent — an `Expr` of the current indent, and returns an `Expr` of the
      decoded value, spliced directly into the generated parser. A sealed sum's wire form is a
      single child compound keyed by the variant's kebab-cased name, so the generated code
      dispatches on that keyword and parses the variant in place. A recursive type ties
      through its own nominal `Tel.Parsable` in scope.

      Instances are ordinary runtime values whose code generation is deferred to the `parse`
      call, so `derived` needs no macro. At an expansion, the instance behind each summoned
      given is obtained *live* by running the implicit search inside an in-macro staging
      compiler, which composes conditional instances — a collection of a custom element —
      through ordinary given resolution. The constraint this inherits: an instance (and its
      type) must be compiled in an earlier run than the expansion; a same-run instance
      degrades to a spliced runtime call through `Tel.Field`. This is the `stratiform.staged`
      component; `BintelInlinable` is its counterpart for the binary form.
  refer stratiform.BintelInlinable
  refer stratiform.TelReader
  entry .parsable   generates a `T is Tel.Parsable` for a case class or sealed sum, at compile time
  entry .derived   the structural instance for a case class, reflected when invoked
      A record field's value is its children, one level deeper than its own entry line, which
      is the derived engine's reading.
  entry .ForTel   the `derives`-clause carrier: `case class Play(…) derives Inlinable.ForTel`
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass cannot appear in a `derives` clause, so this parameterised
        subtrait — which *is* a `Play is Inlinable` — is what the clause synthesizes into
        `Play`'s companion, where the generator's summon finds it; the resolution ladder
        unwraps the delegate so structural instances keep their generator identities.
    entry .derived   the carrier over `Inlinable.derived[value]`
    entry #parse   delegates to the wrapped instance's generator
    entry #absent   delegates to the wrapped instance's absence handling
  entry .int   the leaf generator for `Int`, mirroring `Tel.Parsable`'s instance, with its `Absent`/`NotScalar` split
  entry .long   the leaf generator for `Long`
  entry .boolean   the leaf generator for `Boolean`
  entry .double   the leaf generator for `Double`, parsing the atom's text
  entry .text   the leaf generator for `Text`: the atom, `Absent` if there is none
  entry .string   the leaf generator for `String`
  entry .iterable   a collection of an inlinable element, gathered occurrence by occurrence in field position
      In field position the deriving generator never calls this instance's generator: it
      gathers each occurrence of the keyword through the element's own. A lone entry read as a
      collection is one element, as the runtime `Tel.Parsable.iterable` behaves.
  entry #parse   the code generator: `Expr`s of the reader and the current indent, to an `Expr` of the value
  entry #absent   the code a field of this type yields when its keyword never arrives
      An abort with `Tel.Error(Absent)` unless overridden; the primitive instances raise and
      continue with a sentinel.

entry .stagedInternal   the machinery behind `Inlinable.parsable`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: expansion-time instance resolution through an in-macro staging
      compiler, the structural generators for products, sums and collections, and the entry
      macro.
  entry .fieldInstance   the runtime seam for a field type without an `Inlinable`: its `Tel.Parsing` instance, summoned where the parser is spliced
  entry .inlinableParsable   the macro behind `Inlinable.parsable[T]`

entry .bintelInternal   the machinery behind `BintelInlinable.parsable`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: expansion-time instance resolution, the positional-table generators
      for products and top-level sums, and the entry macro.
  entry .inlinableParsable   the macro behind `BintelInlinable.parsable[T]`

entry .Framed   a `Duplex` connection speaking length-prefixed BinTEL messages: typed values in, typed values out
  detail
      `Framed from inbound to outbound` reads `inbound` values from a `Duplex` and sends
      `outbound` ones, each message a record framed by its four-byte big-endian length
      (`LengthPrefix`) whose body is the value's BinTEL encoding — the framing a daemon speaks
      over TLS to its peers and over a UNIX domain socket to a local tool. One end's `Framed
      from Request to Reply` meets the other's `Framed from Reply to Request`, and a symmetric
      protocol is `Framed from T to T`. Both schemas are derived once, when the connection is
      framed, rather than per message as `Bintel.read` and `value.bintel` derive them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Request(id: Int, text: Text)
      case class Reply(id: Int, length: Int)

      val (near, far) = Duplex.pair()
      val client = near.framed[Reply, Request]
      val server = far.framed[Request, Reply]

      client.send(Request(1, t"hello"))
      client.close()
      server.messages.to[List]   // List(Request(1, t"hello"))
      ```

      The connection is built with `Framed[inbound, outbound](duplex)` or the `framed`
      extension on `Duplex`, and needs a `Buffering`, a `Tactic[Framing.Error]`, a
      `Tactic[Bintel.Error]` and a `Tactic[Tel.Error]`. The tactics are taken when the
      connection is framed, not per message, because a framing or decoding failure surfaces
      lazily as `#messages` is pulled; the connection therefore captures them, and its type
      says so. This is the `stratiform.duplex` component.
  refer stratiform.framed
  refer stratiform.Bintel
  entry .apply   frames a `Duplex` for `inbound` and `outbound` message types
      `Framed[Reply, Request](duplex)` derives the two schemas with `Tels.tels`, so both types
      need a `TelSchematic`, `inbound` a `Tel.Decodable` and `outbound` a `Tel.Encodable`. The
      inbound iterator is opened on the first pull, not when the connection is framed, so
      framing never blocks on a peer which has not yet sent anything.
  entry #send   encodes a message to BinTEL and sends it as one length-prefixed frame
      Writes are serialized by a mutex: two messages' frames interleaved on the wire would be
      unreadable, and `Duplex.send` leaves that to its caller.
  entry #messages   the inbound messages, as one iterator opened once and handed back thereafter
      The `Duplex` contract is a single reader, so the same iterator is returned on every
      call; pulling it decodes each frame under the inbound schema. Messages sent before any
      is read arrive in order, and the iterator ends when the peer closes.
  entry #close   closes the underlying duplex

entry .framed   `duplex.framed[inbound, outbound]`: the `Duplex` as a `Framed` BinTEL connection
    An extension on `Duplex`: `duplex.framed[Reply, Request]` is `Framed[Reply, Request](duplex)`
    — a `Framed from Reply to Request` — with the same contextual needs: a `Buffering`, a
    `Tactic[Framing.Error]`, a `Tactic[Bintel.Error]` and a `Tactic[Tel.Error]`, which the
    result captures. In `stratiform.duplex`.

entry .postables   the given which sends a `Tel` as an HTTP request body, to import by name
  detail
      `import postables.telPostable` lets a `Tel` be posted with telekinesis, as
      `application/vnd.tel` encoded in the `Codepage` in scope. The package is in
      `stratiform.http` and merges with the umbrella's `postables`.
  entry .telPostable   a `Tel is Postable`: the document's text, in the `Codepage` in scope, as `application/vnd.tel`

entry .servables   the given which serves a `Tel` as an HTTP response, to import by name
  detail
      `import servables.telServable` lets a scintillate handler respond with a `Tel`, as a
      fixed body of `application/vnd.tel` encoded in the `Codepage` in scope. In
      `stratiform.http`; merges with the umbrella's `servables`.
  entry .telServable   a `Tel is Servable`: the document's text as a fixed `application/vnd.tel` body

entry .construables   the given which reads an `application/vnd.tel` body as a `Tel`, to import by name
  detail
      `import construables.telConstruable` lets a request or response body of media type
      `application/vnd.tel` be read as a `Tel`. In `stratiform.http`; merges with the
      umbrella's `construables`.
  entry .telConstruable   `"application/vnd.tel" is Construable to Tel`

entry .optics   the panopticon lens and optics over a `Tel`, to import by name
  detail
      The three givens which let panopticon's `Optic` syntax focus into a TEL document: a
      field-keyed lens, a positional optic over a node's child compounds, and an optic over
      every child. They live here, rather than in `Tel`'s implicit scope, so that
      `stratiform.core` does not depend on panopticon, and a call site which fails to import
      them cannot silently degrade: panopticon's generic `deref` lens applies only to
      `Product` types, and `Tel` is a plain class. The package is `stratiform.optics`, and
      merges with the umbrella's `optics`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import optics.{telLens, telOrdinalOptical, telEachOptical}
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicTel

      val tel = t"person\n  name Percy\n  age 40\n".read[Tel]
      tel.lens(_.person.age = Tel.scalar(t"41"))   // the age updated in place, the keyword kept
      ```
  entry .telLens   a field name as a `Lens from Tel onto Tel`
      A name `<: Label` resolves to a lens whose getter is the dynamic field selection and
      whose setter is `Tel.modify`, which replaces an existing child compound with the same
      kebab-case keyword in place or appends a new one. It needs a `(? >: Tel) is Dynamical`
      (`dynamicAccess.dynamicTel`) and a `Tactic[Tel.Error]`.
  entry .telOrdinalOptical   an `Ordinal` as an optic onto the n-th child compound of a node
      TEL has no positional arrays, but a compound's children are ordered, so this mirrors the
      read-side `applyDynamic(field)(index)`. The transform's result keeps the original child's
      keyword, so a positional update preserves the field's identity while replacing its value
      or children; an ordinal out of range leaves the document unchanged.
  entry .telEachOptical   `Each` as an optic onto every child compound of a node
      Applies the transform to each child, keeping each child's keyword.

entry .encodables   the Aviation time codecs for writing TEL atoms, to import by name
  detail
      `import encodables.instantTelEncodable` or `durationTelEncodable` encodes an `Instant
      over Unix` or a `Duration` as a whole-number atom, in milliseconds, mirroring
      jacinta's `time` codecs. They are scoped to a package so that they do not clash with a
      project's own default encodings; `stratiform.time`, merging with the umbrella's
      `encodables`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import encodables.durationTelEncodable
      import decodables.durationTelDecodable

      val duration: Duration = Duration(1500L)
      duration.in[Tel].as[Duration] == duration
      ```
  entry .instantTelEncodable   an `(Instant over Unix) is Tel.Encodable`: the Unix epoch milliseconds as a whole atom
  entry .durationTelEncodable   a `Duration is Tel.Encodable`: the duration in milliseconds as a whole atom

entry .decodables   the Aviation time codecs for reading TEL atoms, to import by name
  detail
      `import decodables.instantTelDecodable` or `durationTelDecodable` reads a whole-number
      atom as an `Instant over Unix` or a `Duration`, in milliseconds. Each needs a
      `Tactic[Tel.Error]`, which it captures, and an atom which is not a number is reported as
      `Tel.Error(BadVersion)`. In `stratiform.time`; merges with the umbrella's `decodables`.
  entry .instantTelDecodable   an `(Instant over Unix) is Tel.Decodable`, capturing the `Tactic[Tel.Error]` in scope
  entry .durationTelDecodable   a `Duration is Tel.Decodable`, capturing the `Tactic[Tel.Error]` in scope

entry .CheckedTests   a suite exercising the derived TEL codecs from a capture-checked module; compiling is its purpose
  hidden
  detail
      The `stratiform.checked` component is a test suite compiled with capture checking on:
      every shape of field which once reached a pure slot or a re-freshened array in the
      derivation — scalars, a nested product, repeated `List` and `Set` fields with and
      without defaults, `Optional`, `Map`, an enum and a recursive type — read from text with
      `read[Tel].as[T]`. The values it asserts are secondary. Its fixtures (`Language`,
      `Script`, `Plain`, `Sets`, `Table`, `Counted`, `Shape`, `Shapes`, `Tree`) are not for use.
  entry .run   runs the suite
